WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.985
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4623.

2
00:00:00.985 --> 00:00:01.970
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:02.299
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright.

4
00:00:02.299 --> 00:00:04.269
Today is Thursday May twenty-sixth 2016.

5
00:00:04.269 --> 00:00:07.224
I’m here with Ms. Theresa Lee in her home.

6
00:00:07.224 --> 00:00:08.866
Is this Carlyss [Louisiana], technically?

7
00:00:08.866 --> 00:00:09.194
<v THERESA LEE>Yes.

8
00:00:09.194 --> 00:00:09.851
<v ARSENEAULT>In Carlyss.

9
00:00:09.851 --> 00:00:10.179
Okay.

10
00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:14.119
In Carlyss and we’re here to . . . For the Mossville Oral History Project.

11
00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:16.090
She’s graciously agreed to be interviewed.

12
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:22.000
And the first question I always ask is just to state your full name for the recording.

13
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:22.462
<v LEE>Okay.

14
00:00:22.462 --> 00:00:26.154
My full name is Theresa Ann Whitehead Lee

15
00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:27.538
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright Ms. Theresa.

16
00:00:27.538 --> 00:00:29.846
And Whitehead’s your maiden name?

17
00:00:29.846 --> 00:00:31.231
<v LEE>That is correct.

18
00:00:31.231 --> 00:00:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And Lee is your married name?

19
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:34.462
<v LEE>Yes.

20
00:00:34.462 --> 00:00:34.923
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

21
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:37.692
And tell me about your parents.

22
00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:40.000
Where were they from?

23
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.903
<v LEE>My parents . . . My father was from Killona which is very close to New Orleans area.

24
00:00:46.903 --> 00:00:53.345
And my mother was from the Patoutville area which is right outside of Jeanerette.

25
00:00:53.345 --> 00:00:57.027
All of this is east of Lafayette [Louisiana].

26
00:00:57.027 --> 00:01:00.248
<v ARSENEAULT>And what was your mother’s maiden name?

27
00:01:00.248 --> 00:01:00.708
<v LEE>NewChurch.

28
00:01:00.708 --> 00:01:02.549
<v ARSENEAULT>What were their names?

29
00:01:02.549 --> 00:01:05.310
<v LEE>His name . . . He was a pastor.

30
00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.150
Reverend Preston Earl Whitehead.

31
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.912
And her name, Sarah NewChurch Whitehead.

32
00:01:09.912 --> 00:01:13.133
And it’s spelled just like it sounds.

33
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:13.593
N-E-W-C-H-U-R-C-H.

34
00:01:13.593 --> 00:01:14.973
<v ARSENEAULT>And Whitehead is . . .?

35
00:01:14.973 --> 00:01:19.575
<v LEE>And Whitehead is my maiden name . . . My father’s name, yes.

36
00:01:19.575 --> 00:01:21.876
<v ARSENEAULT>And it’s spelled like it’s . . .?

37
00:01:21.876 --> 00:01:24.637
<v LEE>Just as you would say it.

38
00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:26.018
The compound name.

39
00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:26.478
Right.

40
00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:27.398
<v ARSENEAULT>Got it.

41
00:01:27.398 --> 00:01:27.858
<v LEE>Whitehead.

42
00:01:27.858 --> 00:01:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So Jeanerette and . . . Right outside of New Orleans.

43
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.810
<v LEE>No. Jeanerette is actually thirty-one miles east of Lafayette.

44
00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:36.233
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

45
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:40.466
<v LEE>Okay, but he was born in the New Orleans area.

46
00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.582
It’s a place called Killona.

47
00:01:42.582 --> 00:01:45.545
K-I-L-O- N-A I think it is spelled.

48
00:01:45.545 --> 00:01:47.661
<v ARSENEAULT>And how did they meet?

49
00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:53.164
<v LEE>He moved to the Jeanerette area and he worked at the sugar mill.

50
00:01:53.164 --> 00:02:00.360
And he saw this beautiful woman in a red dress, and the red dress caught his eyes.

51
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.746
And he . . . His friend had met her first.

52
00:02:03.746 --> 00:02:06.709
But Dad said, “No this is mine.”

53
00:02:06.709 --> 00:02:10.942
And with his charm and everything he won her over.

54
00:02:10.942 --> 00:02:14.751
And they celebrated their fiftieth wedding anniversary in ’92.

55
00:02:14.751 --> 00:02:20.677
He passed in 1997 at eighty-seven and she passed in 2010 on Valentine’s Day . . .

56
00:02:20.677 --> 00:02:21.101
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

57
00:02:21.101 --> 00:02:22.370
<v LEE>. . . At eighty-seven also.

58
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:29.989
So when she passed we said she had gone home to be with her sweetheart on Valentine’s Day.

59
00:02:29.989 --> 00:02:30.836
<v DAWN HOLMAN>Your siblings.

60
00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:31.683
<v LEE>And . . . Hmm?

61
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:32.529
<v HOLMAN>Your siblings.

62
00:02:32.529 --> 00:02:32.952
<v LEE>Yes.

63
00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:34.222
And my siblings.

64
00:02:34.222 --> 00:02:35.915
There are ten children.

65
00:02:35.915 --> 00:02:38.878
Mother was . . . Well one of two children.

66
00:02:38.878 --> 00:02:43.534
She had a half-brother and my dad was oldest of three.

67
00:02:43.534 --> 00:02:49.037
So together they had ten children and two of them have now passed.

68
00:02:49.037 --> 00:02:52.000
And I’m the third oldest child.

69
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.562
<v ARSENEAULT>What were their names?

70
00:02:53.562 --> 00:02:53.952
<v LEE>Yes.

71
00:02:53.952 --> 00:02:58.248
My oldest sister who lives in Houston [Texas] is Alzina Fontenot.

72
00:02:58.248 --> 00:03:01.762
She worked for several university presidents through her career.

73
00:03:01.762 --> 00:03:03.324
She is now retired.

74
00:03:03.324 --> 00:03:05.276
She’s in her seventies also.

75
00:03:05.276 --> 00:03:07.619
My second sister attended Grambling [University].

76
00:03:07.619 --> 00:03:11.133
She lives in New Iberia . . . Well, Youngsville it is.

77
00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:11.524
<v HOLMAN>Patricia.

78
00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:12.305
<v LEE>And . . . Patricia.

79
00:03:12.305 --> 00:03:16.600
And she has a daycare at her old home in Jeanerette.

80
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.162
She’s a retired educator.

81
00:03:18.162 --> 00:03:20.114
Graduated from Grambling in ’61.

82
00:03:20.114 --> 00:03:23.629
And she . . . What else can I say about her?

83
00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:24.019
Okay.

84
00:03:24.019 --> 00:03:25.190
That is it.

85
00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.143
And then I’m the third.

86
00:03:27.143 --> 00:03:28.705
I’m the third oldest.

87
00:03:28.705 --> 00:03:38.076
I have pictures here . . . I graduated from Francis Marion Boley High School and it was there that I saw Richard for the first time.

88
00:03:38.076 --> 00:03:43.543
Never spoke to him or anything else but I saw him in a distance.

89
00:03:43.543 --> 00:03:51.743
I was a cheerleader and he had come with his team from Mossville because they were getting ready to play Jeanerette.

90
00:03:51.743 --> 00:03:54.476
Francis Marion Boley High School, in regionals.

91
00:03:54.476 --> 00:04:02.286
And there he was, standing in the corner on crutches and I was a cheerleader so . . . It was a dance.

92
00:04:02.286 --> 00:04:04.238
And I say, “Mmm okay.”

93
00:04:04.238 --> 00:04:09.705
And it wasn’t until many years later at Grambling as juniors we met again.

94
00:04:09.705 --> 00:04:13.219
And I guess I can talk about that later.

95
00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

96
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:14.438
<v LEE>Okay.

97
00:04:14.438 --> 00:04:22.762
And I . . . My first job after graduating Grambling in ’68 . . . I was recruited by the Racine Wisconsin School District.

98
00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:34.152
They were trying to integrate their schools there, so they were going to the predominately black schools to recruit black educators and so I was recruited.

99
00:04:34.152 --> 00:04:39.410
And I worked in the library there for one year in Racine.

100
00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:45.543
My husband . . . My later husband Richard and I were engaged before I went off.

101
00:04:45.543 --> 00:04:52.552
And I tried to get him to join me in Wisconsin but he had other plans.

102
00:04:52.552 --> 00:04:52.990
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

103
00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:53.429
<v LEE>Okay.

104
00:04:53.429 --> 00:04:55.619
<v ARSENEAULT>So when were you born?

105
00:04:55.619 --> 00:04:58.686
<v LEE>And I was born April ninth, 1946.

106
00:04:58.686 --> 00:04:59.124
<v ARSENEAULT>’46?

107
00:04:59.124 --> 00:05:00.000
<v LEE>’46.

108
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.745
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you tell me about your grandparents?

109
00:05:02.745 --> 00:05:09.412
<v LEE>I never knew my father’s father because he died in ’45 and I was born in ’46.

110
00:05:09.412 --> 00:05:12.941
Now my dad’s mother lived next door to us.

111
00:05:12.941 --> 00:05:16.078
It was a plot of land between us.

112
00:05:16.078 --> 00:05:18.039
Her name was Maggie Whitehead.

113
00:05:18.039 --> 00:05:19.608
Maggie Paul [Johnson] Whitehead.

114
00:05:19.608 --> 00:05:25.098
And she was a good cook and she made homemade wine on that porch.

115
00:05:25.098 --> 00:05:29.804
She raised a garden, she had chickens, she had all of that.

116
00:05:29.804 --> 00:05:31.765
She was a beautiful woman.

117
00:05:31.765 --> 00:05:38.431
And my dad’s . . . not my dad, but my mother’s father . . . He died when I was very young.

118
00:05:38.431 --> 00:05:47.843
I just remember a man standing in the doorway when we’d walk to his house and he would say, “Zina, the children are coming.”

119
00:05:47.843 --> 00:05:50.980
And they always had something good for us.

120
00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:59.216
And they . . . She died soon thereafter so I didn’t know her as well as I knew my . . . father’s mother. [Lee adds:

121
00:05:59.216 --> 00:06:04.314
Zina’s full name was Alzenia Jenkins NewChurch and grandfather’s name was Louis NewChurch.

122
00:06:04.314 --> 00:06:05.882
They called him “Popee”.]

123
00:06:05.882 --> 00:06:06.275
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . .

124
00:06:06.275 --> 00:06:08.627
<v HOLMAN>You didn’t finish listing the siblings.

125
00:06:08.627 --> 00:06:10.980
<v LEE>Oh I didn’t finish my siblings.

126
00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:11.373
Okay.

127
00:06:11.373 --> 00:06:11.573
So the . . .

128
00:06:11.373 --> 00:06:12.157
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

129
00:06:12.157 --> 00:06:12.549
<v LEE>Brief.

130
00:06:12.549 --> 00:06:14.118
Oh my goodness [laughs].

131
00:06:14.118 --> 00:06:15.686
Paul Earl Whitehead, he’s . . .

132
00:06:15.686 --> 00:06:18.039
<v ARSENEAULT>And your name for the recording?

133
00:06:18.039 --> 00:06:20.000
So this is . . . Dawn?

134
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:20.319
<v HOLMAN>Correct.

135
00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:20.639
<v ARSENEAULT>Dawn . . .

136
00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:21.278
<v HOLMAN>Dawn Lee.

137
00:06:21.278 --> 00:06:22.875
<v ARSENEAULT>This is Dawn Lee . . . Holman?

138
00:06:22.875 --> 00:06:23.194
<v HOLMAN>Holman.

139
00:06:23.194 --> 00:06:23.514
H-O-L-M-A-N.

140
00:06:23.514 --> 00:06:23.833
<v ARSENEAULT>M-A-N.

141
00:06:23.833 --> 00:06:25.750
And this is your oldest daughter?

142
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.347
<v LEE>This is my only child.

143
00:06:27.347 --> 00:06:28.944
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re only . . . the only child.

144
00:06:28.944 --> 00:06:29.903
<v LEE>The only child.

145
00:06:29.903 --> 00:06:30.542
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

146
00:06:30.542 --> 00:06:31.500
<v LEE>The only child.

147
00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.417
And her name is Dawn Nicole . . .

148
00:06:33.417 --> 00:06:34.694
<v ARSENEAULT>Nicole . . . And she’s here . . .

149
00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:38.528
<v LEE>Because we always called her “Nicki” and that’s why I said that.

150
00:06:38.528 --> 00:06:40.125
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s like my Aunt Nicky.

151
00:06:40.125 --> 00:06:43.000
And she’s here to help Ms. Theresa remember.

152
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:43.589
<v LEE>Yes.

153
00:06:43.589 --> 00:06:47.123
And to say, “Mom move on.”

154
00:06:47.123 --> 00:06:54.190
Okay so the fourth child, Paul, we were only fourteen months apart.

155
00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:55.368
Very close.

156
00:06:55.368 --> 00:07:03.025
We were in preschool together, elementary school together, high school together, college together.

157
00:07:03.025 --> 00:07:07.736
When he went off to Germany I cried.

158
00:07:07.736 --> 00:07:10.092
I couldn’t help it.

159
00:07:10.092 --> 00:07:13.626
And then when he passed [crying] . . .

160
00:07:13.626 --> 00:07:15.982
<v ARSENEAULT>Y’all were very close.

161
00:07:15.982 --> 00:07:23.049
<v LEE>When he passed it was like a part of me gone forever.

162
00:07:23.049 --> 00:07:34.828
And he was in the army and of course he had . . . He was also a graduate of Grambling State University.

163
00:07:34.828 --> 00:07:38.362
He worked in the school system.

164
00:07:38.362 --> 00:07:44.252
He moved to Georgia and . . . this is where he passed.

165
00:07:44.252 --> 00:07:46.607
<v ARSENEAULT>He passed in Georgia?

166
00:07:46.607 --> 00:07:47.196
<v LEE>[Agrees].

167
00:07:47.196 --> 00:07:47.785
Yes.

168
00:07:47.785 --> 00:07:50.141
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

169
00:07:50.141 --> 00:07:52.497
<v LEE>He passed in 2010.

170
00:07:52.497 --> 00:07:58.387
Matter of fact, in 2010 we lost four important people.

171
00:07:58.387 --> 00:08:03.687
My mother died Valentine’s Day February fourteenth in 2010.

172
00:08:03.687 --> 00:08:06.632
He died September second, 2010.

173
00:08:06.632 --> 00:08:09.577
Richard died December twelfth, 2010.

174
00:08:09.577 --> 00:08:16.644
And then on December thirty-first, my aunt and my daughter’s godmother passed.

175
00:08:16.644 --> 00:08:19.000
So 2010 was . . .

176
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.701
<v ARSENEAULT>Was a hard year.

177
00:08:20.701 --> 00:08:22.402
<v LEE>. . . Not a good year.

178
00:08:22.402 --> 00:08:24.102
Not a good year.

179
00:08:24.102 --> 00:08:25.803
Not a good year.

180
00:08:25.803 --> 00:08:27.079
<v ARSENEAULT>And after Paul . . .

181
00:08:27.079 --> 00:08:29.630
<v LEE>And after Paul, my sister Sylvia.

182
00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:32.181
She passed in 2012 of cancer.

183
00:08:32.181 --> 00:08:35.583
And she was a secretary for many years.

184
00:08:35.583 --> 00:08:37.283
She worked in California.

185
00:08:37.283 --> 00:08:38.984
She worked in Houston.

186
00:08:38.984 --> 00:08:40.685
And she settled in

187
00:08:40.685 --> 00:08:43.661
<c.p>New Iberia and that’s where she passed.

188
00:08:43.661 --> 00:08:48.339
So all of the children either graduated college or trade school.

189
00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:50.039
All ten of us.

190
00:08:50.039 --> 00:08:53.016
So now she is the what, fourth?

191
00:08:53.016 --> 00:08:54.717
<v HOLMAN>[Next to the fifth?]

192
00:08:54.717 --> 00:08:56.417
<v LEE>She’s the fifth? [laughs].

193
00:08:56.417 --> 00:08:58.118
And then there’s Henry.

194
00:08:58.118 --> 00:08:59.394
Henry’s a minister.

195
00:08:59.394 --> 00:09:01.520
He went to trade school.

196
00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.921
He became a welder and he’s now retired.

197
00:09:04.921 --> 00:09:09.173
He lives in Franklin, which is right out of Jeanerette.

198
00:09:09.173 --> 00:09:11.299
And then there is Dorothy.

199
00:09:11.299 --> 00:09:12.150
No, Gwen.

200
00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.000
<v HOLMAN>Gwen.

201
00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:13.424
<v LEE>Gwendolyn.

202
00:09:13.424 --> 00:09:15.545
Gwendolyn is a truck driver.

203
00:09:15.545 --> 00:09:19.361
She drives tour buses, and she is just gung-ho.

204
00:09:19.361 --> 00:09:23.178
I think she was the tomboy of the family.

205
00:09:23.178 --> 00:09:24.026
Beautiful person.

206
00:09:24.026 --> 00:09:27.419
Then of course the next one is Dorothy.

207
00:09:27.419 --> 00:09:30.812
Dorothy and I are exactly ten years apart.

208
00:09:30.812 --> 00:09:36.749
I made seventy on April the ninth, and in August she will be sixty.

209
00:09:36.749 --> 00:09:38.021
Is that correct?

210
00:09:38.021 --> 00:09:38.445
Okay.

211
00:09:38.445 --> 00:09:38.869
Alright.

212
00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:40.990
She lives in New Iberia.

213
00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:46.079
She’s the one who took care of Mother after my father passed.

214
00:09:46.079 --> 00:09:51.592
She took Mother into her household and they were joined at the hip.

215
00:09:51.592 --> 00:09:57.105
And she reminded my mother I think so much of my mother’s mother.

216
00:09:57.105 --> 00:09:59.225
They were very much alike.

217
00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:01.346
She’s a beautiful person, Dorothy.

218
00:10:01.346 --> 00:10:07.707
So we all are so grateful for how well she took care of our mother.

219
00:10:07.707 --> 00:10:12.372
And then of course the next one, Diane, she graduated also.

220
00:10:12.372 --> 00:10:14.068
Became a secretary also.

221
00:10:14.068 --> 00:10:19.581
She drove trucks for a while and now she works as a dispatcher.

222
00:10:19.581 --> 00:10:22.126
And then the baby girl, Sandra.

223
00:10:22.126 --> 00:10:30.183
Sandra was my flower girl at the age of six and she just thought Richard was stealing me away.

224
00:10:30.183 --> 00:10:32.304
She cried during the entire . . .

225
00:10:32.304 --> 00:10:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>She didn’t understand.

226
00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:34.420
<v LEE>Right.

227
00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.100
During the entire ceremony.

228
00:10:36.100 --> 00:10:38.620
She did not understand at all.

229
00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:40.720
And so that is it.

230
00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:41.560
Ten children.

231
00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:42.820
<v ARSENEAULT>All the siblings.

232
00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:43.240
Wow.

233
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:43.660
<v LEE>Yeah.

234
00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:49.540
And so two are dead, are gone on, and the other eight are living.

235
00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:53.740
And the oldest is seventy-six and the youngest is fifty . . .

236
00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:54.160
<v HOLMAN>Fifty-two.

237
00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:55.000
<v LEE>Fifty-two.

238
00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:56.067
<v ARSENEAULT>Sandra is still living?

239
00:10:56.067 --> 00:10:56.333
<v LEE>Yes.

240
00:10:56.333 --> 00:10:56.867
<v ARSENEAULT>She’s fifty-two?

241
00:10:56.867 --> 00:10:57.933
<v LEE>And she’s fifty-two [agrees].

242
00:10:57.933 --> 00:10:58.200
Yes.

243
00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.333
<v ARSENEAULT>So you all graduated trade school or college?

244
00:11:00.333 --> 00:11:00.867
<v LEE>Or college.

245
00:11:00.867 --> 00:11:01.667
That is correct.

246
00:11:01.667 --> 00:11:04.600
<v ARSENEAULT>So how were you . . . How did your parents bring you up?

247
00:11:04.600 --> 00:11:07.000
What can you tell me about your upbringing?

248
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.288
<v LEE>Oh my God it . . .

249
00:11:08.288 --> 00:11:10.219
<v ARSENEAULT>Well first, where were you born?

250
00:11:10.219 --> 00:11:11.829
<v LEE>In Lafayette at Lafayette Hospital.

251
00:11:11.829 --> 00:11:16.658
But . . . And this is where you went most of them because you went to Lafayette.

252
00:11:16.658 --> 00:11:21.164
Most of the persons were born in that area were at the Lafayette Hospital.

253
00:11:21.164 --> 00:11:23.096
<v ARSENEAULT>Was anyone born in the home?

254
00:11:23.096 --> 00:11:24.384
<v LEE>None of us were.

255
00:11:24.384 --> 00:11:24.705
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

256
00:11:24.705 --> 00:11:25.993
<v LEE>None of us were.

257
00:11:25.993 --> 00:11:30.178
Now there were . . . midwives, but none of us were born in the home.

258
00:11:30.178 --> 00:11:32.432
But we had a very strict upbringing.

259
00:11:32.432 --> 00:11:35.007
Dad said you have to go to church.

260
00:11:35.007 --> 00:11:39.192
You have to go to BTU, and Sunday school, and all of that.

261
00:11:39.192 --> 00:11:42.733
And matter of fact, we saw the church from our home.

262
00:11:42.733 --> 00:11:45.308
<c.p>It was like just through a little path.

263
00:11:45.308 --> 00:11:50.137
We could cut through the path or use the street to get to the church.

264
00:11:50.137 --> 00:11:50.459
Okay.

265
00:11:50.459 --> 00:11:54.000
And let’s see what else I can say about that.

266
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:56.079
<v ARSENEAULT>What does your father do for a living?

267
00:11:56.079 --> 00:11:59.457
<v LEE>My father was a . . . I guess you would call it a boiler maker.

268
00:11:59.457 --> 00:12:02.835
He worked at a sugar mill [Saint Mary Co-op] at the very beginning.

269
00:12:02.835 --> 00:12:05.953
And then after that, close to his retirement, he became a . . . Cut.

270
00:12:05.953 --> 00:12:07.772
I’m trying to think of the name.

271
00:12:07.772 --> 00:12:12.189
A guard. [Lee adds: Father was the first black employee of J &amp; L Iron Works of Jeanerette.

272
00:12:12.189 --> 00:12:15.827
He then worked at Albania Sugar Mill and retired from Saint Mary Sugar Co-op.]

273
00:12:15.827 --> 00:12:16.346
<v ARSENEAULT>A guard?

274
00:12:16.346 --> 00:12:17.126
<v LEE>Yeah, a guard.

275
00:12:17.126 --> 00:12:18.165
Yes, yes, yes, yes.

276
00:12:18.165 --> 00:12:20.764
<v ARSENEAULT>So the sugar mill, what was his work there like?

277
00:12:20.764 --> 00:12:21.803
What were his responsibilities?

278
00:12:21.803 --> 00:12:23.102
<v LEE>Oh, if I can remember.

279
00:12:23.102 --> 00:12:24.142
I need a pause.

280
00:12:24.142 --> 00:12:24.661
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, sure.

281
00:12:24.661 --> 00:12:25.961
<v LEE>The brain is not functioning.

282
00:12:25.961 --> 00:12:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>We can pause . . .

283
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.754
<v LEE>Yeah yes, yes yes yes yes yes [break in tape].

284
00:12:29.754 --> 00:12:35.536
Working at the sugar mill, certainly as you drove here from Baton Rouge to here in Lafayette and New Iberia area,

285
00:12:35.536 --> 00:12:38.565
certainly you saw sugar cane on both sides of the interstate.

286
00:12:38.565 --> 00:12:39.391
And that’s where . . .

287
00:12:39.391 --> 00:12:41.319
<v HOLMAN>Sugar cane festival is held every year . . .

288
00:12:41.319 --> 00:12:41.870
<v LEE>Sugar cane . . .

289
00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:42.420
<v HOLMAN>. . . In November.

290
00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:42.696
<v LEE>[Agrees].

291
00:12:42.696 --> 00:12:43.522
<v HOLMAN>In that area.

292
00:12:43.522 --> 00:12:44.072
<v ARSENEAULT>In Lafayette?

293
00:12:44.072 --> 00:12:44.348
<v HOLMAN>Jeanerette.

294
00:12:44.348 --> 00:12:44.899
<v ARSENEAULT>In Jeanerette?

295
00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:46.000
<v LEE>In Jeanerette [agrees].

296
00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:46.504
<v ARSENEAULT>Sugarcane festival.

297
00:12:46.504 --> 00:12:46.756
<v LEE>Yes.

298
00:12:46.756 --> 00:12:48.519
<v ARSENEAULT>And your father worked in the mill?

299
00:12:48.519 --> 00:12:49.526
<v LEE>In the mill, right.

300
00:12:49.526 --> 00:12:49.778
<v ARSENEAULT>Doing . . .

301
00:12:49.778 --> 00:12:50.785
<v HOLMAN>In the production line.

302
00:12:50.785 --> 00:12:51.793
<v LEE>In the production line.

303
00:12:51.793 --> 00:12:52.044
Right.

304
00:12:52.044 --> 00:12:52.800
That is correct.

305
00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.319
<v ARSENEAULT>In the production . . . Did he ever talk about work or . . .?

306
00:12:55.319 --> 00:12:56.074
<v LEE>He loved it.

307
00:12:56.074 --> 00:12:56.830
He loved it.

308
00:12:56.830 --> 00:13:00.356
And he . . . and the sugar canes were actually . . . Well you could really eat them.

309
00:13:00.356 --> 00:13:01.111
They were good.

310
00:13:01.111 --> 00:13:02.622
They were purple and real fat.

311
00:13:02.622 --> 00:13:04.637
He would always bring us stalks from work.

312
00:13:04.637 --> 00:13:09.674
We would see him coming down when he would . . . Down the road we would see him coming, “Oh, there’s Dad.

313
00:13:09.674 --> 00:13:12.444
So he’s bringing us sugar cane leaning them against the house.”

314
00:13:12.444 --> 00:13:13.704
Those were the good days.

315
00:13:13.704 --> 00:13:15.467
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn’t know you could eat it.

316
00:13:15.467 --> 00:13:15.970
<v LEE>Oh, yeah!

317
00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:16.978
They were good then!

318
00:13:16.978 --> 00:13:17.985
They [aren’t?] good now.

319
00:13:17.985 --> 00:13:18.993
They were good then.

320
00:13:18.993 --> 00:13:20.000
They were purple.

321
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:21.068
<v ARSENEAULT>How would you eat it?

322
00:13:21.068 --> 00:13:23.417
<v LEE>You would cut it and then peel the bark off it.

323
00:13:23.417 --> 00:13:24.272
<v ARSENEAULT>And just eat the . . .

324
00:13:24.272 --> 00:13:25.981
<v LEE>And just chew it and spit it out.

325
00:13:25.981 --> 00:13:26.835
You wouldn’t swallow that.

326
00:13:26.835 --> 00:13:30.466
It was just . . . I mean the only thing you would do was just the juices from it.

327
00:13:30.466 --> 00:13:30.893
<v HOLMAN>The juices . . .

328
00:13:30.893 --> 00:13:31.107
<v LEE>Yes.

329
00:13:31.107 --> 00:13:31.320
<v HOLMAN>Juices.

330
00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:32.388
<v ARSENEAULT>I did not know that.

331
00:13:32.388 --> 00:13:32.602
<v LEE>Yeah.

332
00:13:32.602 --> 00:13:35.379
And then of course when they made the brown sugar you had that.

333
00:13:35.379 --> 00:13:38.369
You could make tea cakes or whatever else you wanted with the brown sugar.

334
00:13:38.369 --> 00:13:42.000
Yes. [Lee adds: the bagasse (husk) was used to make ceiling tiles, etc.— nothing was wasted]

335
00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:43.224
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your mom?

336
00:13:43.224 --> 00:13:44.449
What would she do?

337
00:13:44.449 --> 00:13:49.041
<v LEE>My mother was a housekeeper there where she worked for others out of the house.

338
00:13:49.041 --> 00:13:51.490
And that’s what she did as a living.

339
00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:51.796
Yes.

340
00:13:51.796 --> 00:13:56.082
<v ARSENEAULT>So your . . . You said it was strict and you had to go to church.

341
00:13:56.082 --> 00:13:56.388
<v LEE>Absolutely.

342
00:13:56.388 --> 00:13:56.694
Absolutely.

343
00:13:56.694 --> 00:14:04.653
My dad’s best friend was the pastor and after he passed, Dad became the pastor [Lee clarifies: at Dad’s retirement, he became Pastor Emeritus in 1986].

344
00:14:04.653 --> 00:14:08.327
And when he passed he was the Emeritus pastor at that time.

345
00:14:08.327 --> 00:14:15.367
Well a younger man who grew up in the church became that. [Lee adds that Reverend Arthur Lee Alexander is still the pastor]

346
00:14:15.367 --> 00:14:16.592
<v ARSENEAULT>What church was it?

347
00:14:16.592 --> 00:14:18.429
<v LEE>Mount Calvary Baptist Church in Jeanerette.

348
00:14:18.429 --> 00:14:22.408
And Mount Calvary Baptist Church now . . . Which it’s just amazing how things work.

349
00:14:22.408 --> 00:14:27.918
The school that I graduated from for instance, Francis Marion Boley High School, was bought by the church.

350
00:14:27.918 --> 00:14:34.347
And now the gym of the church has become . . . I mean the gym of the school has become the church now.

351
00:14:34.347 --> 00:14:36.184
So it’s a much bigger place.

352
00:14:36.184 --> 00:14:41.388
And the classrooms that I went to, those classrooms are now used as classrooms for the church.

353
00:14:41.388 --> 00:14:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

354
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.893
<v LEE>So the Mount Calvary moved to a bigger facility.

355
00:14:44.893 --> 00:14:46.179
<v ARSENEAULT>So it’s gotten bigger.

356
00:14:46.179 --> 00:14:47.464
<v LEE>It’s gotten bigger, yes.

357
00:14:47.464 --> 00:14:51.964
<v ARSENEAULT>And your grandparent’s origins . . . do you know what brought your family to that area?

358
00:14:51.964 --> 00:14:52.607
<v LEE>I don’t.

359
00:14:52.607 --> 00:14:52.929
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

360
00:14:52.929 --> 00:14:55.179
<v LEE>I just can’t remember at this time.

361
00:14:55.179 --> 00:14:55.821
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s fine.

362
00:14:55.821 --> 00:15:01.607
I just like to ask just in case there’s like a story about how the family got there.

363
00:15:01.607 --> 00:15:03.536
Some people like to, you know . . .

364
00:15:03.536 --> 00:15:03.857
<v LEE>Yeah.

365
00:15:03.857 --> 00:15:08.357
I know my dad and mom did move to Galveston [Texas] for two years.

366
00:15:08.357 --> 00:15:14.786
This is my . . . my second sister was born in Galveston and after that they moved back to Jeanerette [in 1945].

367
00:15:14.786 --> 00:15:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And your great grandparents, you remember anything about them?

368
00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:27.559
<v LEE>No. Keep in mind now that I never knew my grandfather because he died the year before I was born and his parents are . . .

369
00:15:27.559 --> 00:15:28.356
<v ARSENEAULT>Long gone.

370
00:15:28.356 --> 00:15:28.754
<v LEE>Right.

371
00:15:28.754 --> 00:15:33.932
And so I did not know her grandparents . . . My mother’s grandparents either, so.

372
00:15:33.932 --> 00:15:34.331
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

373
00:15:34.331 --> 00:15:39.508
And any family origin stories that were passed down or anything like that?

374
00:15:39.508 --> 00:15:41.500
Like, “Our family’s from here.”

375
00:15:41.500 --> 00:15:45.881
Or anybody in the family a genealogist, or trace it back?

376
00:15:45.881 --> 00:15:50.263
<v LEE>My oldest sister’s oldest daughter is working on that for us.

377
00:15:50.263 --> 00:15:50.661
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

378
00:15:50.661 --> 00:15:51.458
<v LEE>And so . . .

379
00:15:51.458 --> 00:15:54.246
<v ARSENEAULT>So you don’t really know just yet . . .

380
00:15:54.246 --> 00:15:55.441
<v LEE>That is correct.

381
00:15:55.441 --> 00:15:56.636
<v ARSENEAULT>I got you.

382
00:15:56.636 --> 00:16:05.000
And so your father worked at the sugar mill, your mom was a housekeeper, and probably a home keeper too.

383
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.291
<v LEE>Yes.

384
00:16:05.291 --> 00:16:06.164
All of that.

385
00:16:06.164 --> 00:16:08.201
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a garden growing up?

386
00:16:08.201 --> 00:16:08.783
<v LEE>Yes, yes.

387
00:16:08.783 --> 00:16:11.693
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you tell me a little bit about the garden?

388
00:16:11.693 --> 00:16:11.984
<v LEE>Yes.

389
00:16:11.984 --> 00:16:13.439
My . . . As I mentioned earlier,

390
00:16:13.439 --> 00:16:19.259
my father’s mother had the garden and so it was spread out her yard, the yard between us, and Dad.

391
00:16:19.259 --> 00:16:22.169
So Dad took care of all of that with her.

392
00:16:22.169 --> 00:16:24.497
And of course you had the normal things.

393
00:16:24.497 --> 00:16:28.571
The tomatoes, the okra, and all of that . . . string beans and all of that.

394
00:16:28.571 --> 00:16:29.444
<v ARSENEAULT>Which she canned?

395
00:16:29.444 --> 00:16:31.190
<v LEE>Now we thought . . . Oh, she did.

396
00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:31.772
She did.

397
00:16:31.772 --> 00:16:34.392
Now we thought of Jeanerette as being the city.

398
00:16:34.392 --> 00:16:41.085
And so when we went to my mother’s hometown which was Patoutville on the outskirts, we thought we were going to the country.

399
00:16:41.085 --> 00:16:43.704
And so it was there that they butchered hogs.

400
00:16:43.704 --> 00:16:45.741
And we would always go out there.

401
00:16:45.741 --> 00:16:49.524
And they thought when we came, well we were the city girls, you

402
00:16:49.524 --> 00:16:49.815
<c.p>see.

403
00:16:49.815 --> 00:16:50.106
Yeah.

404
00:16:50.106 --> 00:16:52.725
And they were . . . It was just a grand time.

405
00:16:52.725 --> 00:16:54.471
We looked forward to those days.

406
00:16:54.471 --> 00:16:56.508
And that was on my mother’s side.

407
00:16:56.508 --> 00:16:57.672
My mother’s aunt’s side.

408
00:16:57.672 --> 00:16:58.545
Her mother’s sister.

409
00:16:58.545 --> 00:17:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And they would butcher?

410
00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:00.310
<v LEE>Yes.

411
00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.167
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that?

412
00:17:02.167 --> 00:17:04.333
<v LEE>Oh, it was great going out there.

413
00:17:04.333 --> 00:17:05.262
You felt important.

414
00:17:05.262 --> 00:17:06.190
It was great.

415
00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:09.286
The fireplaces that were tall enough you could walk in.

416
00:17:09.286 --> 00:17:10.214
Just that tall.

417
00:17:10.214 --> 00:17:10.524
Yes.

418
00:17:10.524 --> 00:17:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s where they would smoke the meat?

419
00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:18.000
<v LEE>No, they would do it outside but then what she did . . . She did some cooking inside.

420
00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.500
But it’s just how big those fireplaces were.

421
00:17:20.500 --> 00:17:20.812
Yeah.

422
00:17:20.812 --> 00:17:22.062
<v ARSENEAULT>You said your mother’s . . .

423
00:17:22.062 --> 00:17:23.312
<v LEE>My mother’s, mother’s, sister.

424
00:17:23.312 --> 00:17:23.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Sister . . .

425
00:17:23.625 --> 00:17:24.562
<v LEE>So her aunt.

426
00:17:24.562 --> 00:17:25.500
My mother’s aunt.

427
00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:25.812
<v ARSENEAULT>Gotcha.

428
00:17:25.812 --> 00:17:28.000
And who was the good cook?

429
00:17:28.000 --> 00:17:29.200
<v LEE>The aunt . . . Aunt Elsie.

430
00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:29.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

431
00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:31.000
<v LEE>Aunt Elise Jenkins [Hills].

432
00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:33.224
<v ARSENEAULT>So Elzie Jenkins was the good cook.

433
00:17:33.224 --> 00:17:33.542
<v LEE>Yes.

434
00:17:33.542 --> 00:17:35.766
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things would she cook?

435
00:17:35.766 --> 00:17:36.720
<v LEE>Oh my goodness.

436
00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.308
Well the okra for sure.

437
00:17:38.308 --> 00:17:41.486
And then of course the butchering and all of that.

438
00:17:41.486 --> 00:17:43.710
She took care of all of that.

439
00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:46.570
They made the boudin, the cracklings . . . All of that.

440
00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.794
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they make cracklins I wonder?

441
00:17:48.794 --> 00:17:50.383
<v LEE>Well in the big pots.

442
00:17:50.383 --> 00:17:50.583
They had the big pots in the big . . . Matter of fact, when we lived in Mossville, Richard . . .

443
00:17:50.383 --> 00:17:55.785
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

444
00:17:55.785 --> 00:17:58.327
<v LEE>They cooked in the back all the time.

445
00:17:58.327 --> 00:18:00.234
We had three acres in Mossville.

446
00:18:00.234 --> 00:18:01.822
<v HOLMAN>Just buy slabs of fat.

447
00:18:01.822 --> 00:18:02.458
Cook that.

448
00:18:02.458 --> 00:18:02.776
<v LEE>Yeah.

449
00:18:02.776 --> 00:18:05.000
<v HOLMAN>Soak it and fry it basically.

450
00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:05.647
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah.

451
00:18:05.647 --> 00:18:08.235
Actually using the water in the big pots.

452
00:18:08.235 --> 00:18:14.706
We still had the pots in the . . . And I’ve got a board I’m thinking in the garage that they used.

453
00:18:14.706 --> 00:18:16.000
One of the things.

454
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:16.324
Yeah.

455
00:18:16.324 --> 00:18:20.529
<v ARSENEAULT>So does your mom have any like famous recipes or anything like that?

456
00:18:20.529 --> 00:18:21.824
<v LEE>Mother was a seamstress.

457
00:18:21.824 --> 00:18:24.088
Mother would buy a bolt of material.

458
00:18:24.088 --> 00:18:28.294
She would make . . . with all of the girls . . . Eight girls, two boys, okay?

459
00:18:28.294 --> 00:18:30.235
She would make all our dresses.

460
00:18:30.235 --> 00:18:33.147
She made curtains for her house and for others.

461
00:18:33.147 --> 00:18:35.412
Most of all that’s what she was.

462
00:18:35.412 --> 00:18:38.971
And she taught all eight of her girls how to sew.

463
00:18:38.971 --> 00:18:43.500
So when we took home economics in high school we didn’t have any problems.

464
00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:44.794
<v ARSENEAULT>You already knew how.

465
00:18:44.794 --> 00:18:45.765
<v LEE>We knew how.

466
00:18:45.765 --> 00:18:46.088
Yeah.

467
00:18:46.088 --> 00:18:50.618
She would get . . . I mean newspaper and cut her patterns out of the newspaper.

468
00:18:50.618 --> 00:18:52.235
It was just like that.

469
00:18:52.235 --> 00:18:55.147
She couldn’t afford to buy patterns from the stores.

470
00:18:55.147 --> 00:18:56.441
So she made her . . .

471
00:18:56.441 --> 00:18:58.059
<v ARSENEAULT>So she made them herself.

472
00:18:58.059 --> 00:18:58.382
<v LEE>Yeah.

473
00:18:58.382 --> 00:18:58.706
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

474
00:18:58.706 --> 00:19:00.000
<v LEE>She was great.

475
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.333
<v ARSENEAULT>I wonder if that’s the Depression Era.

476
00:19:02.333 --> 00:19:03.333
<v LEE>Probably so, huh?

477
00:19:03.333 --> 00:19:03.667
Yeah.

478
00:19:03.667 --> 00:19:04.333
Yeah see . . .

479
00:19:04.333 --> 00:19:05.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Everybody learned how to . . .

480
00:19:05.667 --> 00:19:06.000
<v LEE>Yeah.

481
00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . To survive.

482
00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:15.269
<v LEE>See because she was eighty-seven when she passed and that was in 2010 and when you subtract that, you know when she was born.

483
00:19:15.269 --> 00:19:19.403
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she ever talk about the Depression Era or anything like that?

484
00:19:19.403 --> 00:19:21.471
<v LEE>No, not . . . No she never did.

485
00:19:21.471 --> 00:19:22.849
But you know what?

486
00:19:22.849 --> 00:19:29.050
Mother was a mother who was so proud and she stood tall and with her head up high.

487
00:19:29.050 --> 00:19:34.908
And she always said, “Don’t worry what others think of you, but what you think of yourself.”

488
00:19:34.908 --> 00:19:38.697
I have a picture . . . There’s a picture of my mom there.

489
00:19:38.697 --> 00:19:39.731
And my dad.

490
00:19:39.731 --> 00:19:40.420
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wow.

491
00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:44.555
<v LEE>And then that’s a picture of Elaine, Richard’s mother, and his step-father.

492
00:19:44.555 --> 00:19:44.899
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

493
00:19:44.899 --> 00:19:46.622
<v LEE>And that’s his father there.

494
00:19:46.622 --> 00:19:48.000
<v HOLMAN>Richard Lee, Senior.

495
00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:48.935
<v LEE>Richard Lee, Senior.

496
00:19:48.935 --> 00:19:49.247
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

497
00:19:49.247 --> 00:19:49.558
<v LEE>Yeah.

498
00:19:49.558 --> 00:19:50.805
And with his beagles.

499
00:19:50.805 --> 00:19:51.740
He loved beagles.

500
00:19:51.740 --> 00:19:52.052
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?] father.

501
00:19:52.052 --> 00:19:52.675
<v LEE>Kenny’s father.

502
00:19:52.675 --> 00:19:52.987
Yeah.

503
00:19:52.987 --> 00:19:53.299
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

504
00:19:53.299 --> 00:19:54.545
And that’s the land.

505
00:19:54.545 --> 00:19:56.416
They love the land don’t they?

506
00:19:56.416 --> 00:19:57.039
<v LEE>Oh my.

507
00:19:57.039 --> 00:19:57.351
Absolutely.

508
00:19:57.351 --> 00:19:58.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Wide open spaces.

509
00:19:58.286 --> 00:19:58.597
<v LEE>Absolutely.

510
00:19:58.597 --> 00:20:00.779
That’s one thing I liked about Mossville.

511
00:20:00.779 --> 00:20:04.831
The neighbors were good but you weren’t so close that . . . but close enough.

512
00:20:04.831 --> 00:20:07.636
<v ARSENEAULT>So tell me about . . . I’m trying to stay chronological.

513
00:20:07.636 --> 00:20:07.948
<v LEE>Okay.

514
00:20:07.948 --> 00:20:08.260
Okay.

515
00:20:08.260 --> 00:20:09.818
Stay with me too, huh?

516
00:20:09.818 --> 00:20:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Definitely want to get to Mossville.

517
00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:12.293
<v LEE>Okay.

518
00:20:12.293 --> 00:20:16.402
<v ARSENEAULT>But growing up, could you tell me more about your community and your neighborhood?

519
00:20:16.402 --> 00:20:17.576
What was that like?

520
00:20:17.576 --> 00:20:19.043
Like who were your neighbors?

521
00:20:19.043 --> 00:20:19.337
And . . .

522
00:20:19.337 --> 00:20:21.391
<v LEE>You’re talking about in the Jeanerette area?

523
00:20:21.391 --> 00:20:22.272
<v ARSENEAULT>In Jeanerette [Agrees].

524
00:20:22.272 --> 00:20:24.620
<v LEE>Yes and they were so God fearing people.

525
00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:26.674
We lived on a dead end street.

526
00:20:26.674 --> 00:20:29.902
The lady [Viola Francis] across the street was my math teacher.

527
00:20:29.902 --> 00:20:36.359
My cousin [Catherine Whitehead Jones] who lived next door, the one I talked about who graduated Grambling, she was my business teacher.

528
00:20:36.359 --> 00:20:37.239
So . . . Oh, yes.

529
00:20:37.239 --> 00:20:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So everyone . . . It was close.

530
00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:39.290
<v LEE>Yes.

531
00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:40.160
Very much so.

532
00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.352
It was actually as they say now, “Village raising a child.”

533
00:20:43.352 --> 00:20:47.123
Dad knew what had happened down the road before we would get home.

534
00:20:47.123 --> 00:20:49.444
And you had what you called party lines.

535
00:20:49.444 --> 00:20:50.025
The phones.

536
00:20:50.025 --> 00:20:53.796
Where you called and someone else might have been talking on the line.

537
00:20:53.796 --> 00:20:56.407
Calling Mom to let her know what had happened.

538
00:20:56.407 --> 00:20:57.568
So it was just . . .

539
00:20:57.568 --> 00:21:00.469
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you grow up with a phone in the house?

540
00:21:00.469 --> 00:21:00.759
<v LEE>Yeah.

541
00:21:00.759 --> 00:21:01.630
Yeah we did.

542
00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:02.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was . . .

543
00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:03.080
<v LEE>We did.

544
00:21:03.080 --> 00:21:05.691
But we had a party line for a while.

545
00:21:05.691 --> 00:21:07.142
I remember that black phone.

546
00:21:07.142 --> 00:21:07.722
Certainly did.

547
00:21:07.722 --> 00:21:09.753
<v ARSENEAULT>And that’s when you’d have to share . . .?

548
00:21:09.753 --> 00:21:10.914
<v LEE>Yeah with another party.

549
00:21:10.914 --> 00:21:11.204
Yes.

550
00:21:11.204 --> 00:21:11.494
Absolutely.

551
00:21:11.494 --> 00:21:12.654
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever eavesdrop?

552
00:21:12.654 --> 00:21:12.944
<v LEE>Yes.

553
00:21:12.944 --> 00:21:15.846
I would be lying if I need to . . . But yes.

554
00:21:15.846 --> 00:21:17.586
And I’m sure they did too.

555
00:21:17.586 --> 00:21:22.809
<v ARSENEAULT>I can just tell you I ask that question to everybody and they always say that they did.

556
00:21:22.809 --> 00:21:23.099
<v LEE>Yeah!

557
00:21:23.099 --> 00:21:25.420
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s when I get my funny story [laughs].

558
00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:26.000
<v LEE>Yeah!

559
00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:28.734
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s like even if you don’t mean to . . .

560
00:21:28.734 --> 00:21:29.076
<v LEE>Yeah!

561
00:21:29.076 --> 00:21:30.443
It’s like, “Okay . . .” Yeah.

562
00:21:30.443 --> 00:21:35.911
My mother was president of the usher board at church and they always had fundraising projects.

563
00:21:35.911 --> 00:21:43.089
And they had what they called a lemon squeeze where you would take the lemon put it on this little thing,

564
00:21:43.089 --> 00:21:49.241
squeeze it, and if you could . . . The person who had the most seeds would win whatever the prize.

565
00:21:49.241 --> 00:21:50.266
It was neat.

566
00:21:50.266 --> 00:21:53.000
And they went from house to house.

567
00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:54.339
<v ARSENEAULT>To do fundraising?

568
00:21:54.339 --> 00:21:56.125
<v LEE>To do . . . Yes [agrees].

569
00:21:56.125 --> 00:21:56.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Huh.

570
00:21:56.571 --> 00:21:57.911
<v LEE>It was neat.

571
00:21:57.911 --> 00:22:00.589
<v ARSENEAULT>That was for the Baptist church?

572
00:22:00.589 --> 00:22:01.036
<v LEE>Yes.

573
00:22:01.036 --> 00:22:04.161
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y’all have any other church traditions?

574
00:22:04.161 --> 00:22:05.054
Or . . . Cakewalks?

575
00:22:05.054 --> 00:22:05.946
<v LEE>Cakewalks, yes.

576
00:22:05.946 --> 00:22:06.393
Yes.

577
00:22:06.393 --> 00:22:07.732
All of that.

578
00:22:07.732 --> 00:22:08.625
Certainly did.

579
00:22:08.625 --> 00:22:11.304
<v ARSENEAULT>The queen . . . What was another one?

580
00:22:11.304 --> 00:22:13.536
A queen . . . A penny rally?

581
00:22:13.536 --> 00:22:14.875
A penny march?

582
00:22:14.875 --> 00:22:18.000
<v LEE>A penny march, a shoe rally . . .

583
00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:18.453
<v HOLMAN>Pew rally.

584
00:22:18.453 --> 00:22:19.811
<v LEE>A pew rally . . . A shoe rally.

585
00:22:19.811 --> 00:22:21.396
<v ARSENEAULT>I haven’t heard about a shoe rally.

586
00:22:21.396 --> 00:22:22.528
You have to tell me.

587
00:22:22.528 --> 00:22:25.245
<v LEE>Well a shoe rally goes according to the size the shoe is.

588
00:22:25.245 --> 00:22:27.962
So if you wear size ten, you have to pay ten dollars.

589
00:22:27.962 --> 00:22:28.642
Five, five dollars.

590
00:22:28.642 --> 00:22:30.000
So a dollar per size.

591
00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:30.646
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

592
00:22:30.646 --> 00:22:31.291
Any other . . .?

593
00:22:31.291 --> 00:22:31.937
<v HOLMAN>Pew rally.

594
00:22:31.937 --> 00:22:33.551
<v LEE>Oh, and the pew rally.

595
00:22:33.551 --> 00:22:34.843
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s a pew rally?

596
00:22:34.843 --> 00:22:38.717
<v LEE>The number of people you can get to sit in your area.

597
00:22:38.717 --> 00:22:42.591
If you got the most, you got some kind of a prize.

598
00:22:42.591 --> 00:22:42.791
First, second, and third prize.

599
00:22:42.591 --> 00:22:44.205
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

600
00:22:44.205 --> 00:22:44.528
<v LEE>Yeah.

601
00:22:44.528 --> 00:22:47.110
Almost like family day or something I’m thinking.

602
00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:47.433
Yeah.

603
00:22:47.433 --> 00:22:47.756
Yeah.

604
00:22:47.756 --> 00:22:48.079
<v ARSENEAULT>Interesting.

605
00:22:48.079 --> 00:22:48.402
<v LEE>Yeah.

606
00:22:48.402 --> 00:22:57.764
I think the most important thing that sticks to me . . . Even at Mount . . . and the thing about it . . . I was brought up in Mount Calvary Baptist Church in Jeanerette.

607
00:22:57.764 --> 00:23:02.929
And when I moved here I . . . We finally settled to another Mount Calvary in Lake Charles.

608
00:23:02.929 --> 00:23:04.866
I feel so much at home.

609
00:23:04.866 --> 00:23:09.709
One thing that touches me so much in the Baptist church is Lord Supper Day.

610
00:23:09.709 --> 00:23:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

611
00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.106
<v LEE>When we take Communion and the memories come back.

612
00:23:14.106 --> 00:23:18.248
When we were at home in Jeanerette they even set the table.

613
00:23:18.248 --> 00:23:26.186
And the deacons of the church, all twelve of them, sat around the table and the pastor sat where Jesus would have sat.

614
00:23:26.186 --> 00:23:33.088
And they go through the blessing of the wine and the bread and then it’s passed out to the congregation.

615
00:23:33.088 --> 00:23:35.850
And the lights were turned down real dim.

616
00:23:35.850 --> 00:23:36.195
Beautiful.

617
00:23:36.195 --> 00:23:37.920
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it . . . once a year?

618
00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:38.956
<v LEE>No. every month.

619
00:23:38.956 --> 00:23:39.991
Once a month.

620
00:23:39.991 --> 00:23:41.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Once a month.

621
00:23:41.027 --> 00:23:42.062
<v LEE>Once a month.

622
00:23:42.062 --> 00:23:42.407
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

623
00:23:42.407 --> 00:23:42.752
<v LEE>Yeah.

624
00:23:42.752 --> 00:23:43.788
Once a month.

625
00:23:43.788 --> 00:23:44.133
Yeah.

626
00:23:44.133 --> 00:23:46.894
<v ARSENEAULT>Any other traditions or any you can remember?

627
00:23:46.894 --> 00:23:50.000
I know I’m putting you on the spot . . .

628
00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:51.953
<v LEE>Well we had Easter egg hunts.

629
00:23:51.953 --> 00:23:53.256
We always had that.

630
00:23:53.256 --> 00:23:58.140
And the older children would be responsible for boiling the eggs for the Sunday school.

631
00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:00.744
And so Geraldine [Edwards Williams], she’s passed now,

632
00:24:00.744 --> 00:24:06.279
and I . . . when we were . . . about to go off to Grambling . . . We were then the older teenagers.

633
00:24:06.279 --> 00:24:13.442
So we boiled the eggs and dyed the eggs for the younger people the Sunday [Lee clarifies it was Saturday] before Easter.

634
00:24:13.442 --> 00:24:17.023
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was it like being the child of a pastor?

635
00:24:17.023 --> 00:24:18.000
<v LEE>It was good.

636
00:24:18.000 --> 00:24:18.977
It really was.

637
00:24:18.977 --> 00:24:19.953
It really was.

638
00:24:19.953 --> 00:24:22.558
Someone say, “Well this is all you knew.”

639
00:24:22.558 --> 00:24:26.791
Then he had . . . There was so much respect for him, you felt . . . Hey.

640
00:24:26.791 --> 00:24:27.442
Very important.

641
00:24:27.442 --> 00:24:28.093
But being

642
00:24:28.093 --> 00:24:32.651
<c.p>a pk kid, it was not bad because I was never a difficult child.

643
00:24:32.651 --> 00:24:35.256
My second sister always says, “Theresa never talked.

644
00:24:35.256 --> 00:24:39.488
I mean she was sitting . . . we’d be playing out there watching the clouds.

645
00:24:39.488 --> 00:24:40.791
Looking at the clouds.”

646
00:24:40.791 --> 00:24:42.419
And I said, “Oh well.”

647
00:24:42.419 --> 00:24:42.744
So . . .

648
00:24:42.744 --> 00:24:43.721
<v ARSENEAULT>You were daydreaming.

649
00:24:43.721 --> 00:24:44.698
<v LEE>I guess so.

650
00:24:44.698 --> 00:24:46.000
I was a dreamer.

651
00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.302
I was a dreamer.

652
00:24:47.302 --> 00:24:53.163
Mother said it was okay to be a dreamer but wake up and do something you know, so.

653
00:24:53.163 --> 00:24:53.488
Yeah.

654
00:24:53.488 --> 00:24:53.814
So.

655
00:24:53.814 --> 00:25:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did any of the other siblings have any opinion about being a pastor’s child or anything like that?

656
00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.618
<v LEE>I think the . . . There were like three generations of us, actually.

657
00:25:03.618 --> 00:25:07.237
Like Alzina which is the oldest, Pat, and Paul and Sylvia.

658
00:25:07.237 --> 00:25:08.882
We were . . . It was fine.

659
00:25:08.882 --> 00:25:16.118
But then as we got onto the last one they were like . . . They got a little rebellious you know, that second generation.

660
00:25:16.118 --> 00:25:20.724
And Mother and Dad were getting older and a little bit more lenient so.

661
00:25:20.724 --> 00:25:22.368
<v ARSENEAULT>I sympathize, I’m the oldest.

662
00:25:22.368 --> 00:25:22.697
<v LEE>Yeah.

663
00:25:22.697 --> 00:25:23.684
Oh, you are?

664
00:25:23.684 --> 00:25:24.342
Oh, okay.

665
00:25:24.342 --> 00:25:25.000
Okay.

666
00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:26.620
<v ARSENEAULT>I understand about the leniencies.

667
00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:27.268
<v LEE>Oh yeah.

668
00:25:27.268 --> 00:25:27.592
Yeah.

669
00:25:27.592 --> 00:25:27.792
Yeah.

670
00:25:27.592 --> 00:25:27.915
<v HOLMAN>[. . . ?]

671
00:25:27.915 --> 00:25:28.887
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah, yeah.

672
00:25:28.887 --> 00:25:30.183
<v HOLMAN>We couldn’t play monopoly.

673
00:25:30.183 --> 00:25:30.507
<v LEE>No!

674
00:25:30.507 --> 00:25:31.479
Not with Dad.

675
00:25:31.479 --> 00:25:32.775
Dad was such a . . .

676
00:25:32.775 --> 00:25:34.070
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?] with dice or cards.

677
00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:34.394
<v LEE>Nope.

678
00:25:34.394 --> 00:25:36.662
And he would . . . He loved his grandchildren.

679
00:25:36.662 --> 00:25:39.901
He would come behind the couch when they were playing.

680
00:25:39.901 --> 00:25:42.817
He’d tap them on the shoulder and then duck.

681
00:25:42.817 --> 00:25:44.437
They’d be looking for him.

682
00:25:44.437 --> 00:25:46.380
He’d be such a jolly person.

683
00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Had a big heart.

684
00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.398
<v LEE>Oh my goodness, yes.

685
00:25:49.398 --> 00:25:50.097
Oh, yes.

686
00:25:50.097 --> 00:25:51.145
He really did.

687
00:25:51.145 --> 00:25:58.134
<v ARSENEAULT>And talking about . . . A lot of this are kind of geared toward Mossville, but what is your earliest childhood memory?

688
00:25:58.134 --> 00:26:03.726
<v LEE>Going to a birthday party and there were . . . she’s laughing because she knows about this story.

689
00:26:03.726 --> 00:26:10.366
And it was . . . we were playing pin the tail on the donkey and I wanted to win this prize.

690
00:26:10.366 --> 00:26:18.403
So they tied the scarf around my eyes and they said, “Alright Theresa can you see?” and I said, “No I can’t.” and . . .

691
00:26:18.403 --> 00:26:19.801
<v ARSENEAULT>But you could see?

692
00:26:19.801 --> 00:26:26.441
<v LEE>And I won . . . All I know is I won that little monkey [laughs]. [. . .?] I will say no more [laughs].

693
00:26:26.441 --> 00:26:29.237
And so I was like seven I’m thinking.

694
00:26:29.237 --> 00:26:30.984
There’s a picture of that.

695
00:26:30.984 --> 00:26:32.032
I don’t know.

696
00:26:32.032 --> 00:26:35.177
We had it when I celebrated my seventieth birthday.

697
00:26:35.177 --> 00:26:37.274
We had a picture of that.

698
00:26:37.274 --> 00:26:42.516
And there I am standing at the end of the line and my granddaughter said,

699
00:26:42.516 --> 00:26:53.000
“Maw Maw you had a big head when you were little!” and my brother who passed he stood next to me and my sister so the three of us.

700
00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:54.857
<v ARSENEAULT>So the prize was a monkey.

701
00:26:54.857 --> 00:26:55.786
<v LEE>Yes it was.

702
00:26:55.786 --> 00:27:03.214
That you could wind up and it danced and it played the little . . . and I won that and I will say no more [laughs].

703
00:27:03.214 --> 00:27:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And school . . . Where did you go to elementary?

704
00:27:06.000 --> 00:27:06.462
<v LEE>Okay.

705
00:27:06.462 --> 00:27:09.231
It was called Canal Street Elementary.

706
00:27:09.231 --> 00:27:14.308
And of course again like I said, this was before integration.

707
00:27:14.308 --> 00:27:21.692
And this was maybe about a mile from the home and we actually walked to school.

708
00:27:21.692 --> 00:27:26.769
And then we didn’t have such a thing as middle school.

709
00:27:26.769 --> 00:27:27.692
It was

710
00:27:27.692 --> 00:27:30.000
<c.p>elementary and then high school.

711
00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:36.462
And so in ninth grade we were moving on to the ninth grade school.

712
00:27:36.462 --> 00:27:43.385
This was Francis Marion Boley High school on Martin Luther King Drive [originally Pellerin Street].

713
00:27:43.385 --> 00:27:54.000
And so I was there for ninth grade through twelfth and it was there that I was a cheerleader and an honor student,

714
00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:56.769
treasurer of student council, library club.

715
00:27:56.769 --> 00:28:00.462
And the librarian was not only the librarian.

716
00:28:00.462 --> 00:28:02.769
She was the English teacher.

717
00:28:02.769 --> 00:28:03.231
Excellent.

718
00:28:03.231 --> 00:28:05.077
We had excellent teachers.

719
00:28:05.077 --> 00:28:10.154
When I compare those teachers to when I started teaching . . . amazing.

720
00:28:10.154 --> 00:28:18.000
And most of those teachers went to Grambling, Southern, Leland, Dillard, those are the schools they attended.

721
00:28:18.000 --> 00:28:20.769
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite teacher?

722
00:28:20.769 --> 00:28:21.692
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

723
00:28:21.692 --> 00:28:22.615
Mrs. Rochon.

724
00:28:22.615 --> 00:28:26.308
She was the librarian and the English teacher.

725
00:28:26.308 --> 00:28:27.692
She was precious.

726
00:28:27.692 --> 00:28:29.538
Elitha was her name.

727
00:28:29.538 --> 00:28:30.000
E-L-I-T-H-A.

728
00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.769
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about her?

729
00:28:32.769 --> 00:28:33.692
<v LEE>Her freckles.

730
00:28:33.692 --> 00:28:36.000
I mean that stood out.

731
00:28:36.000 --> 00:28:38.769
But she was a short lady.

732
00:28:38.769 --> 00:28:39.692
Portly build.

733
00:28:39.692 --> 00:28:40.154
Pleasant.

734
00:28:40.154 --> 00:28:41.077
Not mean.

735
00:28:41.077 --> 00:28:42.000
Very assertive.

736
00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:50.769
Business always in the classroom, but then out of the classroom she could really sit and talk with you.

737
00:28:50.769 --> 00:28:54.000
Make you feel important and at home.

738
00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:55.385
She was great.

739
00:28:55.385 --> 00:29:00.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would she have y’all read?

740
00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:00.968
<v LEE>Oh my goodness.

741
00:29:00.968 --> 00:29:01.613
Oh boy.

742
00:29:01.613 --> 00:29:03.548
<v ARSENEAULT>You want me to pause it?

743
00:29:03.548 --> 00:29:03.871
<v LEE>Yes.

744
00:29:03.871 --> 00:29:05.161
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure [break in tape].

745
00:29:05.161 --> 00:29:05.806
<v LEE>High school.

746
00:29:05.806 --> 00:29:06.774
<v ARSENEAULT>High school, okay.

747
00:29:06.774 --> 00:29:08.387
<v LEE>Going back to high school.

748
00:29:08.387 --> 00:29:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Back to high school.

749
00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:14.957
<v LEE>Which we already turned to Ms. Rochon, but in high school I was a cheerleader.

750
00:29:14.957 --> 00:29:16.939
I was . . . I did play basketball.

751
00:29:16.939 --> 00:29:20.904
I was what we called . . . We played what we called half court.

752
00:29:20.904 --> 00:29:22.887
So you had a roaming guard.

753
00:29:22.887 --> 00:29:25.530
That is if . . . I stayed on one side.

754
00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.513
I was not a roaming guard.

755
00:29:27.513 --> 00:29:31.809
One of the other . . . if you shot well you were a roaming guard.

756
00:29:31.809 --> 00:29:36.104
She would move from one side of the court to the other side.

757
00:29:36.104 --> 00:29:39.409
And then so I took care of the defensive side.

758
00:29:39.409 --> 00:29:42.383
So there were five players if I’m thinking correctly.

759
00:29:42.383 --> 00:29:42.583
So what else did I . . .?

760
00:29:42.383 --> 00:29:44.035
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

761
00:29:44.035 --> 00:29:44.696
<v LEE>No, no.

762
00:29:44.696 --> 00:29:48.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So you played basketball and you were a cheerleader?

763
00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:48.405
<v LEE>Yes.

764
00:29:48.405 --> 00:29:50.027
Cheerleader during football season.

765
00:29:50.027 --> 00:29:50.838
<v ARSENEAULT>During football.

766
00:29:50.838 --> 00:29:51.243
<v LEE>Yes.

767
00:29:51.243 --> 00:29:53.270
<v ARSENEAULT>And was this still segregated?

768
00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:53.676
<v LEE>Yes.

769
00:29:53.676 --> 00:29:56.514
<v ARSENEAULT>And were all your teachers black teachers?

770
00:29:56.514 --> 00:29:56.919
<v LEE>Yes.

771
00:29:56.919 --> 00:29:59.351
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember when it started?

772
00:29:59.351 --> 00:30:03.000
Were you in college when they started integrating?

773
00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:03.368
<v LEE>Yes.

774
00:30:03.368 --> 00:30:04.474
<v ARSENEAULT>So we’ll wait.

775
00:30:04.474 --> 00:30:07.053
We’ll get to that in a second.

776
00:30:07.053 --> 00:30:07.421
<v LEE>Okay.

777
00:30:07.421 --> 00:30:07.789
Alright.

778
00:30:07.789 --> 00:30:08.158
Okay.

779
00:30:08.158 --> 00:30:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>But in high school . . .

780
00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:17.683
<v LEE>Now in high school I did go to . . . it was called LIALO which was for . . . just as they have . . . Oh boy.

781
00:30:17.683 --> 00:30:17.883
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it?

782
00:30:17.683 --> 00:30:18.415
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

783
00:30:18.415 --> 00:30:19.878
<v LEE>No, just pause it.

784
00:30:19.878 --> 00:30:20.610
<v ARSENEAULT>Pause it?

785
00:30:20.610 --> 00:30:20.976
<v LEE>Yeah.

786
00:30:20.976 --> 00:30:22.439
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay [break in tape].

787
00:30:22.439 --> 00:30:22.805
<v LEE>Okay.

788
00:30:22.805 --> 00:30:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So you did state choir?

789
00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:26.947
<v LEE>I did state choir, yes.

790
00:30:26.947 --> 00:30:29.282
At Southern University all three years.

791
00:30:29.282 --> 00:30:32.397
All . . . My last three years in high school.

792
00:30:32.397 --> 00:30:33.565
<v ARSENEAULT>What were you?

793
00:30:33.565 --> 00:30:39.794
<v LEE>I was an alto, but if you ask me now, oh it’s a little too high.

794
00:30:39.794 --> 00:30:42.130
I’ve got to go tenor now.

795
00:30:42.130 --> 00:30:43.687
But it was great.

796
00:30:43.687 --> 00:30:45.244
High school was good.

797
00:30:45.244 --> 00:30:49.527
We were a very close knit class, the class of ’64.

798
00:30:49.527 --> 00:30:52.641
We celebrated our fiftieth class reunion in 2014.

799
00:30:52.641 --> 00:30:54.198
Had a great time.

800
00:30:54.198 --> 00:30:59.260
Many of our students could no longer say they were thirty-nine and holding.

801
00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:01.595
Not at a fiftieth class reunion.

802
00:31:01.595 --> 00:31:04.710
And then it was the fall of ’63.

803
00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:12.885
Football season was just about to come to an end and this is as I said when I first saw Richard.

804
00:31:12.885 --> 00:31:19.893
I just remember a tall man . . . tall young boy in the corner, big, but he was on crutches.

805
00:31:19.893 --> 00:31:21.450
I said, “Hmm okay.”

806
00:31:21.450 --> 00:31:30.405
And later on I came to know that they had come to scout our team because they were to play us in regionals.

807
00:31:30.405 --> 00:31:37.412
And my cousin, his name is Roger Williams, he later on was drafted and played for the Rams.

808
00:31:37.412 --> 00:31:39.748
He was the outstanding running back.

809
00:31:39.748 --> 00:31:42.473
So we never said we made touchdowns.

810
00:31:42.473 --> 00:31:44.031
His nickname was “Tick.”

811
00:31:44.031 --> 00:31:45.588
We made “tick downs.”

812
00:31:45.588 --> 00:31:46.366
<v ARSENEAULT>Tick downs?

813
00:31:46.366 --> 00:31:47.924
<v LEE>Tick downs [phone ringing].

814
00:31:47.924 --> 00:31:51.817
I’ve got . . . that’s a 225 number . . . okay, you’ve got it?

815
00:31:51.817 --> 00:31:56.099
And so they had come to scout the team and all.

816
00:31:56.099 --> 00:31:57.656
My brother was center.

817
00:31:57.656 --> 00:32:00.382
He was a junior at that time.

818
00:32:00.382 --> 00:32:03.496
He was the center on the football team.

819
00:32:03.496 --> 00:32:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your first impression of Mr. Richard?

820
00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:09.739
<v LEE>Just this tall person in the corner on crutches.

821
00:32:09.739 --> 00:32:10.652
That was it.

822
00:32:10.652 --> 00:32:11.261
It’s just . . .

823
00:32:11.261 --> 00:32:12.174
<v ARSENEAULT>Just a memory?

824
00:32:12.174 --> 00:32:13.696
<v LEE>Light skinned and just that.

825
00:32:13.696 --> 00:32:15.826
I never spoke to him or anything.

826
00:32:15.826 --> 00:32:16.739
Never got close.

827
00:32:16.739 --> 00:32:20.087
It was just someone I saw in the corner on crutches.

828
00:32:20.087 --> 00:32:22.217
And the gym was just about packed.

829
00:32:22.217 --> 00:32:22.826
We danced.

830
00:32:22.826 --> 00:32:25.261
We had a good time after the game.

831
00:32:25.261 --> 00:32:30.739
And it was not until we were juniors at Grambling State University . . . It was actually Grambling College then,

832
00:32:30.739 --> 00:32:32.870
that I saw him again in class.

833
00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:35.913
And I guess we’ll talk about that a little later.

834
00:32:35.913 --> 00:32:36.217
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure.

835
00:32:36.217 --> 00:32:39.565
<v LEE>So we finished with high school and I graduated that May.

836
00:32:39.565 --> 00:32:40.783
I got a T.H.

837
00:32:40.783 --> 00:32:42.609
Harris scholarship to go to Grambling.

838
00:32:42.609 --> 00:32:45.652
I think there were about five of us who did.

839
00:32:45.652 --> 00:32:45.957
And . . .

840
00:32:45.957 --> 00:32:47.174
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that scholarship?

841
00:32:47.174 --> 00:32:50.826
<v LEE>That’s a scholarship . . . It has to do with your grade point average.

842
00:32:50.826 --> 00:32:54.783
It was predominately for the black students, I mean that’s what we had.

843
00:32:54.783 --> 00:33:01.478
It didn’t pay the whole thing but then again the cost of the tuition was not as great as it is now.

844
00:33:01.478 --> 00:33:03.609
So as long as you kept your

845
00:33:03.609 --> 00:33:06.043
<c.p>average up you could hold onto that scholarship.

846
00:33:06.043 --> 00:33:07.565
And so there I was.

847
00:33:07.565 --> 00:33:13.652
So we . . . most of us rested during the summer and most of us began college in the fall of ’64.

848
00:33:13.652 --> 00:33:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What were the entertainment options for young people in Jeanerette?

849
00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:19.909
<v LEE>Well next street next to the high school was a teenage center.

850
00:33:19.909 --> 00:33:21.364
It was hopping all the time.

851
00:33:21.364 --> 00:33:24.273
And so if you left school if you didn’t ride the bus,

852
00:33:24.273 --> 00:33:29.121
if you walked you normally . . . you stopped there or you went there until the bus came to pick you up.

853
00:33:29.121 --> 00:33:32.273
That was the thing to go to teenage dances and things like that.

854
00:33:32.273 --> 00:33:33.485
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it have a name?

855
00:33:33.485 --> 00:33:34.697
<v LEE>I cannot . . . I can’t remember.

856
00:33:34.697 --> 00:33:41.000
I can’t remember the name [Lee adds it was Berry’s Teenage Center, and across the street from the school was Clavelle Teen Center and grocery store].

857
00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.697
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would y’all do?

858
00:33:42.697 --> 00:33:43.667
<v LEE>The Popeye where you . . .

859
00:33:43.667 --> 00:33:44.636
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that a dance?

860
00:33:44.636 --> 00:33:47.545
<v LEE>It was a dance when just like Popeye just like you . . . A . . .

861
00:33:47.545 --> 00:33:47.788
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh!

862
00:33:47.788 --> 00:33:48.273
<v LEE>Oh, yeah.

863
00:33:48.273 --> 00:33:52.152
And we did the watusi I can’t remember some of . . . [Lee states others were “the slop”,

864
00:33:52.152 --> 00:33:55.545
“the walk” (geared toward Fats Domino’s song “Walking to New Orleans”) and “The Twist”]

865
00:33:55.545 --> 00:33:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>All the different dance moves . . .

866
00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:03.581
<v LEE>All of the [. . .?]. we had . . . Matter of fact there was a talent show and my sister and I,

867
00:34:03.581 --> 00:34:06.140
Pat, and I were three years apart.

868
00:34:06.140 --> 00:34:13.086
We . . . Evidently I was in ninth grade and I guess she was a junior or senior in high school.

869
00:34:13.086 --> 00:34:21.860
And we were in this talent show and so the science [social] studies teacher said, “Are you sure your daddy don’t mind you dancing?”

870
00:34:21.860 --> 00:34:22.591
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, right.

871
00:34:22.591 --> 00:34:25.882
<v LEE>And we said, “No it’s going to be okay.”

872
00:34:25.882 --> 00:34:27.710
And so we did it.

873
00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:32.097
I don’t know if Dad ever found out about it or not.

874
00:34:32.097 --> 00:34:33.194
I don’t know.

875
00:34:33.194 --> 00:34:34.290
I don’t know.

876
00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:34.656
So.

877
00:34:34.656 --> 00:34:36.484
Yeah, so I . . . that’s it.

878
00:34:36.484 --> 00:34:42.699
The graduating class, it was a great class and we are still together after all those years.

879
00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.258
We still communicate after all those years.

880
00:34:45.258 --> 00:34:48.548
One of the only classes who still does that.

881
00:34:48.548 --> 00:34:59.882
And we were the class that the school was changed from just Jeanerette Colored High School to Frances Marion Boley High School and we were the ones that bought the sign.

882
00:34:59.882 --> 00:35:07.925
Now since the school has closed, the sign is gone and now as I said it is now Mount Calvary Baptist Church.

883
00:35:07.925 --> 00:35:10.118
The church I grew up in.

884
00:35:10.118 --> 00:35:10.484
The

885
00:35:10.484 --> 00:35:13.774
<c.p>church moved there because it’s just a larger facility.

886
00:35:13.774 --> 00:35:19.258
And the football field has allowed them all the parking space and all of that.

887
00:35:19.258 --> 00:35:19.624
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

888
00:35:19.624 --> 00:35:24.742
<v LEE>So it was a great move I think for them to have done that.

889
00:35:24.742 --> 00:35:32.419
Otherwise we don’t know what would have happened to that building as is happening to Mossville School just being wiped away.

890
00:35:32.419 --> 00:35:41.925
<v ARSENEAULT>So based on your parent’s education, and your education, and seeing your daughter get educated, do you have any thought s on how education has changed?

891
00:35:41.925 --> 00:35:43.387
Or how they’re different?

892
00:35:43.387 --> 00:35:45.215
Or how they’re the same?

893
00:35:45.215 --> 00:35:52.892
<v LEE>The teachers that taught me were more business-like and they worked hard to make sure you understood and they were disciplinarians.

894
00:35:52.892 --> 00:36:02.398
You had no one in the classroom who would disrupt a classroom and children were not being taught because there was just one who disrupted you.

895
00:36:02.398 --> 00:36:04.591
You . . . We just didn’t have that.

896
00:36:04.591 --> 00:36:05.688
If not anything.

897
00:36:05.688 --> 00:36:13.000
And then as years have come and gone we see that it’s getting a little bit more permissive, so . . .

898
00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:15.215
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your parents go to school?

899
00:36:15.215 --> 00:36:18.169
<v LEE>My . . . neither of the two graduated high school.

900
00:36:18.169 --> 00:36:22.231
They both . . . Dad finished I think seventh grade, my mom eighth.

901
00:36:22.231 --> 00:36:28.508
And my uncle, his younger brother of the three, he’s the only one that graduated high school.

902
00:36:28.508 --> 00:36:31.462
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it mostly just because you worked after?

903
00:36:31.462 --> 00:36:32.200
<v LEE>Worked after.

904
00:36:32.200 --> 00:36:33.308
That is correct.

905
00:36:33.308 --> 00:36:34.046
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

906
00:36:34.046 --> 00:36:34.415
<v LEE>[Agrees].

907
00:36:34.415 --> 00:36:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I know a lot of people . . .

908
00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:43.439
<v LEE>And so they were certainly wanting their children to get as much education as they possibly could.

909
00:36:43.439 --> 00:36:44.955
That was number one.

910
00:36:44.955 --> 00:36:47.227
That was their number one goal.

911
00:36:47.227 --> 00:36:49.121
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they encourage that?

912
00:36:49.121 --> 00:36:51.773
<v LEE>You [they] encouraged by saying, “You’re going.”

913
00:36:51.773 --> 00:36:54.045
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was not an option . . .

914
00:36:54.045 --> 00:36:58.591
<v LEE>And I’ve got to say this about my brother who’s passed on.

915
00:36:58.591 --> 00:37:00.106
<v ARSENEAULT>Is this Mr. Patrick?

916
00:37:00.106 --> 00:37:01.242
<v LEE>This is Paul.

917
00:37:01.242 --> 00:37:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Paul.

918
00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:02.386
<v LEE>Paul.

919
00:37:02.386 --> 00:37:03.545
Paul Earl Whitehead.

920
00:37:03.545 --> 00:37:07.795
We were at Grambling together and it was during the sixties.

921
00:37:07.795 --> 00:37:10.500
A lot of protests were going on.

922
00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:13.205
And so they were protesting about everything.

923
00:37:13.205 --> 00:37:16.295
The food was not right, the classes, whatever.

924
00:37:16.295 --> 00:37:20.545
They were putting more emphasis on the athletics rather than academics.

925
00:37:20.545 --> 00:37:24.023
So my brother was a part of that movement.

926
00:37:24.023 --> 00:37:27.500
And they were running us out of our classes.

927
00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:35.614
And so they filmed it all and so many of those were kicked out especially if they were out there protesting.

928
00:37:35.614 --> 00:37:37.159
Couldn’t pass their classes.

929
00:37:37.159 --> 00:37:46.818
So he arrived home and my dad said, “Well you don’t want an education so you’re going to work out here in the sugarcane field.”

930
00:37:46.818 --> 00:37:54.932
My brother said he was looking for the first bus back to Grambling and from there on, he learned his lesson.

931
00:37:54.932 --> 00:37:56.091
That was it.

932
00:37:56.091 --> 00:37:57.250
They didn’t play.

933
00:37:57.250 --> 00:37:58.795
That was just said . . .

934
00:37:58.795 --> 00:38:00.727
<v ARSENEAULT>He went to school at . . .

935
00:38:00.727 --> 00:38:04.205
<v LEE>He went to school, finished school, graduated and all.

936
00:38:04.205 --> 00:38:04.591
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

937
00:38:04.591 --> 00:38:10.000
So Grambling, tell me about your first impressions freshman year coming from Jeanerette . . .

938
00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:10.331
<v LEE>Yes.

939
00:38:10.331 --> 00:38:12.317
Coming from a little place, Jeanerette.

940
00:38:12.317 --> 00:38:16.620
We . . . most persons . . . Most people did not have cars so we had buses,

941
00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:19.599
school buses, the yellow . . . buses that picked us up.

942
00:38:19.599 --> 00:38:24.894
They picked us up Jeanerette, the New Iberia area, and we would usually meet in Alexandria.

943
00:38:24.894 --> 00:38:29.859
The buses coming from the east area like in Baton Rouge area we’d meet there.

944
00:38:29.859 --> 00:38:33.169
Some were going to Southern, some were going to Grambling.

945
00:38:33.169 --> 00:38:39.458
And that’s how we . . . It would take an entire day to get from Jeanerette to Grambling in a sense.

946
00:38:39.458 --> 00:38:40.120
<v ARSENEAULT>Why Grambling?

947
00:38:40.120 --> 00:38:42.437
<v LEE>Well my old . . . as I told you,

948
00:38:42.437 --> 00:38:47.732
the . . . one of my . . . The cousin who graduated college in ’55, she was the first one.

949
00:38:47.732 --> 00:38:52.035
And that was the place we wanted to go because she had gone.

950
00:38:52.035 --> 00:38:52.697
She had . . .

951
00:38:52.697 --> 00:38:54.352
<v ARSENEAULT>So you lived on campus?

952
00:38:54.352 --> 00:38:55.676
<v LEE>We lived on campus.

953
00:38:55.676 --> 00:38:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Tell me about . . .

954
00:38:57.000 --> 00:39:01.398
<v LEE>No one lived . . . I mean we lost weight in college.

955
00:39:01.398 --> 00:39:05.795
Others gained weight when they all went off to college.

956
00:39:05.795 --> 00:39:07.554
We had meal tickets.

957
00:39:07.554 --> 00:39:11.512
I mean you did that or you didn’t eat.

958
00:39:11.512 --> 00:39:12.831
That was it.

959
00:39:12.831 --> 00:39:16.789
So my first . . . My impression of Grambling was, “My!

960
00:39:16.789 --> 00:39:17.669
The hills!

961
00:39:17.669 --> 00:39:18.108
Beautiful.

962
00:39:18.108 --> 00:39:19.428
The rolling hills.

963
00:39:19.428 --> 00:39:20.307
Beautiful country.”

964
00:39:20.307 --> 00:39:22.946
And the earth so healthy there.

965
00:39:22.946 --> 00:39:24.265
I loved it.

966
00:39:24.265 --> 00:39:29.542
The lines were long but we were used to things like that.

967
00:39:29.542 --> 00:39:31.301
What you compare to.

968
00:39:31.301 --> 00:39:33.500
The dormitories all had matrons.

969
00:39:33.500 --> 00:39:42.735
And we had one in Dormitory 3, Freshman 3, she was called “Moby Dick” because she was a tall bright lady.

970
00:39:42.735 --> 00:39:43.614
Very portly.

971
00:39:43.614 --> 00:39:46.253
No one fooled with Moby Dick.

972
00:39:46.253 --> 00:39:50.651
And so they were the ones who monitored the halls.

973
00:39:50.651 --> 00:39:54.169
And I think my room number was 313.

974
00:39:54.169 --> 00:39:59.886
Gwen [Gwendolyn Smith] and I, we graduated high school together, we were roomies.

975
00:39:59.886 --> 00:40:06.482
Normally you tried to room with someone from your hometown and that’s what we did.

976
00:40:06.482 --> 00:40:10.000
I . . . So all of that went well.

977
00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:12.344
I met Richard in my junior year.

978
00:40:12.344 --> 00:40:14.687
We were in a physical science class.

979
00:40:14.687 --> 00:40:15.692
Very large class.

980
00:40:15.692 --> 00:40:24.062
When the class and the . . . Mr. Williams asked some questions so no one was answering and no one was holding up his or her hand.

981
00:40:24.062 --> 00:40:28.414
So I heard a voice in the back that said, “Ms. Whitehead knows.”

982
00:40:28.414 --> 00:40:30.423
And I’m saying, “Oh my God!

983
00:40:30.423 --> 00:40:33.771
Who is that?” and so I can’t remember what happened.

984
00:40:33.771 --> 00:40:35.445
I was just . . . went blank.

985
00:40:35.445 --> 00:40:39.128
But anyway on my way . . . so from that class Beverly [Gibson],

986
00:40:39.128 --> 00:40:45.824
another home girl and I were walking to the cafeteria for lunch because it was the class right before lunch.

987
00:40:45.824 --> 00:40:53.189
And there was someone behind us and he said, “Hey up there,” he said, “Do you want to go to the movies?”

988
00:40:53.189 --> 00:40:54.529
So Beverly said, “Who?

989
00:40:54.529 --> 00:40:54.863
What?”

990
00:40:54.863 --> 00:40:57.542
So he said, “I’m not talking to you.”

991
00:40:57.542 --> 00:41:00.220
And I said, “Well who is this fellow?”

992
00:41:00.220 --> 00:41:01.559
So he followed us.

993
00:41:01.559 --> 00:41:02.229
We met.

994
00:41:02.229 --> 00:41:02.899
We talked.

995
00:41:02.899 --> 00:41:11.269
And in talking, that’s when I found out that he was the man, the young man that I saw standing in the corner in ’63.

996
00:41:11.269 --> 00:41:16.960
And so it must have been ’67 or sixty . . . ’66 or ’67 when I saw him again.

997
00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:26.000
And I came to find out that he was the young man that I saw in the corner all those years so our path crossed again.

998
00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:28.500
<v ARSENEAULT>So how long did you date?

999
00:41:28.500 --> 00:41:32.250
<v LEE>We met in ’67 as I said as juniors.

1000
00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.167
I graduated Grambling in May of ’68.

1001
00:41:35.167 --> 00:41:40.583
He played ball the fall of ’68 so he graduated December of ’68.

1002
00:41:40.583 --> 00:41:48.917
In the meantime, I had gone on to Wisconsin, but in the summer before leaving for Wisconsin we became engaged.

1003
00:41:48.917 --> 00:41:49.750
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1004
00:41:49.750 --> 00:41:50.167
<v LEE>Okay?

1005
00:41:50.167 --> 00:41:50.583
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1006
00:41:50.583 --> 00:41:55.167
<v LEE>So I worked in Wisconsin and he was still at Grambling.

1007
00:41:55.167 --> 00:42:00.583
And I remember flying to New York to see them play in the . . .

1008
00:42:00.583 --> 00:42:03.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it an arena in New York?

1009
00:42:03.500 --> 00:42:04.333
Or the . . .

1010
00:42:04.333 --> 00:42:06.000
<v HOLMAN>Bronx Sugar bowl.

1011
00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:06.833
<v LEE>No it was not.

1012
00:42:06.833 --> 00:42:08.083
It was the . . . What is that?

1013
00:42:08.083 --> 00:42:09.125
<v HOLMAN>One that was on ESPN?

1014
00:42:09.125 --> 00:42:10.167
<v LEE>Whitney Young . . . yeah. [Lee adds:

1015
00:42:10.167 --> 00:42:14.542
the Whitney Young Football Classic of 1971 became the first black college game to be aired on national television, CBS Sports]

1016
00:42:14.542 --> 00:42:15.167
<v ARSENEAULT>I don’t know.

1017
00:42:15.167 --> 00:42:16.208
<v HOLMAN>There’s a documentary on it.

1018
00:42:16.208 --> 00:42:18.708
First and Ten in the Bronx [1st and Goal in the Bronx].

1019
00:42:18.708 --> 00:42:18.917
<v LEE>Yeah.

1020
00:42:18.917 --> 00:42:20.583
<v ARSENEAULT>First and ten . . . is it a special field?

1021
00:42:20.583 --> 00:42:21.000
Or . . .?

1022
00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:22.358
<v LEE>It was the field that the New York Giants played in. [Lee notes:

1023
00:42:22.358 --> 00:42:24.030
aside from playing in Yankee Stadium, they also played in the Rose Bowl stadium in Pasadena,

1024
00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:26.119
the Pecan Bowl in Abilene and the Tiger Paw Classic in the Astrodome and were also the Orange Bowl Champs]

1025
00:42:26.119 --> 00:42:26.328
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1026
00:42:26.328 --> 00:42:26.528
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1027
00:42:26.433 --> 00:42:27.164
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was playing there with the . . .?

1028
00:42:27.164 --> 00:42:27.687
<v LEE>With the Grambling State University.

1029
00:42:27.687 --> 00:42:28.000
ARSENEAULT: Okay.

1030
00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:31.338
<v LEE>And they flew there because they were playing in a bowl there.

1031
00:42:31.338 --> 00:42:33.841
And I flew from Wisconsin to meet him there.

1032
00:42:33.841 --> 00:42:34.397
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1033
00:42:34.397 --> 00:42:39.404
<v LEE>And one of our friends [Henry “Hatchet” Davis] who had already begun playing for the New York Giants,

1034
00:42:39.404 --> 00:42:46.636
he made sure that I got to the game in time, I got back to the airport so I could take my flight back to Wisconsin.

1035
00:42:46.636 --> 00:42:49.417
And Rich of course they were flying back to Grambling.

1036
00:42:49.417 --> 00:42:53.311
And so I was able to go to a professional game when I was . . .

1037
00:42:53.311 --> 00:42:53.589
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1038
00:42:53.589 --> 00:42:54.702
<v LEE>And I felt important.

1039
00:42:54.702 --> 00:42:55.815
The New York Giants!

1040
00:42:55.815 --> 00:42:56.927
I felt very important.

1041
00:42:56.927 --> 00:42:57.205
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

1042
00:42:57.205 --> 00:42:59.430
<v LEE>I even passed by . . . What is his name?

1043
00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:00.265
He passed recently.

1044
00:43:00.265 --> 00:43:02.212
His wife has this show on television.

1045
00:43:02.212 --> 00:43:06.662
She and . . . what is his name? [Frank Gifford, a Hall of Famer] He passed just recently . . .

1046
00:43:06.662 --> 00:43:07.497
<v HOLMAN>I don’t know.

1047
00:43:07.497 --> 00:43:10.000
ARSENEAULT: Do you need me to pause it?

1048
00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:11.068
<v LEE>Yeah pause it.

1049
00:43:11.068 --> 00:43:11.424
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1050
00:43:11.424 --> 00:43:14.627
<v LEE>Come back and we’ll do that [break in tape].

1051
00:43:14.627 --> 00:43:18.186
So anyway . . . But anyway, that was my first professional game.

1052
00:43:18.186 --> 00:43:19.254
That was great.

1053
00:43:19.254 --> 00:43:19.610
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1054
00:43:19.610 --> 00:43:20.322
So Grambling.

1055
00:43:20.322 --> 00:43:23.169
Was Coach Eddie Robinson there at the time?

1056
00:43:23.169 --> 00:43:26.373
<v LEE>Oh, he’s . . . He was there since the fifties so . . .

1057
00:43:26.373 --> 00:43:26.729
<v HOLMAN>’45.

1058
00:43:26.729 --> 00:43:27.797
<v LEE>Forty . . . Forty what?

1059
00:43:27.797 --> 00:43:28.153
<v HOLMAN>’45.

1060
00:43:28.153 --> 00:43:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What can you tell me about him?

1061
00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:35.030
<v LEE>He was a philosopher and he was a disciplinarian.

1062
00:43:35.030 --> 00:43:39.955
He knew how to get his players to give their best.

1063
00:43:39.955 --> 00:43:43.537
And when you said Eddie Robinson, like wow.

1064
00:43:43.537 --> 00:43:47.567
He worked hard to get the Bayou Classic started.

1065
00:43:47.567 --> 00:43:50.254
I think it was in ’74.

1066
00:43:50.254 --> 00:43:50.701
<v HOLMAN>’74.

1067
00:43:50.701 --> 00:43:51.149
<v LEE>’74.

1068
00:43:51.149 --> 00:44:00.104
He lived . . . He’s from Baton Rouge so he lived close to LSU so he would always hear all the noise.

1069
00:44:00.104 --> 00:44:04.134
He said, “I want Grambling to be like this.”

1070
00:44:04.134 --> 00:44:14.881
To be able to bring in eighty thousand or more people, and so it started I think at Tulane university was the first classic.

1071
00:44:14.881 --> 00:44:22.940
And then it moved to the Superdome where it still is there except for the hurricane I think.

1072
00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:23.388
<v HOLMAN>Katrina.

1073
00:44:23.388 --> 00:44:24.284
<v LEE>Katrina, yeah.

1074
00:44:24.284 --> 00:44:27.418
It went to Houston for that one.

1075
00:44:27.418 --> 00:44:27.866
But . . .

1076
00:44:27.866 --> 00:44:31.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And that was Mr. Richard’s coach?

1077
00:44:31.000 --> 00:44:31.800
<v LEE>Oh, yes!

1078
00:44:31.800 --> 00:44:34.200
And he thought highly of Richard.

1079
00:44:34.200 --> 00:44:43.000
According to all that we’ve checked, that Richard has been the only . . . was the only three time captain during Coach Rob’s legacy.

1080
00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:43.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1081
00:44:43.400 --> 00:44:44.200
<v LEE>That’s right.

1082
00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:45.400
The only one.

1083
00:44:45.400 --> 00:44:53.400
And so that says a lot about Richard as being responsible, leading by example and how others thought of him.

1084
00:44:53.400 --> 00:45:02.200
They even said Richard and the other captain [Ernest Sterling, drafted by the Cowboys] was like they walked on water with Rob.

1085
00:45:02.200 --> 00:45:04.200
They could do no wrong.

1086
00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:11.400
But they were well respected and it says that Rich always had that big smile on his face.

1087
00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:17.000
He would block [you] into the ground and help you get up still smiling.

1088
00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:19.000
He was just that kind.

1089
00:45:19.000 --> 00:45:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his position?

1090
00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:21.320
<v LEE>Tackle.

1091
00:45:21.320 --> 00:45:23.563
When he was at . . . He was offensive.

1092
00:45:23.563 --> 00:45:27.408
And then he was drafted by the Patriots and it became defensive.

1093
00:45:27.408 --> 00:45:30.291
But he was offensive so he blocked really well.

1094
00:45:30.291 --> 00:45:37.340
And the first black quarterback, who actually was drafted as a quarterback and actually played as a quarterback, James Harris from Grambling.

1095
00:45:37.340 --> 00:45:42.466
He played with Richard and Richard is the one who blocked for him, who protected him.

1096
00:45:42.466 --> 00:45:42.786
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1097
00:45:42.786 --> 00:45:47.272
<v LEE>We have a picture in the game room with the two of them together.

1098
00:45:47.272 --> 00:45:50.476
You’ll see that later if we ever get out there.

1099
00:45:50.476 --> 00:45:52.398
<v ARSENEAULT>So why did he pick Grambling?

1100
00:45:52.398 --> 00:45:53.359
<v LEE>Why did Richard?

1101
00:45:53.359 --> 00:45:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

1102
00:45:54.000 --> 00:45:57.677
<v LEE>He was recruited by several schools from high school because of his size.

1103
00:45:57.677 --> 00:46:00.505
But he broke his leg . . . this is what is ironic.

1104
00:46:00.505 --> 00:46:05.596
He broke his leg at a high school game at Paul Breaux High school in Lafayette in ’63.

1105
00:46:05.596 --> 00:46:10.121
Okay I think the cap below his knee cap. [Lee notes: injury to the medial condyle,

1106
00:46:10.121 --> 00:46:15.495
the bone beneath the knee cap, on Saturday, September 7, 1963 during the final play of the third quarter]

1107
00:46:15.495 --> 00:46:16.061
<v HOLMAN>Patella, yeah.

1108
00:46:16.061 --> 00:46:17.192
<v LEE>That’s what it was?

1109
00:46:17.192 --> 00:46:19.737
And my daughter worked at that school years later.

1110
00:46:19.737 --> 00:46:22.283
The same school where her dad had gotten hurt.

1111
00:46:22.283 --> 00:46:23.697
And so they backed off.

1112
00:46:23.697 --> 00:46:25.111
And it was Johnny Comeaux.

1113
00:46:25.111 --> 00:46:27.939
I don’t know if anyone has talked about Johnny Comeaux.

1114
00:46:27.939 --> 00:46:30.768
Johnny Comeaux was at Grambling at the time from Westlake.

1115
00:46:30.768 --> 00:46:32.747
And he and Richard were very close.

1116
00:46:32.747 --> 00:46:35.293
And he told Richard, “Do not go to Southern.

1117
00:46:35.293 --> 00:46:36.990
I will get you at Grambling.

1118
00:46:36.990 --> 00:46:38.404
I will talk to Rob.

1119
00:46:38.404 --> 00:46:39.535
You will get there.

1120
00:46:39.535 --> 00:46:40.384
Do not sign.”

1121
00:46:40.384 --> 00:46:44.909
And so Johnny Comeaux was instrumental in getting Richard to Grambling rather than going to Southern.

1122
00:46:44.909 --> 00:46:45.758
<v ARSENEAULT>Why did he . . .?

1123
00:46:45.758 --> 00:46:48.020
Did he just want to be with him?

1124
00:46:48.020 --> 00:46:50.848
<v LEE>Yes and he said, “This is the place to be.

1125
00:46:50.848 --> 00:46:53.111
You want to be here with Coach Rob.”

1126
00:46:53.111 --> 00:46:53.394
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1127
00:46:53.394 --> 00:46:54.525
<v LEE>And so it happened.

1128
00:46:54.525 --> 00:46:57.636
Rich said he arrived there in the middle of the night.

1129
00:46:57.636 --> 00:46:59.333
The taxi driver dropped him off.

1130
00:46:59.333 --> 00:47:05.556
He said, “Where am I going?” so he walked over to this building here and it was a . . . The door was open.

1131
00:47:05.556 --> 00:47:06.687
It was a dormitory.

1132
00:47:06.687 --> 00:47:08.384
The . . . one of the men’s dormitories.

1133
00:47:08.384 --> 00:47:14.323
And he walked in, no one was on the bottom floor so he said, “Well I’ve got to go to sleep.”

1134
00:47:14.323 --> 00:47:14.523
So he propped a chair against the door and he fell asleep.

1135
00:47:14.323 --> 00:47:18.000
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

1136
00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:18.300
<v LEE>Yeah.

1137
00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:21.894
And he fell asleep on the couch there until the next morning.

1138
00:47:21.894 --> 00:47:27.586
I mean kids talk about rough today, and I mean it all comes from being how you were reared.

1139
00:47:27.586 --> 00:47:32.079
Being strong, high self-esteem, and nothing’s going to stop me from getting to my goal.

1140
00:47:32.079 --> 00:47:32.678
Being focused.

1141
00:47:32.678 --> 00:47:35.974
And I guess Coach Rob saw all of that in him.

1142
00:47:35.974 --> 00:47:40.167
And he said . . . I guess he said, “This is going to be my captain.”

1143
00:47:40.167 --> 00:47:44.960
So as a sophomore, junior, and senior, he was one of the captains of the team.

1144
00:47:44.960 --> 00:47:46.159
<v ARSENEAULT>What were his responsibilities?

1145
00:47:46.159 --> 00:47:53.348
<v LEE>Making sure that the players were where they were supposed to have been and do and all that kind of stuff, like an overseer.

1146
00:47:53.348 --> 00:47:56.044
I’m sure there were things he let slip by.

1147
00:47:56.044 --> 00:48:09.225
He talked to them and stuff like that and they just . . . but it . . . and there is . . . and this why we know it was . . . how much they loved Richard because the Richard Lee . . . The G-Men Gathering that began in 2008 is still going on now.

1148
00:48:09.225 --> 00:48:14.916
And they changed it at Richard’s death to the Richard Lee G-Men Gathering [Richard H. Lee, Jr. G-Men Gathering].

1149
00:48:14.916 --> 00:48:17.313
And they talked about him as being their

1150
00:48:17.313 --> 00:48:17.612
<c.p>captain.

1151
00:48:17.612 --> 00:48:23.604
I mean these are men in their sixties and seventies who remember him so well and had so much respect.

1152
00:48:23.604 --> 00:48:25.101
<v ARSENEAULT>What is the G-Man Gathering?

1153
00:48:25.101 --> 00:48:26.000
<v LEE>Well G-Men . . .

1154
00:48:26.000 --> 00:48:26.371
<v ARSENEAULT>G-Men?

1155
00:48:26.371 --> 00:48:27.857
<v LEE>G stands for Grambling.

1156
00:48:27.857 --> 00:48:32.686
And so you remember with the government, G-Men, so we just, “Hey G-Men.

1157
00:48:32.686 --> 00:48:33.429
Grambling Men.

1158
00:48:33.429 --> 00:48:34.171
Government Men.”

1159
00:48:34.171 --> 00:48:36.029
So that’s how it started.

1160
00:48:36.029 --> 00:48:40.114
<v HOLMAN>It’s male athletes at Grambling State University are all considered “G-Men.”

1161
00:48:40.114 --> 00:48:40.486
<v LEE>G-Men.

1162
00:48:40.486 --> 00:48:42.714
<v ARSENEAULT>So it’s like a special club?

1163
00:48:42.714 --> 00:48:43.829
An elite club?

1164
00:48:43.829 --> 00:48:44.200
<v LEE>[Agrees.]

1165
00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:47.171
<v ARSENEAULT>So did he ever talk about Coach Eddie?

1166
00:48:47.171 --> 00:48:49.771
What kinds of things would he say?

1167
00:48:49.771 --> 00:48:50.886
<v LEE>All the time.

1168
00:48:50.886 --> 00:48:52.000
All the time.

1169
00:48:52.000 --> 00:48:52.743
<v ARSENEAULT>Coach Rob?

1170
00:48:52.743 --> 00:48:55.343
<v LEE>He said was not one to curse.

1171
00:48:55.343 --> 00:48:56.829
Coach Rob didn’t curse,

1172
00:48:56.829 --> 00:49:05.000
but he always had a way to get you to do what you had to do . . . would get into your head.

1173
00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:06.385
<v ARSENEAULT>How would he do that?

1174
00:49:06.385 --> 00:49:08.877
<v LEE>I guess of how he would talk to them.

1175
00:49:08.877 --> 00:49:10.262
Talk to them as men.

1176
00:49:10.262 --> 00:49:14.969
And he always said, “They came to me as boys, but they had to leave as men.”

1177
00:49:14.969 --> 00:49:18.569
And he taught them . . . He would take them out to eat . . . Taught them . . .

1178
00:49:18.569 --> 00:49:18.846
<v HOLMAN>Etiquette.

1179
00:49:18.846 --> 00:49:19.123
<v LEE>Right.

1180
00:49:19.123 --> 00:49:27.431
What to eat with and all of that so when they were being recruited by these professional personnel they knew when they took them out to eat what to use,

1181
00:49:27.431 --> 00:49:30.200
and that you leave a tip, and all of this.

1182
00:49:30.200 --> 00:49:31.308
He taught them everything.

1183
00:49:31.308 --> 00:49:33.246
The full gamut of being a man.

1184
00:49:33.246 --> 00:49:37.677
Not just . . . And a thing also about Coach Rob, he was so interested in their education.

1185
00:49:37.677 --> 00:49:43.492
I mean you had to get . . . he had . . . they talked about how he would go down the hallway with his bell.

1186
00:49:43.492 --> 00:49:43.692
Shaking his bell.

1187
00:49:43.492 --> 00:49:44.323
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

1188
00:49:44.323 --> 00:49:44.600
<v LEE>Yeah.

1189
00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:48.200
Making sure that those athletes were up and on their way to class.

1190
00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:49.862
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was in the dorms?

1191
00:49:49.862 --> 00:49:50.415
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

1192
00:49:50.415 --> 00:49:51.523
He walked the dorms.

1193
00:49:51.523 --> 00:49:54.846
<v HOLMAN>You had to go to breakfast . . . They had to go to breakfast . . .

1194
00:49:54.846 --> 00:49:57.338
<v LEE>See, she went with an athlete so she knows.

1195
00:49:57.338 --> 00:49:59.000
<v HOLMAN>This was in the nineties.

1196
00:49:59.000 --> 00:49:59.402
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1197
00:49:59.402 --> 00:50:09.862
<v LEE>He rang the bell around six o clock, picked up ID’s, and in order to eat for the rest of the day you needed your ID.

1198
00:50:09.862 --> 00:50:12.678
So they had to go to breakfast . . .

1199
00:50:12.678 --> 00:50:13.885
<v ARSENEAULT>Get their ID . . .

1200
00:50:13.885 --> 00:50:15.897
<v LEE>After breakfast was study hall . . .

1201
00:50:15.897 --> 00:50:16.299
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1202
00:50:16.299 --> 00:50:17.506
He was invested.

1203
00:50:17.506 --> 00:50:18.713
<v HOLMAN>And then practice.

1204
00:50:18.713 --> 00:50:21.126
And this was in the nineties.

1205
00:50:21.126 --> 00:50:26.356
<v LEE>And that was in the nineties, and Rich was there in the sixties.

1206
00:50:26.356 --> 00:50:30.379
So he had not mellowed very much, had he? [Laughs].

1207
00:50:30.379 --> 00:50:34.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mr. Richard always want to go pro?

1208
00:50:34.000 --> 00:50:40.824
<v LEE>I think most of all he wanted to go to college and get an education and that was like lagniappe.

1209
00:50:40.824 --> 00:50:42.188
If it happens, good.

1210
00:50:42.188 --> 00:50:46.965
And then as the others were going . . . I mean when you . . . listen to this.

1211
00:50:46.965 --> 00:50:53.447
When you stood on Grambling’s campus, at the end of the year you could just see the cars coming,

1212
00:50:53.447 --> 00:50:59.929
those persons who had been drafted, because the . . . the pros were coming to the black schools for their athletes.

1213
00:50:59.929 --> 00:51:01.976
And I mean Grambling was just . . .

1214
00:51:01.976 --> 00:51:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Everybody got . . .

1215
00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:04.012
<v LEE>[Skewing?] them out.

1216
00:51:04.012 --> 00:51:04.688
Oh, yes.

1217
00:51:04.688 --> 00:51:06.375
They were coming with cars.

1218
00:51:06.375 --> 00:51:09.075
You would say, “He’s going to the Rams.

1219
00:51:09.075 --> 00:51:10.425
He’s here, he’s there.”

1220
00:51:10.425 --> 00:51:13.462
So it was a part of that . . . that element.

1221
00:51:13.462 --> 00:51:13.800
Yeah.

1222
00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:16.838
He wanted to be a part of that too.

1223
00:51:16.838 --> 00:51:17.175
Yeah.

1224
00:51:17.175 --> 00:51:17.512
Absolutely.

1225
00:51:17.512 --> 00:51:19.200
What else can we say?

1226
00:51:19.200 --> 00:51:20.550
<v ARSENEAULT>What was his major?

1227
00:51:20.550 --> 00:51:22.238
<v LEE>His major was industrial arts.

1228
00:51:22.238 --> 00:51:23.250
<v ARSENEAULT>What is that?

1229
00:51:23.250 --> 00:51:24.262
<v LEE>Carpentry and all.

1230
00:51:24.262 --> 00:51:24.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1231
00:51:24.600 --> 00:51:25.612
<v HOLMAN>Like to teach . . .

1232
00:51:25.612 --> 00:51:25.950
<v LEE>Yeah.

1233
00:51:25.950 --> 00:51:30.000
<v HOLMAN>But now that kind of . . . I think that subject is obsolete.

1234
00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:32.404
<v LEE>Yeah I don’t know what . . . I don’t know.

1235
00:51:32.404 --> 00:51:36.311
<v HOLMAN>I don’t even know if they still teach that in public schools anymore.

1236
00:51:36.311 --> 00:51:36.612
<v LEE>Yeah.

1237
00:51:36.612 --> 00:51:39.016
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it because he was raised doing that?

1238
00:51:39.016 --> 00:51:40.820
That he was comfortable with it?

1239
00:51:40.820 --> 00:51:41.120
Or . . .?

1240
00:51:41.120 --> 00:51:46.831
<v LEE>Well there were several persons in his life his . . . two of his grandmother’s brothers were carpenters and they built.

1241
00:51:46.831 --> 00:51:47.432
Ida Lemelle . . .

1242
00:51:47.432 --> 00:51:47.732
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1243
00:51:47.732 --> 00:51:48.333
<v LEE>They were.

1244
00:51:48.333 --> 00:51:56.448
He also worked with his . . . one of his uncles at a doctor’s ranch and they would build . . . Put the fences back up and all kind of things.

1245
00:51:56.448 --> 00:51:59.153
So probably from all of that he probably did.

1246
00:51:59.153 --> 00:52:05.464
He always liked to . . . he was good in . . . he was very good in math and measuring and in . . . Matter of fact,

1247
00:52:05.464 --> 00:52:10.874
if someone needed to build something then they would always come to Richard even in his later years,

1248
00:52:10.874 --> 00:52:13.579
“How many of these do you think I need?

1249
00:52:13.579 --> 00:52:15.082
How many two by fours?”

1250
00:52:15.082 --> 00:52:16.885
And Richard could figure that out.

1251
00:52:16.885 --> 00:52:21.995
When they would go to the lumber company it was very close to what he had said.

1252
00:52:21.995 --> 00:52:23.197
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was naturally . . .

1253
00:52:23.197 --> 00:52:25.000
<v LEE>He was just a good . . .

1254
00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:25.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Spatially . . .

1255
00:52:25.270 --> 00:52:27.158
<v LEE>Just a good . . . Oh, very much so.

1256
00:52:27.158 --> 00:52:27.967
Very much so.

1257
00:52:27.967 --> 00:52:34.710
He would be in a room doing something and I’d be doing something and he would say, “Now this is what it’s going to be.”

1258
00:52:34.710 --> 00:52:38.755
And I said, “But okay there . . .” He said, “I don’t know how you got it,

1259
00:52:38.755 --> 00:52:42.801
but you do have the right answer though, but I don’t know what you did.”

1260
00:52:42.801 --> 00:52:44.689
But he was very good at that.

1261
00:52:44.689 --> 00:52:52.780
That’s why I hate that his passing . . . He passed before he could really really enjoy his grandchildren and teach them the math that he knew [. . .?] because he just loved it.

1262
00:52:52.780 --> 00:52:53.589
He loved it.

1263
00:52:53.589 --> 00:52:56.556
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he talk about Mossville when y’all were together in Grambling?

1264
00:52:56.556 --> 00:52:57.635
<v LEE>Oh, my God yes.

1265
00:52:57.635 --> 00:52:59.793
He would always say, “We beat you guys!”

1266
00:52:59.793 --> 00:53:01.950
Because they did beat us in high school.

1267
00:53:01.950 --> 00:53:05.726
When the team came back after he had . . . After they had scouted our team,

1268
00:53:05.726 --> 00:53:09.502
they did beat us that year, but it was like about forty-two to thirty-five.

1269
00:53:09.502 --> 00:53:10.581
<v ARSENEAULT>So it was close.

1270
00:53:10.581 --> 00:53:11.390
<v LEE>It was close.

1271
00:53:11.390 --> 00:53:12.199
It was close.

1272
00:53:12.199 --> 00:53:16.245
And he and the young man I talked about, Rogers Williams, they were very close.

1273
00:53:16.245 --> 00:53:18.133
They played of course at Grambling together.

1274
00:53:18.133 --> 00:53:20.021
And so he often talked about Mossville.

1275
00:53:20.021 --> 00:53:21.909
We have his class ring down there.

1276
00:53:21.909 --> 00:53:22.718
I found that.

1277
00:53:22.718 --> 00:53:24.876
And he lost it, or it was pawned.

1278
00:53:24.876 --> 00:53:27.573
We’re not going to say how, but someone got it.

1279
00:53:27.573 --> 00:53:28.921
Pawned it and someone said . . .

1280
00:53:28.921 --> 00:53:30.000
<v HOLMAN>Who saw it . . .

1281
00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:31.455
<v LEE>And someone saw it in the . . .

1282
00:53:31.455 --> 00:53:31.939
<v HOLMAN>The size.

1283
00:53:31.939 --> 00:53:33.636
<v LEE>Yeah, saw it in the pawn shop.

1284
00:53:33.636 --> 00:53:35.818
<v HOLMAN>And they . . . someone saw it at the pawn shop . . .

1285
00:53:35.818 --> 00:53:36.545
<v LEE>Say this . . . yeah!

1286
00:53:36.545 --> 00:53:37.273
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . And got lucky.

1287
00:53:37.273 --> 00:53:37.515
<v LEE>Yeah!

1288
00:53:37.515 --> 00:53:39.697
Said, “Richard there’s a . . . And that’s a big ring!”

1289
00:53:39.697 --> 00:53:41.636
So he went and that was his ring.

1290
00:53:41.636 --> 00:53:42.848
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure enough that was his . . .

1291
00:53:42.848 --> 00:53:43.333
<v LEE>Yes, yeah.

1292
00:53:43.333 --> 00:53:44.303
That was his ring!

1293
00:53:44.303 --> 00:53:44.545
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1294
00:53:44.545 --> 00:53:45.515
Can I see it?

1295
00:53:45.515 --> 00:53:46.000
<v LEE>Yes.

1296
00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:48.092
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, that is a big ring isn’t it?

1297
00:53:48.092 --> 00:53:48.354
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1298
00:53:48.354 --> 00:53:51.231
Now, when we got married he was wearing a size thirteen.

1299
00:53:51.231 --> 00:53:53.062
I think that might be a thirteen.

1300
00:53:53.062 --> 00:53:53.846
I don’t know.

1301
00:53:53.846 --> 00:53:55.677
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, it’s got a pirate on it.

1302
00:53:55.677 --> 00:53:56.723
<v LEE>Okay, there’s the pirate.

1303
00:53:56.723 --> 00:53:57.769
Has Mossville . . . everything, yeah.

1304
00:53:57.769 --> 00:53:58.554
<v ARSENEAULT>The pirates. ’64.

1305
00:53:58.554 --> 00:53:59.862
<v HOLMAN>Most thing was the size.

1306
00:53:59.862 --> 00:54:01.169
Nobody had fingers that size.

1307
00:54:01.169 --> 00:54:01.431
<v LEE>Yeah.

1308
00:54:01.431 --> 00:54:01.692
Yeah.

1309
00:54:01.692 --> 00:54:02.477
<v ARSENEAULT>Mossville High School.

1310
00:54:02.477 --> 00:54:03.000
<v LEE>Yes.

1311
00:54:03.000 --> 00:54:05.835
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever talk about Mossville High School?

1312
00:54:05.835 --> 00:54:06.544
The teachers?

1313
00:54:06.544 --> 00:54:06.899
Coaches?

1314
00:54:06.899 --> 00:54:07.608
<v LEE>Oh, yeah.

1315
00:54:07.608 --> 00:54:09.025
There is . . . Mrs. Rogers.

1316
00:54:09.025 --> 00:54:11.506
Well Mrs. Rogers taught English at Mossville.

1317
00:54:11.506 --> 00:54:19.304
And Mrs. Rogers taught my husband way back in the sixties, and I think her last job was at Barbe High School.

1318
00:54:19.304 --> 00:54:21.076
Now that’s a long time.

1319
00:54:21.076 --> 00:54:22.848
You know Barbe High School?

1320
00:54:22.848 --> 00:54:23.911
Where it is?

1321
00:54:23.911 --> 00:54:26.392
That’s a long time to be teaching.

1322
00:54:26.392 --> 00:54:29.582
And it was like a couple of years ago.

1323
00:54:29.582 --> 00:54:31.000
Maybe she retired.

1324
00:54:31.000 --> 00:54:32.000
<v HOLMAN>She’s still living.

1325
00:54:32.000 --> 00:54:33.333
<v LEE>And she’s still living.

1326
00:54:33.333 --> 00:54:34.000
She’s a . . .

1327
00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve interviewed her for the project.

1328
00:54:36.000 --> 00:54:36.333
<v LEE>Yeah.

1329
00:54:36.333 --> 00:54:38.000
She’s a spunky little lady.

1330
00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:38.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1331
00:54:38.333 --> 00:54:39.333
<v LEE>Oh my goodness.

1332
00:54:39.333 --> 00:54:41.000
Yeah that was his teacher.

1333
00:54:41.000 --> 00:54:44.000
He often talked about his industrial arts teacher [Mr.

1334
00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:44.333
Jones].

1335
00:54:44.333 --> 00:54:47.333
I can’t think of his name at this time.

1336
00:54:47.333 --> 00:54:50.000
He often talked about a Rigmaiden . . . James Rigmaiden.

1337
00:54:50.000 --> 00:54:51.667
He was the math teacher.

1338
00:54:51.667 --> 00:54:54.000
He’d say, “That was a smart man.”

1339
00:54:54.000 --> 00:54:56.667
A lot of disciplinarians again at that school.

1340
00:54:56.667 --> 00:54:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1341
00:54:57.000 --> 00:54:58.000
<v HOLMAN>Coach Williams.

1342
00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:00.048
<v LEE>And Coach Williams that was . . . LaSalle Williams.

1343
00:55:00.048 --> 00:55:02.095
That was his high school coach, yes.

1344
00:55:02.095 --> 00:55:04.435
<v ARSENEAULT>I bet he was proud of Mr. Richard.

1345
00:55:04.435 --> 00:55:05.020
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

1346
00:55:05.020 --> 00:55:05.313
Absolutely.

1347
00:55:05.313 --> 00:55:09.408
<v HOLMAN>Especially winning the state championship in the shotput on crutches with a broken leg.

1348
00:55:09.408 --> 00:55:09.701
<v LEE>Yes.

1349
00:55:09.701 --> 00:55:15.551
He won it once in single A, and then as a school [group?] he won it twice in double A.

1350
00:55:15.551 --> 00:55:17.891
<v ARSENEAULT>And he won it with a broken leg?

1351
00:55:17.891 --> 00:55:19.061
<v LEE>[Agrees] his last year.

1352
00:55:19.061 --> 00:55:19.354
Shotput.

1353
00:55:19.354 --> 00:55:26.374
And so when his daughter began to throw the shotput in high school, he was the one who taught her how to do it.

1354
00:55:26.374 --> 00:55:32.224
I put some pictures to the side I said I didn’t know if you wanted to know that or not . . .

1355
00:55:32.224 --> 00:55:32.517
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah

1356
00:55:32.517 --> 00:55:37.197
<v LEE>. . . But I put them to the side . . . of her throwing the shotput at Sulphur High School.

1357
00:55:37.197 --> 00:55:39.830
<v ARSENEAULT>You got an advantage that your dad’s your coach.

1358
00:55:39.830 --> 00:55:41.000
<v HOLMAN>Yes I did.

1359
00:55:41.000 --> 00:55:41.410
<v LEE>Yes.

1360
00:55:41.410 --> 00:55:41.821
Yeah.

1361
00:55:41.821 --> 00:55:45.103
He loved him some Dawn I tell ya.

1362
00:55:45.103 --> 00:55:51.667
So anything else about Grambling I’m trying to think of . . . as I said I did leave . . .

1363
00:55:51.667 --> 00:55:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So during integration, what you do you remember like in the sixties?

1364
00:55:57.000 --> 00:55:57.345
<v LEE>Okay.

1365
00:55:57.345 --> 00:56:00.448
At . . . well, actually it was like two separate worlds.

1366
00:56:00.448 --> 00:56:08.724
So we were . . . it was . . . I think in my mind it was so good for us because we could see ourselves in leading positions.

1367
00:56:08.724 --> 00:56:11.138
Roles of leading and governing and things.

1368
00:56:11.138 --> 00:56:16.655
But then some students who went to the other schools, they were pushed back and profiled.

1369
00:56:16.655 --> 00:56:20.793
And I could . . . Matter of fact, I have to tell you this.

1370
00:56:20.793 --> 00:56:24.931
As I said I got my bachelors at Grambling State University, okay?

1371
00:56:24.931 --> 00:56:31.828
And I worked at LaGrange one year and so for high school I went for my masters . . . Went to McNeese.

1372
00:56:31.828 --> 00:56:34.931
And I tell you it was a rude awakening.

1373
00:56:34.931 --> 00:56:38.724
When I went to McNeese they talked so down to them.

1374
00:56:38.724 --> 00:56:40.793
And I’m saying, “Oh my God!

1375
00:56:40.793 --> 00:56:41.828
How could they . . .”

1376
00:56:41.828 --> 00:56:43.897
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean to the black students?

1377
00:56:43.897 --> 00:56:44.241
<v LEE>Yes!

1378
00:56:44.241 --> 00:56:47.345
And to just some of the students in general.

1379
00:56:47.345 --> 00:56:52.517
I said, “How could they sit in this class and do this?” and guess what?

1380
00:56:52.517 --> 00:56:56.655
I dropped that class, took another and it was not any better.

1381
00:56:56.655 --> 00:57:01.138
So I say, “Theresa, get your master’s degree and get out of here.”

1382
00:57:01.138 --> 00:57:09.414
And I spoke to other teachers who did not go to a predominately black school so they could not relate to what I felt.

1383
00:57:09.414 --> 00:57:13.552
And so it didn’t faze them, but I could see the difference.

1384
00:57:13.552 --> 00:57:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Because they had been exposed to it for longer?

1385
00:57:17.000 --> 00:57:17.294
<v LEE>Yeah!

1386
00:57:17.294 --> 00:57:17.588
Yeah!

1387
00:57:17.588 --> 00:57:19.059
And it’s just not right.

1388
00:57:19.059 --> 00:57:19.941
It wasn’t right.

1389
00:57:19.941 --> 00:57:21.118
It’s still not right.

1390
00:57:21.118 --> 00:57:22.294
It’s still not alright.

1391
00:57:22.294 --> 00:57:23.176
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember . . .?

1392
00:57:23.176 --> 00:57:24.647
What else do you remember?

1393
00:57:24.647 --> 00:57:27.000
You mentioned the protests a little bit . . .

1394
00:57:27.000 --> 00:57:27.543
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

1395
00:57:27.543 --> 00:57:31.618
We were . . . I think I was a senior in college and so they were saying,

1396
00:57:31.618 --> 00:57:34.607
“Don’t go to sleep tonight we’re going to burn it down.

1397
00:57:34.607 --> 00:57:36.237
We’re going to burn it down.”

1398
00:57:36.237 --> 00:57:40.584
So this girlfriend of mine, she was . . . Matter of fact she took care of my daughter.

1399
00:57:40.584 --> 00:57:42.757
My daughter got her first job at Lafayette.

1400
00:57:42.757 --> 00:57:43.572
We were roommates.

1401
00:57:43.572 --> 00:57:46.289
We were seniors and we were on the third floor.

1402
00:57:46.289 --> 00:57:49.549
And girl I say, “Joan,” her name is Joan Smith, I said,

1403
00:57:49.549 --> 00:57:54.983
“Joan don’t go to sleep they’re going to burn it down tonight!” she said, “Oh, girl I’m going to sleep.”

1404
00:57:54.983 --> 00:57:56.613
I say, “I am not going.”

1405
00:57:56.613 --> 00:57:56.884
Girl!

1406
00:57:56.884 --> 00:57:57.699
I smelled smoke!

1407
00:57:57.699 --> 00:57:59.058
I say, “Joan wake up!”

1408
00:57:59.058 --> 00:58:00.688
Joan beat me down the stairs.

1409
00:58:00.688 --> 00:58:02.318
She got up before I did.

1410
00:58:02.318 --> 00:58:05.850
And they had started burning the lobby of the dorm where we lived!

1411
00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:07.751
<v ARSENEAULT>So they were literally burning it down?

1412
00:58:07.751 --> 00:58:08.023
<v LEE>Yes!

1413
00:58:08.023 --> 00:58:08.566
They were!

1414
00:58:08.566 --> 00:58:13.185
<v ARSENEAULT>It was not like a saying that meant they were just going to stay up all night.

1415
00:58:13.185 --> 00:58:14.000
<v LEE>No, no!

1416
00:58:14.000 --> 00:58:15.667
<v ARSENEAULT>They were going to burn the dorms down.

1417
00:58:15.667 --> 00:58:17.542
<v LEE>They were, but I mean they didn’t get far.

1418
00:58:17.542 --> 00:58:17.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Why?

1419
00:58:17.750 --> 00:58:17.958
<v LEE>Just . . .

1420
00:58:17.958 --> 00:58:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>To make a statement?

1421
00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:19.308
<v LEE>Whatever.

1422
00:58:19.308 --> 00:58:21.462
To make statement . . . Whatever the reasonings were.

1423
00:58:21.462 --> 00:58:24.231
And see my brother was a part of that.

1424
00:58:24.231 --> 00:58:27.615
And then the national guards came out and I called home.

1425
00:58:27.615 --> 00:58:31.000
I said, “Dad,” and I was like crying on the phone.

1426
00:58:31.000 --> 00:58:32.538
And hey, so but anyway.

1427
00:58:32.538 --> 00:58:38.385
He did go back to Grambling and he did finish and things worked out and we moved on so . . .

1428
00:58:38.385 --> 00:58:42.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it have to do with integration that they were burning it?

1429
00:58:42.077 --> 00:58:45.154
<v LEE>It had to do with that stage where things were . . .

1430
00:58:45.154 --> 00:58:46.077
<v ARSENEAULT>In the sixties . . .

1431
00:58:46.077 --> 00:58:51.308
<v LEE>Yeah the sixties were . . . you know if . . . They just weren’t accepting you or being rebellious about everything.

1432
00:58:51.308 --> 00:58:55.000
But I was just the one that went along with the norm.

1433
00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:55.923
I was focused.

1434
00:58:55.923 --> 00:58:59.000
I wanted to get out and get a job [laughs].

1435
00:58:59.000 --> 00:58:59.923
You know, so.

1436
00:58:59.923 --> 00:59:00.538
But anyway.

1437
00:59:00.538 --> 00:59:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about Doctor King?

1438
00:59:03.000 --> 00:59:06.446
<v LEE>I was doing my student teaching in April of ’68.

1439
00:59:06.446 --> 00:59:11.270
I was in Monroe, and I was doing my student teaching in library science.

1440
00:59:11.270 --> 00:59:18.850
So I came home that day and every time we would come home during that time, I have to also say this,

1441
00:59:18.850 --> 00:59:26.086
we would listen to the radio and it would always update us with those persons who had been killed in Vietnam.

1442
00:59:26.086 --> 00:59:32.633
And I remember them saying . . . and I know I’m getting a little off but I need to say this.

1443
00:59:32.633 --> 00:59:35.734
Sylvester Wright from Jeanerette was killed in friendly fire.

1444
00:59:35.734 --> 00:59:39.869
I said that’s my homeboy who’s the same age with my brother.

1445
00:59:39.869 --> 00:59:44.004
My brother had gone to Germany and he had gone to Vietnam.

1446
00:59:44.004 --> 00:59:47.105
That was like . . . that was not a good thing.

1447
00:59:47.105 --> 00:59:55.030
So the next couple of days I had come home to . . . and then there it was that Martin Luther King had been killed.

1448
00:59:55.030 --> 00:59:56.753
And it was like, “Wow.”

1449
00:59:56.753 --> 00:59:57.787
Couldn’t believe it.

1450
00:59:57.787 --> 00:59:58.820
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were . . .?

1451
00:59:58.820 --> 01:00:05.367
<v LEE>And so the thing about . . . Let me also say this about those of us who came through that time.

1452
01:00:05.367 --> 01:00:05.712
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure.

1453
01:00:05.712 --> 01:00:10.880
<v LEE>As a senior in high school November of 1963, we know our president was assassinated.

1454
01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:17.772
I was upstairs in the main building and it was like everything had just . . . Life had come to an end.

1455
01:00:17.772 --> 01:00:20.528
How would you kill a president, you know?

1456
01:00:20.528 --> 01:00:21.562
That was horrible.

1457
01:00:21.562 --> 01:00:22.940
And that was in

1458
01:00:22.940 --> 01:00:23.285
<c.p>‘63.

1459
01:00:23.285 --> 01:00:23.974
November ’63.

1460
01:00:23.974 --> 01:00:27.419
And then in April and then of course later on.

1461
01:00:27.419 --> 01:00:30.176
So it was not . . . it was not good.

1462
01:00:30.176 --> 01:00:32.588
<v ARSENEAULT>So your brother went to Vietnam too?

1463
01:00:32.588 --> 01:00:35.000
Or no, he went to Germany.

1464
01:00:35.000 --> 01:00:37.398
<v LEE>No, my brother went to Germany instead.

1465
01:00:37.398 --> 01:00:43.565
But one of my homeboys, Sylvester Wright, he went to Vietnam and he was killed by friendly fire.

1466
01:00:43.565 --> 01:00:44.935
<v ARSENEAULT>It was an accident?

1467
01:00:44.935 --> 01:00:45.620
Someone accidently . . .

1468
01:00:45.620 --> 01:00:45.963
<v LEE>Whatever . . .

1469
01:00:45.963 --> 01:00:46.991
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . Whatever the cause.

1470
01:00:46.991 --> 01:00:50.074
<v LEE>You know how things like that you never know.

1471
01:00:50.074 --> 01:00:53.157
You just know he was killed by an American.

1472
01:00:53.157 --> 01:00:57.269
<v ARSENEAULT>So Vietnam . . . Did anyone else in the family serve or get drafted?

1473
01:00:57.269 --> 01:00:58.981
<v LEE>No, not on my side.

1474
01:00:58.981 --> 01:01:02.407
My . . . now my father’s middle brother was in the army.

1475
01:01:02.407 --> 01:01:09.259
He . . . and that was so many years ago that was way back in the forties and the fifties and all.

1476
01:01:09.259 --> 01:01:11.315
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was World War II?

1477
01:01:11.315 --> 01:01:12.000
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1478
01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:13.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he talk about it?

1479
01:01:13.667 --> 01:01:15.333
<v LEE>No, no he never did.

1480
01:01:15.333 --> 01:01:16.333
He never did.

1481
01:01:16.333 --> 01:01:17.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he serve overseas?

1482
01:01:17.667 --> 01:01:18.667
<v LEE>I’m not sure.

1483
01:01:18.667 --> 01:01:20.333
I’m not sure he did.

1484
01:01:20.333 --> 01:01:27.667
All I know he settled in Chicago . . . Became a cook and then he moved to New Orleans and that’s where he passed.

1485
01:01:27.667 --> 01:01:28.667
In New Orleans.

1486
01:01:28.667 --> 01:01:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1487
01:01:29.000 --> 01:01:30.333
<v LEE>I’m trying to think . . .

1488
01:01:30.333 --> 01:01:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And Paul, how long was he in Germany?

1489
01:01:33.000 --> 01:01:34.000
<v LEE>He was . . . Two.

1490
01:01:34.000 --> 01:01:42.333
I think it was two years he was there because I can’t . . . He had graduated high school and I guess it must have been ’71,

1491
01:01:42.333 --> 01:01:44.667
’72, somewhere in that time I’m thinking.

1492
01:01:44.667 --> 01:01:46.000
I’m trying to think.

1493
01:01:46.000 --> 01:01:48.667
Cut it off please I’m trying to think.

1494
01:01:48.667 --> 01:01:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sure.

1495
01:01:49.000 --> 01:01:50.333
Sure [break in tape].

1496
01:01:50.333 --> 01:01:57.667
<v LEE>. . . Grambling and as I said, many of these northern states were trying to integrate their schools so they knew where to go.

1497
01:01:57.667 --> 01:01:59.000
Go to the . . .

1498
01:01:59.000 --> 01:01:59.664
<v ARSENEAULT>The black . . .

1499
01:01:59.664 --> 01:02:00.992
<v LEE>The black colleges, yeah.

1500
01:02:00.992 --> 01:02:03.980
So several had come down to Grambling and Southern.

1501
01:02:03.980 --> 01:02:06.967
Some of the other southern states and Texas Southern.

1502
01:02:06.967 --> 01:02:12.611
I had offers to Lacassine right here, and then one in Racine, and a couple other places.

1503
01:02:12.611 --> 01:02:16.926
So when I chose Racine my dad said, he would call me “black”.

1504
01:02:16.926 --> 01:02:19.914
He said, “Black, who do you know up there?”

1505
01:02:19.914 --> 01:02:20.578
“Nobody Daddy.”

1506
01:02:20.578 --> 01:02:25.889
“Well why are you . . .?” and today I don’t really know why but I just did it.

1507
01:02:25.889 --> 01:02:39.500
And so they sent me the letter and all and the man had come down . . . [phone ringing] get that . . . To recruit and so I signed the papers and they had the rooming wherever I wanted to stay and all of that.

1508
01:02:39.500 --> 01:02:40.828
So I said okay.

1509
01:02:40.828 --> 01:02:49.459
So my dad and my sister drove me to New Orleans and I caught a . . . the train there in New Orleans and went on to Chicago.

1510
01:02:49.459 --> 01:02:54.770
Then from there was the . . . what they call limousine service where they took you to Racine.

1511
01:02:54.770 --> 01:03:00.082
So they took me to the district building and the person was considered as my sponsor.

1512
01:03:00.082 --> 01:03:03.070
So he was my sponsor his name was Knudsen.

1513
01:03:03.070 --> 01:03:04.066
He was German.

1514
01:03:04.066 --> 01:03:08.713
And so in finding me a place . . . I lived with them about two weeks.

1515
01:03:08.713 --> 01:03:12.365
And his daughter and I were like about five years apart.

1516
01:03:12.365 --> 01:03:14.689
I was twenty-three, she was like eighteen.

1517
01:03:14.689 --> 01:03:17.012
She was a senior in high school.

1518
01:03:17.012 --> 01:03:18.008
Name is Susan.

1519
01:03:18.008 --> 01:03:18.672
Susan Knudsen.

1520
01:03:18.672 --> 01:03:20.000
Very pleasant people.

1521
01:03:20.000 --> 01:03:23.649
And going to church and all I found this family.

1522
01:03:23.649 --> 01:03:28.757
Last name . . . Matter of fact, I found a letter from her the other day.

1523
01:03:28.757 --> 01:03:29.486
Ann Riley.

1524
01:03:29.486 --> 01:03:31.676
I’m sure she’s passed by now.

1525
01:03:31.676 --> 01:03:33.500
Her husband . . . not her husband.

1526
01:03:33.500 --> 01:03:37.149
Her son was an educator and so was her daughter-in-law.

1527
01:03:37.149 --> 01:03:39.338
And he was president of NAACP.

1528
01:03:39.338 --> 01:03:44.446
So I . . . she was renting out rooms so I rented a room from her.

1529
01:03:44.446 --> 01:03:45.905
We lived on West

1530
01:03:45.905 --> 01:03:47.365
<c.p>Street in Racine, Wisconsin.

1531
01:03:47.365 --> 01:03:48.824
And it was nice.

1532
01:03:48.824 --> 01:03:50.284
It was . . . an experience.

1533
01:03:50.284 --> 01:03:51.378
It really was.

1534
01:03:51.378 --> 01:03:53.932
And she just showed me the ropes.

1535
01:03:53.932 --> 01:03:56.851
And around the corner I met another family.

1536
01:03:56.851 --> 01:03:59.405
They were Baptist because she was Methodist.

1537
01:03:59.405 --> 01:04:00.865
So they were Baptist.

1538
01:04:00.865 --> 01:04:03.054
They were the . . . Oh, my goodness.

1539
01:04:03.054 --> 01:04:03.784
Dorothy . . . Eanes.

1540
01:04:03.784 --> 01:04:04.149
E-A-N-E-S.

1541
01:04:04.149 --> 01:04:07.797
They were from Mississippi, and her husband was from Georgia.

1542
01:04:07.797 --> 01:04:09.622
They had moved to Wisconsin.

1543
01:04:09.622 --> 01:04:11.811
So I became friends with them.

1544
01:04:11.811 --> 01:04:18.743
We went to church together, we shopped together, we did all of that together and it was very interesting.

1545
01:04:18.743 --> 01:04:19.473
Eye opening.

1546
01:04:19.473 --> 01:04:23.122
And I joined the NAACP there and we went places.

1547
01:04:23.122 --> 01:04:24.216
It was interesting.

1548
01:04:24.216 --> 01:04:27.865
I loved the . . . You know what I liked about Racine?

1549
01:04:27.865 --> 01:04:32.608
To see during the fall of the year to see the different colors.

1550
01:04:32.608 --> 01:04:33.703
The brilliant colors.

1551
01:04:33.703 --> 01:04:39.176
You drive up to Milwaukee, you drive up to Madison, you keep going . . . Green Bay.

1552
01:04:39.176 --> 01:04:41.000
What it’s like . . . Wow!

1553
01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:41.695
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s beautiful.

1554
01:04:41.695 --> 01:04:42.042
<v LEE>Yes!

1555
01:04:42.042 --> 01:04:45.864
And so I tried my hardest to get my future husband.

1556
01:04:45.864 --> 01:04:47.602
And they said, “Oh, yes!

1557
01:04:47.602 --> 01:04:48.644
We’ll recruit him.”

1558
01:04:48.644 --> 01:04:51.424
They sent him letters and all of that.

1559
01:04:51.424 --> 01:04:53.161
“We want him up here.

1560
01:04:53.161 --> 01:04:53.856
Industrial arts?

1561
01:04:53.856 --> 01:04:54.551
Oh, yes!”

1562
01:04:54.551 --> 01:04:55.593
He said, “Okay.”

1563
01:04:55.593 --> 01:05:02.542
And when I got back home after I married him I found those letters . . . Nothing was ever written on them.

1564
01:05:02.542 --> 01:05:04.627
They were as I sent them.

1565
01:05:04.627 --> 01:05:08.449
I guess he was just not . . . But anyway, it was good.

1566
01:05:08.449 --> 01:05:10.186
It was a good experience.

1567
01:05:10.186 --> 01:05:14.356
And the family I met, the Eanes, they visited us in Louisiana.

1568
01:05:14.356 --> 01:05:16.441
And they have now passed on.

1569
01:05:16.441 --> 01:05:18.873
The daughter [Marcie Eanes] became a poet.

1570
01:05:18.873 --> 01:05:20.263
She lives in California.

1571
01:05:20.263 --> 01:05:20.958
Very interesting.

1572
01:05:20.958 --> 01:05:21.305
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1573
01:05:21.305 --> 01:05:22.000
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1574
01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:23.182
<v ARSENEAULT>What a life, huh?

1575
01:05:23.182 --> 01:05:24.068
<v LEE>It was good.

1576
01:05:24.068 --> 01:05:24.955
It was good.

1577
01:05:24.955 --> 01:05:27.909
So I guess Dad said, “Well Black is home again.

1578
01:05:27.909 --> 01:05:28.205
Okay.

1579
01:05:28.205 --> 01:05:29.091
You did it.”

1580
01:05:29.091 --> 01:05:36.182
I guess having a lot of children you couldn’t . . . you had to kind of give them a little way to do their own thing.

1581
01:05:36.182 --> 01:05:41.500
Back off a little bit and let them . . . so when my daughter . . . when my daughter graduated I said,

1582
01:05:41.500 --> 01:05:48.886
“Wherever she wants to go. “ I can’t say a word because here was this little country girl from Jeanerette going all the way to Racine.

1583
01:05:48.886 --> 01:05:51.250
A place I’d never gone before, but anyway . . .

1584
01:05:51.250 --> 01:05:52.727
<v ARSENEAULT>So you finished college there?

1585
01:05:52.727 --> 01:05:54.205
<v LEE>No, I finished at Grambling.

1586
01:05:54.205 --> 01:05:54.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1587
01:05:54.500 --> 01:05:55.091
<v LEE>And then . . .

1588
01:05:55.091 --> 01:05:56.864
<v ARSENEAULT>And what was . . . you were education?

1589
01:05:56.864 --> 01:05:58.341
<v LEE>Yes, English and library science.

1590
01:05:58.341 --> 01:05:59.523
<v ARSENEAULT>English and library science.

1591
01:05:59.523 --> 01:05:59.818
<v LEE>[Agrees].

1592
01:05:59.818 --> 01:06:01.000
Yes, but there . . .

1593
01:06:01.000 --> 01:06:03.750
<v ARSENEAULT>So you were going to be a teacher?

1594
01:06:03.750 --> 01:06:04.094
<v LEE>Yes.

1595
01:06:04.094 --> 01:06:06.500
But there I worked in the library.

1596
01:06:06.500 --> 01:06:08.562
I set up a library there.

1597
01:06:08.562 --> 01:06:09.250
<v ARSENEAULT>In Racine?

1598
01:06:09.250 --> 01:06:09.938
<v LEE>In Racine.

1599
01:06:09.938 --> 01:06:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So that was your first job?

1600
01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:13.719
<v LEE>That was my first job.

1601
01:06:13.719 --> 01:06:14.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1602
01:06:14.062 --> 01:06:14.406
<v LEE>Yes.

1603
01:06:14.406 --> 01:06:16.125
That was my first job.

1604
01:06:16.125 --> 01:06:18.188
<v ARSENEAULT>And you set up a library.

1605
01:06:18.188 --> 01:06:18.531
<v LEE>Yes.

1606
01:06:18.531 --> 01:06:18.875
<v ARSENEAULT>Where?

1607
01:06:18.875 --> 01:06:20.938
<v LEE>In . . . At the school in Racine.

1608
01:06:20.938 --> 01:06:23.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So at the high school?

1609
01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:25.691
<v LEE>At the . . . No it was an elementary school.

1610
01:06:25.691 --> 01:06:26.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1611
01:06:26.027 --> 01:06:27.709
Do you remember the name?

1612
01:06:27.709 --> 01:06:29.727
<v LEE>Lincoln Elementary I think it is.

1613
01:06:29.727 --> 01:06:30.400
Lincoln Elementary.

1614
01:06:30.400 --> 01:06:32.418
<v ARSENEAULT>What was . . . What were your responsibilities?

1615
01:06:32.418 --> 01:06:34.436
What is setting up a library?

1616
01:06:34.436 --> 01:06:36.118
I can’t even imagine that.

1617
01:06:36.118 --> 01:06:38.473
<v LEE>Well you had books . . . You had nothing.

1618
01:06:38.473 --> 01:06:40.155
It was on a stage.

1619
01:06:40.155 --> 01:06:42.173
They used the stage as that.

1620
01:06:42.173 --> 01:06:46.545
So the curtains . . . We closed the curtains and so that became the library.

1621
01:06:46.545 --> 01:06:48.564
And I remember this so well.

1622
01:06:48.564 --> 01:06:50.582
Two girls passed by my door.

1623
01:06:50.582 --> 01:06:53.609
One was name Dawn and the other one Joy.

1624
01:06:53.609 --> 01:06:55.291
And they said, “She’s colored!”

1625
01:06:55.291 --> 01:06:58.991
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you the first colored person to work in the school?

1626
01:06:58.991 --> 01:06:59.327
Wow!

1627
01:06:59.327 --> 01:07:00.000
<v LEE>Yeah.

1628
01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:02.136
<v ARSENEAULT>Were there colored people in the community?

1629
01:07:02.136 --> 01:07:04.576
Or were there black people in the community?

1630
01:07:04.576 --> 01:07:08.542
<v LEE>Yes, this is like I said when I met the Germans, the Knudsens.

1631
01:07:08.542 --> 01:07:14.034
Then I met . . . At church I met these other families and then it just went on from there.

1632
01:07:14.034 --> 01:07:14.339
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1633
01:07:14.339 --> 01:07:14.644
<v LEE>Yes.

1634
01:07:14.644 --> 01:07:15.864
It was an experience.

1635
01:07:15.864 --> 01:07:20.441
It was like I guess the prayers were there because I was . . . I wasn’t afraid.

1636
01:07:20.441 --> 01:07:24.407
When you’re young . . . I think when you’re young you don’t fear too much.

1637
01:07:24.407 --> 01:07:26.542
Now it’s like, how in the world?

1638
01:07:26.542 --> 01:07:28.068
Why did I do that?

1639
01:07:28.068 --> 01:07:30.814
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you experience any hostility that you can remember?

1640
01:07:30.814 --> 01:07:32.339
<v LEE>Oh, I do remember once.

1641
01:07:32.339 --> 01:07:37.831
Ms. Ann Riley had gone to another state for something and I was at the house by myself.

1642
01:07:37.831 --> 01:07:39.966
And the house was on a hill.

1643
01:07:39.966 --> 01:07:43.932
And I heard someone outside and I said, “My God, who are they?”

1644
01:07:43.932 --> 01:07:45.763
So there were two young teenagers.

1645
01:07:45.763 --> 01:07:49.424
They were just . . . And someone said, “I think we scared her enough.”

1646
01:07:49.424 --> 01:07:50.339
And they left.

1647
01:07:50.339 --> 01:07:51.559
I said, “My goodness!”

1648
01:07:51.559 --> 01:07:52.780
<v ARSENEAULT>Just trying to be . . .

1649
01:07:52.780 --> 01:07:53.390
<v LEE>Yeah, whatever.

1650
01:07:53.390 --> 01:07:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Bullies.

1651
01:07:54.000 --> 01:07:54.974
<v LEE>Yeah I guess.

1652
01:07:54.974 --> 01:07:55.623
I guess.

1653
01:07:55.623 --> 01:07:55.947
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1654
01:07:55.947 --> 01:07:57.894
<v LEE>Yeah that was the only thing.

1655
01:07:57.894 --> 01:07:59.192
Yeah so it was . . .

1656
01:07:59.192 --> 01:08:02.113
<v ARSENEAULT>So you had to order books for the school?

1657
01:08:02.113 --> 01:08:02.437
<v LEE>Right.

1658
01:08:02.437 --> 01:08:06.007
