01:22:01.000 --> 01:22:02.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was he wounded?

2090
01:22:02.077 --> 01:22:02.615
<v LEE>Can you . . .?

2091
01:22:02.615 --> 01:22:03.962
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it talk about it?

2092
01:22:03.962 --> 01:22:04.231
Or . . .

2093
01:22:04.231 --> 01:22:05.846
<v LEE>Kenny probably has all of that.

2094
01:22:05.846 --> 01:22:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were the women in the picture?

2095
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:08.200
<v LEE>Hey . . .

2096
01:22:08.000 --> 01:22:08.432
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?]

2097
01:22:08.432 --> 01:22:11.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he always surrounded by . . .? [Laughs]

2098
01:22:11.027 --> 01:22:11.459
<v HOLMAN>Yes!

2099
01:22:11.459 --> 01:22:13.622
<v LEE>I’m not . . . saying anything [laughs].

2100
01:22:13.622 --> 01:22:15.784
He was a lady’s man.

2101
01:22:15.784 --> 01:22:19.243
He was just a . . . He had much charisma.

2102
01:22:19.243 --> 01:22:21.405
<v ARSENEAULT>He was very good looking.

2103
01:22:21.405 --> 01:22:22.270
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

2104
01:22:22.270 --> 01:22:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Very good looking.

2105
01:22:24.000 --> 01:22:24.432
<v HOLMAN>That article . . .

2106
01:22:24.432 --> 01:22:24.865
<v ARSENEAULT>This one?

2107
01:22:24.865 --> 01:22:25.081
<v LEE>Yes.

2108
01:22:25.081 --> 01:22:26.595
<v HOLMAN>There were some tourists that were in . . .

2109
01:22:26.595 --> 01:22:27.243
<v LEE>In New Zealand?

2110
01:22:27.243 --> 01:22:28.108
<v HOLMAN>New Zealand [Cook Island].

2111
01:22:28.108 --> 01:22:28.324
<v LEE>Yeah.

2112
01:22:28.324 --> 01:22:30.270
<v HOLMAN>And the dog tag washed up on the shore . . .

2113
01:22:30.270 --> 01:22:30.486
<v ARSENEAULT>No!

2114
01:22:30.486 --> 01:22:33.081
<v HOLMAN>The New Zealand president called us . . . Well, called my dad. [Lee’s correction:

2115
01:22:33.081 --> 01:22:38.270
it was Chief Warrant Officer Donald R. Absher of the United States Navy Operations Coordinator at the United States embassy in Wellington, New Zealand]

2116
01:22:38.270 --> 01:22:38.486
<v ARSENEAULT>No!

2117
01:22:38.486 --> 01:22:39.568
That is like a movie.

2118
01:22:39.568 --> 01:22:40.000
<v LEE>Yes.

2119
01:22:40.000 --> 01:22:43.250
<v HOLMAN>And they called him and said they found it.

2120
01:22:43.250 --> 01:22:46.861
That was the closest kin in that area; in Louisiana.

2121
01:22:46.861 --> 01:22:50.472
Because he was a senior, my dad was a junior.

2122
01:22:50.472 --> 01:22:52.278
<v ARSENEAULT>So his dad’s dog tags . . .

2123
01:22:52.278 --> 01:22:52.639
<v LEE>Yes.

2124
01:22:52.639 --> 01:22:54.444
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . Washed up in New Zealand?

2125
01:22:54.444 --> 01:22:54.806
<v LEE>Yes.

2126
01:22:54.806 --> 01:22:56.250
<v ARSENEAULT>How did that happen?

2127
01:22:56.250 --> 01:22:56.972
<v HOLMAN>Who knows?

2128
01:22:56.972 --> 01:22:59.861
<v LEE>So we often say you might have . . . Siblings . . .

2129
01:22:59.861 --> 01:23:00.944
<v HOLMAN>In New Zealand.

2130
01:23:00.944 --> 01:23:01.306
<v LEE>Wherever.

2131
01:23:01.306 --> 01:23:03.833
But anyway, it is what it is.

2132
01:23:03.833 --> 01:23:06.000
<v HOLMAN>What year was that, ’86?

2133
01:23:06.000 --> 01:23:08.612
<v LEE>It was eighty . . . I’m thinking I wrote the dates because . . .

2134
01:23:08.612 --> 01:23:09.657
<v ARSENEAULT>Let’s see I can . . .

2135
01:23:09.657 --> 01:23:10.440
<v HOLMAN>At the top . . .

2136
01:23:10.440 --> 01:23:11.485
<v LEE>Look at the top.

2137
01:23:11.485 --> 01:23:12.269
<v ARSENEAULT>The top . . . ’68?

2138
01:23:12.269 --> 01:23:13.052
Oh no, ’87 . . .

2139
01:23:13.052 --> 01:23:13.313
<v HOLMAN>1986.

2140
01:23:13.313 --> 01:23:13.836
<v ARSENEAULT>’87. ’87.

2141
01:23:13.836 --> 01:23:17.493
The page is sixty . . . [Lee adds: dog tags were found in December of 1986,

2142
01:23:17.493 --> 01:23:23.761
and the call came in December also; the letter signed by Absher and the framed tags signed by Ambassador Cleveland arrived February of 1987]

2143
01:23:23.761 --> 01:23:25.590
<v LEE>Now listen to this very closely, Chelsea.

2144
01:23:25.590 --> 01:23:28.463
This is . . . I know God is real because listen to this.

2145
01:23:28.463 --> 01:23:30.813
Those dog tags washed up . . . and they found Richard.

2146
01:23:30.813 --> 01:23:35.254
Those dog tags were returned to Richard the week of his grandmother who was his mother’s funeral.

2147
01:23:35.254 --> 01:23:35.515
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2148
01:23:35.515 --> 01:23:40.478
<v LEE>So when everyone had gathered for the funeral, his mother’s funeral, we had those dog tags the same week.

2149
01:23:40.478 --> 01:23:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2150
01:23:41.000 --> 01:23:42.213
<v LEE>Now tell me . . .

2151
01:23:42.213 --> 01:23:43.021
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s insane.

2152
01:23:43.021 --> 01:23:43.426
<v LEE>Yes.

2153
01:23:43.426 --> 01:23:43.830
Yes.

2154
01:23:43.830 --> 01:23:46.660
<v ARSENEAULT>He received a call from the U.S.

2155
01:23:46.660 --> 01:23:48.681
Ambassador in Wellington, New Zealand.

2156
01:23:48.681 --> 01:23:49.085
Wow.

2157
01:23:49.085 --> 01:23:49.894
That’s crazy.

2158
01:23:49.894 --> 01:23:53.532
Do you know what he did in the military?

2159
01:23:53.532 --> 01:23:54.745
<v HOLMAN>I don’t know.

2160
01:23:54.745 --> 01:23:56.766
Uncle Kenny was with surveying.

2161
01:23:56.766 --> 01:24:04.851
I don’t know. [Lee adds: he was wounded on the island of Bougainville, and sent to Cook Island to recover]

2162
01:24:04.851 --> 01:24:05.660
<v LEE>Yeah he . . .

2163
01:24:05.660 --> 01:24:06.064
<v ARSENEAULT>Surveying?

2164
01:24:06.064 --> 01:24:08.085
<v HOLMAN>Yeah that was my uncle.

2165
01:24:08.085 --> 01:24:10.915
I don’t remember what my grandfather did.

2166
01:24:10.915 --> 01:24:11.319
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2167
01:24:11.319 --> 01:24:12.936
And his wife’s name?

2168
01:24:12.936 --> 01:24:14.957
<v LEE>Well, he was married twice.

2169
01:24:14.957 --> 01:24:15.362
<v ARSENEAULT>Twice.

2170
01:24:15.362 --> 01:24:19.000
<v LEE>And Elaine is his . . . was his first wife.

2171
01:24:19.000 --> 01:24:19.588
<v ARSENEAULT>First wife.

2172
01:24:19.588 --> 01:24:21.353
And that’s . . . both of their mother?

2173
01:24:21.353 --> 01:24:21.647
<v LEE>Right.

2174
01:24:21.647 --> 01:24:22.529
That is correct.

2175
01:24:22.529 --> 01:24:25.765
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn’t have . . . Did he have children with the second wife?

2176
01:24:25.765 --> 01:24:26.059
<v LEE>Yes.

2177
01:24:26.059 --> 01:24:27.824
<v ARSENEAULT>He had . . . so they have step-brothers?

2178
01:24:27.824 --> 01:24:28.118
<v LEE>Yes.

2179
01:24:28.118 --> 01:24:28.412
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2180
01:24:28.412 --> 01:24:30.471
<v LEE>A brother . . . A boy and a girl.

2181
01:24:30.471 --> 01:24:30.765
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2182
01:24:30.765 --> 01:24:31.059
Wow.

2183
01:24:31.059 --> 01:24:31.941
I’m trying to . . .

2184
01:24:31.941 --> 01:24:35.176
<v LEE>See, he was . . . Rich was thirteen when his parents were divorced.

2185
01:24:35.176 --> 01:24:35.471
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2186
01:24:35.471 --> 01:24:36.353
<v LEE>He was thirteen.

2187
01:24:36.353 --> 01:24:39.000
He would tell me that all the time.

2188
01:24:39.000 --> 01:24:40.652
<v HOLMAN>He’s the oldest boy.

2189
01:24:40.652 --> 01:24:41.065
<v LEE>Yeah.

2190
01:24:41.065 --> 01:24:42.304
<v ARSENEAULT>He’s the oldest?

2191
01:24:42.304 --> 01:24:42.717
<v HOLMAN>Boy.

2192
01:24:42.717 --> 01:24:43.130
<v ARSENEAULT>Boy?

2193
01:24:43.130 --> 01:24:43.543
<v LEE>[Agrees].

2194
01:24:43.543 --> 01:24:43.957
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2195
01:24:43.957 --> 01:24:44.370
Wow.

2196
01:24:44.370 --> 01:24:44.783
Wow

2197
01:24:44.783 --> 01:24:45.196
<v LEE>Yep.

2198
01:24:45.196 --> 01:24:49.326
<v ARSENEAULT>And this is his picture from ’87 with the dog?

2199
01:24:49.326 --> 01:24:50.152
With the . . .?

2200
01:24:50.152 --> 01:24:50.565
<v LEE>[Agrees].

2201
01:24:50.565 --> 01:24:53.457
That was at our home in Mossville.

2202
01:24:53.457 --> 01:24:58.000
The photographer had come out and got in the newspaper.

2203
01:24:58.000 --> 01:24:59.132
<v ARSENEAULT>What a story!

2204
01:24:59.132 --> 01:24:59.332
<v LEE>[Agrees].

2205
01:24:59.132 --> 01:24:59.510
<v HOLMAN>[. . . ?]

2206
01:24:59.510 --> 01:25:01.775
<v LEE>The same week of her funeral.

2207
01:25:01.775 --> 01:25:02.152
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2208
01:25:02.152 --> 01:25:05.172
<v LEE>When everyone had gathered it was like, “Wow!”

2209
01:25:05.172 --> 01:25:08.191
<v ARSENEAULT>So how long did you live in Mossville?

2210
01:25:08.191 --> 01:25:10.078
<v LEE>We got married in ’69.

2211
01:25:10.078 --> 01:25:12.343
Lived in Lake Charles a year.

2212
01:25:12.343 --> 01:25:14.608
Moved to Mossville in . . . October thirty-first.

2213
01:25:14.608 --> 01:25:14.985
<v ARSENEAULT>Halloween.

2214
01:25:14.985 --> 01:25:16.118
<v LEE>October thirty-first 1970.

2215
01:25:16.118 --> 01:25:23.667
We were trying . . . we found land, we had bought it, but we couldn’t get it straight to build on it.

2216
01:25:23.667 --> 01:25:28.574
So we did not build our home until . . . Of the summer of 1973.

2217
01:25:28.574 --> 01:25:30.083
1973 we moved in.

2218
01:25:30.083 --> 01:25:31.593
And . . . Where we are.

2219
01:25:31.593 --> 01:25:34.613
So I lived there from 1973 to now.

2220
01:25:34.613 --> 01:25:47.446
We’re looking at forty some years and that was the most . . . some people are saying . . . like I look at and I have to say this and I don’t care if it stays on this.

2221
01:25:47.446 --> 01:25:59.525
Like when I looked at that documentary and they were talking about Sasol and how everybody was happy and all of this and they had the fifteen minute . . . I looked at that.

2222
01:25:59.525 --> 01:26:02.544
My daughter showed me that the other night.

2223
01:26:02.544 --> 01:26:05.186
I’m saying, “I don’t feel this way.”

2224
01:26:05.186 --> 01:26:13.113
So I wrote a note and we put it on Facebook and that was the hardest thing for me to do.

2225
01:26:13.113 --> 01:26:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it say?

2226
01:26:15.000 --> 01:26:20.978
<v LEE>It . . . You know it said like, everyone was happy and they were this and that you know,

2227
01:26:20.978 --> 01:26:24.143
[?] better thing, the best thing and all of that.

2228
01:26:24.143 --> 01:26:25.901
<v HOLMAN>It felt like slavery again.

2229
01:26:25.901 --> 01:26:29.418
And the reason why, it’s like you’re separating the family.

2230
01:26:29.418 --> 01:26:31.527
You know, men go here and . . .

2231
01:26:31.527 --> 01:26:31.879
<v LEE>Women . . .

2232
01:26:31.879 --> 01:26:34.341
<v HOLMAN>Women and children go in this direction.

2233
01:26:34.341 --> 01:26:36.802
It was like a community being spilt.

2234
01:26:36.802 --> 01:26:39.967
And our community . . . we were somewhere in Lake Charles.

2235
01:26:39.967 --> 01:26:41.374
Somewhere in Moss Bluff.

2236
01:26:41.374 --> 01:26:43.132
The fun times we had?

2237
01:26:43.132 --> 01:26:44.890
You can’t do that anymore.

2238
01:26:44.890 --> 01:26:46.297
It’s like it’s lost.

2239
01:26:46.297 --> 01:26:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2240
01:26:47.000 --> 01:26:48.602
<v LEE>And that’s how we felt.

2241
01:26:48.602 --> 01:26:55.330
And then see, when it . . . burying my husband in 2010, and I’m not originally from Mossville, but there forty some years.

2242
01:26:55.330 --> 01:26:59.816
And then having to leave it again was like leaving him all over again.

2243
01:26:59.816 --> 01:27:04.301
When I first heard about the buyout, I’m saying . . . well I’ll tell you this.

2244
01:27:04.301 --> 01:27:07.184
They talked about things like that before Rich passed.

2245
01:27:07.184 --> 01:27:10.388
Matter of fact, his cousin talked to him about it.

2246
01:27:10.388 --> 01:27:11.990
Rich thought it was ridiculous.

2247
01:27:11.990 --> 01:27:14.553
He said, “Where are you going to move?

2248
01:27:14.553 --> 01:27:18.398
All these chemical plants are connected by pipelines, railway, all of this.

2249
01:27:18.398 --> 01:27:19.680
Where are you going?”

2250
01:27:19.680 --> 01:27:22.883
And so Rich opted to stay just where we were.

2251
01:27:22.883 --> 01:27:24.485
He said, “We aren’t going.”

2252
01:27:24.485 --> 01:27:26.087
That was before he died.

2253
01:27:26.087 --> 01:27:28.971
Well, it had to have been before he died.

2254
01:27:28.971 --> 01:27:32.175
So when this happened, it’s just how they handled it.

2255
01:27:32.175 --> 01:27:35.379
We found about the buying and that it was serious.

2256
01:27:35.379 --> 01:27:41.786
We had got then . . . called us to have a meeting at the recreation center to talk about jobs and stuff.

2257
01:27:41.786 --> 01:27:44.029
I’m saying, okay you’re talking about jobs.

2258
01:27:44.029 --> 01:27:44.990
What about Mossville?

2259
01:27:44.990 --> 01:27:50.117
And then we were saying what they moving they . . . They building this and what’s going on?

2260
01:27:50.117 --> 01:27:56.524
So they never came to us as a community first to let us know, “we’re thinking about this get ready.”

2261
01:27:56.524 --> 01:27:56.845
Okay?

2262
01:27:56.845 --> 01:27:58.767
It was like as an afterthought.

2263
01:27:58.767 --> 01:28:01.330
So I didn’t care for that at all.

2264
01:28:01.330 --> 01:28:03.893
But anyway, leaving Mossville was difficult for me.

2265
01:28:03.893 --> 01:28:04.534
Very difficult.

2266
01:28:04.534 --> 01:28:08.058
And we were comfortable there, we were closer to everything there.

2267
01:28:08.058 --> 01:28:10.621
My child was raised . . . we were all there.

2268
01:28:10.621 --> 01:28:12.864
Family closely . . . neighbors . . . the . . . closely knit community.

2269
01:28:12.864 --> 01:28:15.107
We loved and took care of each

2270
01:28:15.107 --> 01:28:16.388
<c.p>other from the beginning.

2271
01:28:16.388 --> 01:28:24.398
When I first went to Mossville it was so nice because blacks owned a lot of land and we didn’t see that in many places.

2272
01:28:24.398 --> 01:28:25.359
That was beautiful.

2273
01:28:25.359 --> 01:28:31.767
And just to look around and feel the breeze going through the trees and hear the rustling of the trees.

2274
01:28:31.767 --> 01:28:36.573
And not being so close to your neighbors, but close enough if you needed anyone.

2275
01:28:36.573 --> 01:28:41.699
And to reach out . . . Like Ms. Flo and I, we lived next door to each other.

2276
01:28:41.699 --> 01:28:43.301
We didn’t have a fence.

2277
01:28:43.301 --> 01:28:45.544
A lot of conversations, but no fence.

2278
01:28:45.544 --> 01:28:50.350
On the other side there was this little fence with blackberries growing to the Bernards.

2279
01:28:50.350 --> 01:28:59.320
And we could watch . . . we’d sit on the porch and watch them walking to church at the end of Prater Road to the Methodist church on the corner.

2280
01:28:59.320 --> 01:29:03.165
She was in all white and there he was following his wife.

2281
01:29:03.165 --> 01:29:06.689
And do you know they died within weeks of each other?

2282
01:29:06.689 --> 01:29:08.291
They were just that close.

2283
01:29:08.291 --> 01:29:10.214
It was just a beautiful thing.

2284
01:29:10.214 --> 01:29:14.058
When our house burned in 1983, the community gave us a shower.

2285
01:29:14.058 --> 01:29:20.146
The family gave Dawn . . . Dawn was about seven or eight years old, her own shower at her grandmother’s house.

2286
01:29:20.146 --> 01:29:21.107
This lady’s house.

2287
01:29:21.107 --> 01:29:23.029
They lived just a block down.

2288
01:29:23.029 --> 01:29:27.194
Everybody lived a block . . . I mean you were so close to each other.

2289
01:29:27.194 --> 01:29:29.437
We had grocery stores in the community.

2290
01:29:29.437 --> 01:29:29.757
Conoco . . .

2291
01:29:29.757 --> 01:29:32.000
<v HOLMAN>Everybody took care of each other.

2292
01:29:32.000 --> 01:29:32.661
<v LEE>You’re right.

2293
01:29:32.661 --> 01:29:34.644
Conoco filling station at the corner.

2294
01:29:34.644 --> 01:29:36.627
It was just a beautiful thing.

2295
01:29:36.627 --> 01:29:41.585
And then when they came with the lights on Prater Road, we were so excited!

2296
01:29:41.585 --> 01:29:42.576
And running water.

2297
01:29:42.576 --> 01:29:43.568
All of that.

2298
01:29:43.568 --> 01:29:45.551
You lived through all of that.

2299
01:29:45.551 --> 01:29:47.534
It was just a good place.

2300
01:29:47.534 --> 01:29:52.161
They had parades and a parade down Prater Road from Reesie [Mae Watts] Mouton,

2301
01:29:52.161 --> 01:30:02.407
a member of Mount Zion Baptist Church sponsored the parade that would come down Prater Road into Queensboro and all of that area. [Lee adds that she passed away September 10,

2302
01:30:02.407 --> 01:30:04.390
2016] It was just a closely

2303
01:30:04.390 --> 01:30:05.051
<c.p>knit family.

2304
01:30:05.051 --> 01:30:06.373
And the Mossville Pirates?

2305
01:30:06.373 --> 01:30:08.686
Oh my God, that’s all you heard.

2306
01:30:08.686 --> 01:30:10.008
It was just good.

2307
01:30:10.008 --> 01:30:11.331
So that I miss.

2308
01:30:11.331 --> 01:30:17.610
And I tell you what when they said that, I started . . . It was a feeling of disbelief, then tears.

2309
01:30:17.610 --> 01:30:22.568
I couldn’t stop crying just to look and see how my community was being dismantled.

2310
01:30:22.568 --> 01:30:24.551
And the prices they were offering.

2311
01:30:24.551 --> 01:30:29.178
In other words, they’re . . . And I’m sure there were persons that were better off,

2312
01:30:29.178 --> 01:30:33.475
but then those of us who had more got less in a sense.

2313
01:30:33.475 --> 01:30:35.458
So it was not done fairly.

2314
01:30:35.458 --> 01:30:38.763
So we said, “Why don’t you use a running scale?”

2315
01:30:38.763 --> 01:30:43.390
But it was like giving an offer and you either accept or reject it.

2316
01:30:43.390 --> 01:30:45.373
So it was not done fairly.

2317
01:30:45.373 --> 01:30:47.356
<v HOLMAN>We’re gonna just build around you . . .

2318
