Then we went to Westlake and we put one on the corner of Sampson Street and George . . . what’s his name?

2778
01:47:10.738 --> 01:47:11.611
Gerald Washington Street.

2779
01:47:11.611 --> 01:47:12.775
You remember the mayor?

2780
01:47:12.775 --> 01:47:14.520
It was where . . . On his street.

2781
01:47:14.520 --> 01:47:15.393
On the corner.

2782
01:47:15.393 --> 01:47:20.340
And when you drove into Mossville or you were passing through to get to the chemical plants,

2783
01:47:20.340 --> 01:47:25.578
you knew that this was Obama country. [Lee adds: the Katherine Marie Jourdan Victorian Scholarship began in 1976.

2784
01:47:25.578 --> 01:47:31.107
It is awarded to students at Westlake and Sulphur High School who are residents of Mossville; scholarship still awarded]

2785
01:47:31.107 --> 01:47:34.598
<v ARSENEAULT>I have to ask, what was it like when he got elected?

2786
01:47:34.598 --> 01:47:35.180
What was . . .

2787
01:47:35.180 --> 01:47:35.471
<v LEE>Awesome!

2788
01:47:35.471 --> 01:47:36.053
<v HOLMAN>On fire.

2789
01:47:36.053 --> 01:47:37.217
We had a bonfire.

2790
01:47:37.217 --> 01:47:38.963
<v LEE>Oh yeah we had a bonfire.

2791
01:47:38.963 --> 01:47:39.545
Oh yeah.

2792
01:47:39.545 --> 01:47:41.000
We had a little . . .

2793
01:47:41.000 --> 01:47:42.560
<v HOLMAN>The whole community came.

2794
01:47:42.560 --> 01:47:43.340
<v LEE>Oh, yes.

2795
01:47:43.340 --> 01:47:48.410
We had the yard in the . . . I mean the sign in the yard.

2796
01:47:48.410 --> 01:47:49.190
The balloons.

2797
01:47:49.190 --> 01:47:50.360
The balloon release.

2798
01:47:50.360 --> 01:47:51.920
Especially for his inauguration.

2799
01:47:51.920 --> 01:47:57.380
But “Pop,” the man there, this is Richard’s step-father . . . “Maw Maw” had already passed.

2800
01:47:57.380 --> 01:47:58.940
Elaine had already passed.

2801
01:47:58.940 --> 01:48:00.500
She passed in 2006.

2802
01:48:00.500 --> 01:48:05.180
But Pop passed in 2009 so he was here for the election.

2803
01:48:05.180 --> 01:48:06.350
And he cried.

2804
01:48:06.350 --> 01:48:12.200
He said, “I never thought I would live to see a black person as president.”

2805
01:48:12.200 --> 01:48:13.760
And he just cried.

2806
01:48:13.760 --> 01:48:18.440
Now he was also in the armed services with Elaine’s first husband.

2807
01:48:18.440 --> 01:48:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>They were together?

2808
01:48:20.000 --> 01:48:21.231
<v LEE>No, they were not.

2809
01:48:21.231 --> 01:48:22.462
<v ARSENEAULT>That would be interesting.

2810
01:48:22.462 --> 01:48:24.615
<v LEE>That would have been . . . And guess what?

2811
01:48:24.615 --> 01:48:26.154
His first name was Lee.

2812
01:48:26.154 --> 01:48:27.385
Last name was Palms.

2813
01:48:27.385 --> 01:48:27.692
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2814
01:48:27.692 --> 01:48:28.923
<v LEE>So Paw Paw Lee.

2815
01:48:28.923 --> 01:48:29.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Same . . .

2816
01:48:29.231 --> 01:48:31.077
<v LEE>Same thing just first last name.

2817
01:48:31.077 --> 01:48:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, wow.

2818
01:48:32.000 --> 01:48:32.308
<v LEE>Yeah.

2819
01:48:32.308 --> 01:48:34.773
He was a . . . That was my buddy there.

2820
01:48:34.773 --> 01:48:35.698
Oh my goodness.

2821
01:48:35.698 --> 01:48:38.163
But he cried and . . . but it was beautiful.

2822
01:48:38.163 --> 01:48:42.477
And we had the bonfire, we invited people over, we had the backyard covered.

2823
01:48:42.477 --> 01:48:43.709
It was just something.

2824
01:48:43.709 --> 01:48:44.942
It was just awesome.

2825
01:48:44.942 --> 01:48:46.483
<v ARSENEAULT>What did Mr. Richard say?

2826
01:48:46.483 --> 01:48:48.640
<v LEE>Oh my God he couldn’t stop talking.

2827
01:48:48.640 --> 01:48:49.872
Can you believe this?

2828
01:48:49.872 --> 01:48:53.570
What a beautiful thing, but they have not treated our president right.

2829
01:48:53.570 --> 01:48:55.727
I’ll just say it how it is.

2830
01:48:55.727 --> 01:49:01.273
But history will come down and will let the world know that he has done a good job.

2831
01:49:01.273 --> 01:49:02.506
History will show that.

2832
01:49:02.506 --> 01:49:04.971
Okay, anything else we need to talk about?

2833
01:49:04.971 --> 01:49:05.587
About Mossville.

2834
01:49:05.587 --> 01:49:05.895
Mossville.

2835
01:49:05.895 --> 01:49:08.669
And that was all in Mossville on Prater Road.

2836
01:49:08.669 --> 01:49:12.983
<v ARSENEAULT>So community . . . the community leaders while when you were there, who were they besides . . .?

2837
01:49:12.983 --> 01:49:14.215
<v LEE>James Rigmaiden for sure.

2838
01:49:14.215 --> 01:49:16.680
<v ARSENEAULT>Tell me a little bit more about him.

2839
01:49:16.680 --> 01:49:19.145
<v LEE>Okay let’s see how much I can tell.

2840
01:49:19.145 --> 01:49:21.919
Now see, I’m in the club with his wife.

2841
01:49:21.919 --> 01:49:25.000
I wish you would interview his wife Joan Rigmaiden.

2842
01:49:25.000 --> 01:49:27.121
<v ARSENEAULT>I would love to do that.

2843
01:49:27.121 --> 01:49:27.475
<v LEE>Yeah.

2844
01:49:27.475 --> 01:49:31.717
And her son Kerry Rigmaiden . . . They live right there in Mossville on . . .

2845
01:49:31.717 --> 01:49:33.131
<v ARSENEAULT>I interviewed Ms. Carolyn . . .

2846
01:49:33.131 --> 01:49:33.838
<v LEE>Oh, Carolyn.

2847
01:49:33.838 --> 01:49:35.960
But no, this is a Kerry.

2848
01:49:35.960 --> 01:49:38.081
His name, the son, Kerry Rigmaiden.

2849
01:49:38.081 --> 01:49:38.434
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

2850
01:49:38.434 --> 01:49:40.909
<v LEE>He lives . . . it’s a two story house.

2851
01:49:40.909 --> 01:49:46.566
It’s on Old Spanish Trail when you leave Prater Road you’re turning east, which is right.

2852
01:49:46.566 --> 01:49:50.455
It’s on a corner and his mom lives right behind him.

2853
01:49:50.455 --> 01:49:53.990
They would be able to tell you about Mr. Rigmaiden.

2854
01:49:53.990 --> 01:49:58.232
But he was so outspoken and then he was so business like.

2855
01:49:58.232 --> 01:50:01.768
This is the same math teacher I told you about.

2856
01:50:01.768 --> 01:50:03.889
And he went to Grambling also.

2857
01:50:03.889 --> 01:50:05.657
He’s a graduate of Grambling.

2858
01:50:05.657 --> 01:50:10.253
Now LaSalle Williams later on became president of NAACP and all of that,

2859
01:50:10.253 --> 01:50:15.202
but these were two of the leaders, but that Rigmaiden man, he was awesome.

2860
01:50:15.202 --> 01:50:21.212
I mean he didn’t . . . you couldn’t talk over him or around him or fool him or anything.

2861
01:50:21.212 --> 01:50:25.101
And he wouldn’t give in until he thought it was right.

2862
01:50:25.101 --> 01:50:27.222
So I . . . he impressed me greatly.

2863
01:50:27.222 --> 01:50:28.283
He really did.

2864
01:50:28.283 --> 01:50:28.990
Anything else?

2865
01:50:28.990 --> 01:50:29.697
<v ARSENEAULT>Local businesses?

2866
01:50:29.697 --> 01:50:33.586
<v LEE>The . . . well Heaven on Earth, it is still on Prater Road.

2867
01:50:33.586 --> 01:50:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve eaten there.

2868
01:50:35.000 --> 01:50:36.228
<v LEE>Yeah, you’ve been there.

2869
01:50:36.228 --> 01:50:40.524
And I tell . . . but you never ate it before his . . . when his father lived.

2870
01:50:40.524 --> 01:50:41.444
The older man.

2871
01:50:41.444 --> 01:50:43.593
It was awesome, Heaven on Earth . . . Nikki.

2872
01:50:43.593 --> 01:50:47.582
But he taught his son, but he might have kept some things away.

2873
01:50:47.582 --> 01:50:48.810
But Heaven on Earth.

2874
01:50:48.810 --> 01:50:53.413
There was a Conoco station on the corner of Old Spanish Trail and Prater Road.

2875
01:50:53.413 --> 01:51:09.984
Matter of fact, I found out later . . . I found out matter of fact when I went to the funeral home to view my husband’s body that it was there that my husband would go there and use the pay phone to call me when he was courting me when I was still in Jeanerette.

2876
01:51:09.984 --> 01:51:10.291
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2877
01:51:10.291 --> 01:51:10.598
<v HOLMAN>Trahans.

2878
01:51:10.598 --> 01:51:18.270
<v LEE>And the Trahan Grocery Store was also on . . . Right about a block passed Heaven on Earth on the same side was the Trahan Grocery Store.

2879
01:51:18.270 --> 01:51:19.190
<v ARSENEAULT>Who owned that?

2880
01:51:19.190 --> 01:51:21.646
<v LEE>A white person owned it, did it not?

2881
01:51:21.646 --> 01:51:23.487
The [Trahan’s?] were white . . . are white.

2882
01:51:23.487 --> 01:51:25.942
But I forgot his name who managed it.

2883
01:51:25.942 --> 01:51:26.249
<v HOLMAN>Moses . . .

2884
01:51:26.249 --> 01:51:26.862
<v LEE>Moses, yeah.

2885
01:51:26.862 --> 01:51:29.011
I can’t think of Moses’ last name.

2886
01:51:29.011 --> 01:51:31.159
<v HOLMAN>But they all lived in Mossville area.

2887
01:51:31.159 --> 01:51:33.000
I can’t remember the family.

2888
01:51:33.000 --> 01:51:33.538
<v LEE>I cannot.

2889
01:51:33.538 --> 01:51:34.885
Because it was a Rougeau.

2890
01:51:34.885 --> 01:51:36.769
<v ARSENEAULT>Were they a white family as well?

2891
01:51:36.769 --> 01:51:37.038
<v LEE>Yes.

2892
01:51:37.038 --> 01:51:38.385
Was Rougeau and then that.

2893
01:51:38.385 --> 01:51:40.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anyone protest the Rougeau’s?

2894
01:51:40.000 --> 01:51:45.564
<v LEE>Yes, I know you’ve heard about that because we were . . . yeah, this will be before you were born.

2895
01:51:45.564 --> 01:51:48.655
They had the paddy wagons and all of that stuff.

2896
01:51:48.655 --> 01:51:50.509
Yeah it was all out there.

2897
01:51:50.509 --> 01:51:55.145
It was . . . now the Jourdans know a lot about that because they were really involved.

2898
01:51:55.145 --> 01:51:56.382
Have you spoken with . . .?

2899
01:51:56.382 --> 01:51:58.236
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I’m interviewing them soon.

2900
01:51:58.236 --> 01:52:01.018
<v LEE>Well the one who did . . . what is her name?

2901
01:52:01.018 --> 01:52:01.327
Claudia?

2902
01:52:01.327 --> 01:52:03.491
Claudia was one of the main persons.

2903
01:52:03.491 --> 01:52:03.800
Claudia.

2904
01:52:03.800 --> 01:52:06.273
She’s a Jourdan, but now she’s a Hardy.

2905
01:52:06.273 --> 01:52:07.509
I mean a Handy.

2906
01:52:07.509 --> 01:52:10.291
But yeah they’ll really be able to tell us.

2907
01:52:10.291 --> 01:52:11.836
They have the inside story.

2908
01:52:11.836 --> 01:52:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did it close down after that?

2909
01:52:14.000 --> 01:52:15.884
<v LEE>Yeah for a period of time.

2910
01:52:15.884 --> 01:52:18.081
And then things improved and all that.

2911
01:52:18.081 --> 01:52:20.907
So that was all a part of the city.

2912
01:52:20.907 --> 01:52:24.360
It’s just like, “Okay we moving from Grambling we protesting . . .” Okay.

2913
01:52:24.360 --> 01:52:24.674
Alright.

2914
01:52:24.674 --> 01:52:26.558
So yeah I’m trying to think.

2915
01:52:26.558 --> 01:52:35.977
There was also another . . . A gas station in Mossville that was closer to . . . the corner of Old Spanish Trail and Pryor Street across from where Truth Tabernacle was torn down.

2916
01:52:35.977 --> 01:52:36.291
<v HOLMAN>7-11.

2917
01:52:36.291 --> 01:52:37.547
<v LEE>It was a 7-11?

2918
01:52:37.547 --> 01:52:37.860
Yeah.

2919
01:52:37.860 --> 01:52:39.430
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it the Bell 7-11?

2920
01:52:39.430 --> 01:52:40.058
<v LEE>Yes, yes.

2921
01:52:40.058 --> 01:52:40.372
Okay.

2922
01:52:40.372 --> 01:52:41.000
Alright.

2923
01:52:41.000 --> 01:52:41.870
<v ARSENEAULT>Heard about that one.

2924
01:52:41.870 --> 01:52:42.957
<v LEE>Heard about that one, okay.

2925
01:52:42.957 --> 01:52:44.043
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the Paradise Club?

2926
01:52:44.043 --> 01:52:44.261
<v LEE>Hey . . .

2927
01:52:44.261 --> 01:52:45.565
<v HOLMAN>That was next door to us.

2928
01:52:45.565 --> 01:52:46.000
<v LEE>Yeah.

2929
01:52:46.000 --> 01:52:50.832
<v ARSENEAULT>I figured when you told me on Prater right next to the church, I was like well that’s right . . .

2930
01:52:50.832 --> 01:52:51.341
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah.

2931
01:52:51.341 --> 01:52:53.884
There’s a building . . . there’s a family who lived between us.

2932
01:52:53.884 --> 01:52:57.699
That was one of the reasons I really didn’t want to live on Prater Road.

2933
01:52:57.699 --> 01:53:01.514
When we first moved there we bought property down Evergreen Drive in that first curve.

2934
01:53:01.514 --> 01:53:03.040
And I’m glad we did not.

2935
01:53:03.040 --> 01:53:04.058
It didn’t work out.

2936
01:53:04.058 --> 01:53:09.908
We sold it later on and I had a bigger piece because only one acre on Evergreen, and on Prater it was three.

2937
01:53:09.908 --> 01:53:14.740
I really didn’t want to live on Prater at the beginning because it was so close to the Paradise.

2938
01:53:14.740 --> 01:53:16.012
And they had big entertainment.

2939
01:53:16.012 --> 01:53:17.538
<v HOLMAN>I remember times . . . I was young.

2940
01:53:17.538 --> 01:53:18.555
Maybe four . . . five . . . four.

2941
01:53:18.555 --> 01:53:20.844
And I think it was . . . Was it Tina Turner?

2942
01:53:20.844 --> 01:53:21.098
<v LEE>[Agrees].

2943
01:53:21.098 --> 01:53:25.676
<v HOLMAN>Who was performing and they had cars all the way down the . . . I remember my dad directed traffic.

2944
01:53:25.676 --> 01:53:28.983
Some people parked in the yard. . . [Lee clarifies it was not their yard]

2945
01:53:28.983 --> 01:53:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>In the yard?

2946
01:53:30.000 --> 01:53:30.643
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah.

2947
01:53:30.643 --> 01:53:32.895
Could get your little money from that.

2948
01:53:32.895 --> 01:53:34.182
Charge them for parking.

2949
01:53:34.182 --> 01:53:34.503
<v HOLMAN>Yeah.

2950
01:53:34.503 --> 01:53:35.147
<v LEE>Yeah, yeah.

2951
01:53:35.147 --> 01:53:38.364
So even before it was much bigger than that too.

2952
01:53:38.364 --> 01:53:38.685
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

2953
01:53:38.685 --> 01:53:40.937
<v LEE>It was so much bigger than that.

2954
01:53:40.937 --> 01:53:41.902
It was interesting.

2955
01:53:41.902 --> 01:53:46.727
They say this is the place where cool dudes and the foxy chicks hang out.

2956
01:53:46.727 --> 01:53:48.014
We said, “Okay.” [Laughs].

2957
01:53:48.014 --> 01:53:49.944
And then it gradually went down.

2958
01:53:49.944 --> 01:53:58.629
But I mean . . . when you . . . it’s just . . . and you look at all those good days and you have to cry to see what has happened to us.

2959
01:53:58.629 --> 01:54:05.706
And my question always is, why is it that these chemical plants move into these communities like that and just take over?

2960
01:54:05.706 --> 01:54:06.993
It’s like you’re nothing.

2961
01:54:06.993 --> 01:54:09.245
You’re just . . . and it’s all for progress.

2962
01:54:09.245 --> 01:54:10.853
But what about the life.

2963
01:54:10.853 --> 01:54:16.000
I mean that place has been there since the 1700s and just to . . . It’s heartbreaking.

2964
01:54:16.000 --> 01:54:19.974
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the first buyouts of Bel Air?

2965
01:54:19.974 --> 01:54:22.865
<v LEE>The first buyouts was in Bel Air, right.

2966
01:54:22.865 --> 01:54:29.368
And one of our members of our club, Broussards, they were bought out and they moved to Westlake.

2967
01:54:29.368 --> 01:54:33.342
And they might be bought out again, they’re so very close.

2968
01:54:33.342 --> 01:54:33.703
<v HOLMAN>Kenny.

2969
01:54:33.703 --> 01:54:36.594
<v LEE>And Uncle Kenny, he lived across the school.

2970
01:54:36.594 --> 01:54:36.955
Yeah.

2971
01:54:36.955 --> 01:54:39.845
They were . . . they gave him a difficult time.

2972
01:54:39.845 --> 01:54:43.819
They weren’t fair, but God worked that thing out, and hey.

2973
01:54:43.819 --> 01:54:44.903
He has flourished.

2974
01:54:44.903 --> 01:54:48.877
I just believe that when you don’t do a thing right,

2975
01:54:48.877 --> 01:54:54.297
God is always . . . the right thing is always going to work out in the end.

2976
01:54:54.297 --> 01:54:55.742
And it worked out.

2977
01:54:55.742 --> 01:54:56.826
He’s doing well.

2978
01:54:56.826 --> 01:55:01.161
And it wasn’t because of Conoco either . . . Because of the Lord, actually.

2979
01:55:01.161 --> 01:55:03.690
But his house was across from us.

2980
01:55:03.690 --> 01:55:08.026
He probably has spoken about that house was across from the school.

2981
01:55:08.026 --> 01:55:09.471
And so . . . what else?

2982
01:55:09.471 --> 01:55:10.916
Anything else we’re forgetting?

2983
01:55:10.916 --> 01:55:12.000
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?] about Mossville

2984
01:55:12.000 --> 01:55:12.450
<v LEE>Hmm?

2985
01:55:12.450 --> 01:55:13.802
<v HOLMAN>[. . .?] Touched on everything.

2986
01:55:13.802 --> 01:55:14.252
<v LEE>Yeah.

2987
01:55:14.252 --> 01:55:20.107
I know one thing, the water . . . we were so excited about when we . . .

2988
01:55:20.107 --> 01:55:22.359
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, when did that happen?

2989
01:55:22.359 --> 01:55:24.611
<v LEE>Okay, I’m trying to think.

2990
01:55:24.611 --> 01:55:33.168
We built in ’73 so the water came through . . . it had to have been ’71 or ’72 I’m thinking.

2991
01:55:33.168 --> 01:55:36.771
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they get the water in Mossville?

2992
01:55:36.771 --> 01:55:39.023
<v LEE>Everything . . . It was federally funded.

2993
01:55:39.023 --> 01:55:40.824
Some had matching funds.

2994
01:55:40.824 --> 01:55:42.626
I remember Fisher Road.

2995
01:55:42.626 --> 01:55:44.878
Dorothy . . . no, what’s her name?

2996
01:55:44.878 --> 01:55:48.031
Fishers . . . Lorraine Cole . . . She was a Hartman.

2997
01:55:48.031 --> 01:55:52.084
They had the well water and all of that.

2998
01:55:52.084 --> 01:56:01.542
And then after the water came in you had to pay to do the connecting from the road to your house.

2999
01:56:01.542 --> 01:56:02.443
That part.

3000
01:56:02.443 --> 01:56:09.198
And according to how much you had; how much you could afford to do that.

3001
01:56:09.198 --> 01:56:11.000
But that was . . .

3002
01:56:11.000 --> 01:56:13.761
<v HOLMAN>City sewage just came into effect in like . . .

3003
01:56:13.761 --> 01:56:15.142
<v LEE>I’m trying to think.

3004
01:56:15.142 --> 01:56:19.284
No, no it’s been . . . because I remember we had the running water,

3005
01:56:19.284 --> 01:56:24.117
but we still had the mechanical systems and the septic tanks for a while.

3006
01:56:24.117 --> 01:56:29.294
I’m trying to . . . I can’t remember the year, but we were so excited about that.

3007
01:56:29.294 --> 01:56:33.782
We said, “Oh boy we don’t have to worry about a thing anymore.”

3008
01:56:33.782 --> 01:56:35.508
And so that was great.

3009
01:56:35.508 --> 01:56:37.234
And I’m trying to think.

3010
01:56:37.234 --> 01:56:37.924
Anything else?

3011
01:56:37.924 --> 01:56:41.030
<v ARSENEAULT>Were any politicians kind of instrumental in getting . . . Helping?

3012
01:56:41.030 --> 01:56:41.376
Or . . .?

3013
01:56:41.376 --> 01:56:43.792
<v LEE>It was all [hey?] . . . nothing stands out.

3014
01:56:43.792 --> 01:56:45.173
Nothing really stands out.

3015
01:56:45.173 --> 01:56:47.589
<v ARSENEAULT>I have to ask about the pollution.

3016
01:56:47.589 --> 01:56:53.802
Like when did they first find out that the plants were kind of spilling stuff into the area?

3017
01:56:53.802 --> 01:56:58.635
<v LEE>Well, when I first moved to Mossville, I . . . You didn’t smell all of that.

3018
01:56:58.635 --> 01:57:01.051
But gradually you did start seeing it.

3019
01:57:01.051 --> 01:57:09.335
And you would always know that you were getting close because when you . . . got at the top of the bridge coming from the east,

3020
01:57:09.335 --> 01:57:13.132
going west you said, “Okay we’re getting close to the plants.”

3021
01:57:13.132 --> 01:57:19.000
And there’s not a wall that’s going to stop the thing from coming so it’s coming.

3022
01:57:19.000 --> 01:57:23.060
<v HOLMAN>Start noticing more families in the Bel Air area becoming sick.

3023
01:57:23.060 --> 01:57:23.798
Different cancers.

3024
01:57:23.798 --> 01:57:26.012
That might have been what, eighties?

3025
01:57:26.012 --> 01:57:26.381
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3026
01:57:26.381 --> 01:57:26.750
Probably.

3027
01:57:26.750 --> 01:57:27.488
Probably so.

3028
01:57:27.488 --> 01:57:28.595
I can’t remember.

3029
01:57:28.595 --> 01:57:30.810
I can’t remember when it was.

3030
01:57:30.810 --> 01:57:32.286
<v HOLMAN>Different strands of cancer.

3031
01:57:32.286 --> 01:57:35.976
<v ARSENEAULT>I know there’s been a lot of coverage of it.

3032
01:57:35.976 --> 01:57:42.988
I just like to make sure to ask because it’s kind of part of the history of the place.

3033
01:57:42.988 --> 01:57:47.786
I know they spilled . . . I think I read it was in ’94, the . . .

3034
01:57:47.786 --> 01:57:51.845
<v LEE>And there were some I’m sure that we’ll never know about.

3035
01:57:51.845 --> 01:58:05.500
This is probably . . . this is one of the reasons it’s difficult for them to build a new bridge because they will have to dig so deep in for the pilings and all that that’s down in there.

3036
01:58:05.500 --> 01:58:06.976
That Calcasieu River Bridge.

3037
01:58:06.976 --> 01:58:09.190
<v ARSENEAULT>They don’t want to disturb it?

3038
01:58:09.190 --> 01:58:09.929
<v HOLMAN>Disturb it.

3039
01:58:09.929 --> 01:58:11.774
And that’s exactly what’s happening.

3040
01:58:11.774 --> 01:58:12.143
<v LEE>Right.

3041
01:58:12.143 --> 01:58:18.786
So it’s not just . . . it’s just . . . if you really [. . .?] you could see the whole story it’s this area [?].

3042
01:58:18.786 --> 01:58:23.214
And as Rich would often say, there are risks on every job.

3043
01:58:23.214 --> 01:58:27.274
This he said . . . His job afforded him a very comfortable living.

3044
01:58:27.274 --> 01:58:29.119
And so . . . and it did.

3045
01:58:29.119 --> 01:58:37.238
We were able to send our daughter to college and right now she doesn’t owe a loan to anybody for her education.

3046
01:58:37.238 --> 01:58:39.452
So that was definitely a blessing.

3047
01:58:39.452 --> 01:58:45.726
Some children are still paying for . . . Like the Obamas said they are still paying for their education.

3048
01:58:45.726 --> 01:58:47.940
They’re finished with all of that.

3049
01:58:47.940 --> 01:58:54.583
So I . . . So that was a blessing in being able to work at one of the chemical plants.

3050
01:58:54.583 --> 01:58:57.167
So I guess risk with the advantages.

3051
01:58:57.167 --> 01:59:01.595
Let’s see is there anything else I need . . . another thing else, too.

3052
01:59:01.595 --> 01:59:07.869
It just seemed as the older people started dying out and the young ones started coming in,

3053
01:59:07.869 --> 01:59:14.881
they didn’t have the same love and respect for each other in the community as the older people did.

3054
01:59:14.881 --> 01:59:16.357
Wasn’t as closely knit.

3055
01:59:16.357 --> 01:59:20.786
You couldn’t always just leave your door unlocked and things like that.

3056
01:59:20.786 --> 01:59:23.000
And I guess it’s a . . .

3057
01:59:23.000 --> 01:59:23.605
<v HOLMAN>Generations change.

3058
01:59:23.605 --> 01:59:23.908
<v LEE>Yeah.

3059
01:59:23.908 --> 01:59:24.513
Change, yeah.

3060
01:59:24.513 --> 01:59:28.143
And but then after Hurricane Rita things began to look up again.

3061
01:59:28.143 --> 01:59:33.286
And I’m saying, “Alright Mossville!” the church was being built, the Peters’ family home was being built.

3062
01:59:33.286 --> 01:59:35.706
It still didn’t . . . it has never been completed.

3063
01:59:35.706 --> 01:59:37.824
You probably noticed that on Prater Road.

3064
01:59:37.824 --> 01:59:39.034
It’s a big house.

3065
01:59:39.034 --> 01:59:40.244
When you’re going down . . .

3066
01:59:40.244 --> 01:59:41.151
<v ARSENEAULT>Victorian style house?

3067
01:59:41.151 --> 01:59:43.269
<v LEE>No, that’s a pink house you’re saying.

3068
01:59:43.269 --> 01:59:43.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

3069
01:59:43.571 --> 01:59:44.479
<v LEE>That was moved.

3070
01:59:44.479 --> 01:59:45.387
It was moved.

3071
01:59:45.387 --> 01:59:47.202
That was moved into the community.

3072
01:59:47.202 --> 01:59:47.504
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

3073
01:59:47.504 --> 01:59:50.832
<v LEE>But before that there’s a house that was . . . is being built.

3074
01:59:50.832 --> 01:59:52.042
Well, it’s not anymore.

3075
01:59:52.042 --> 01:59:53.252
It was never completed.

3076
01:59:53.252 --> 01:59:57.487
But it’s on . . . it’s across the street at an angle from where we lived.

3077
01:59:57.487 --> 01:59:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I know.

3078
01:59:59.000 --> 01:59:59.291
<v LEE>Okay.

3079
01:59:59.291 --> 02:00:02.494
It’s going to be on your left if you’re going north.

3080
02:00:02.494 --> 02:00:04.532
But anyway, they began . . . I said, “Alright!”

3081
02:00:04.532 --> 02:00:05.987
<v ARSENEAULT>By like the MEAN trailer?

3082
02:00:05.987 --> 02:00:07.152
The Mossville Environmental Action . . .

3083
02:00:07.152 --> 02:00:07.443
<v LEE>Yeah!

3084
02:00:07.443 --> 02:00:08.608
Oh, no. not that.

3085
02:00:08.608 --> 02:00:09.481
<v ARSENEAULT>Not that one?

3086
02:00:09.481 --> 02:00:14.430
<v LEE>No no. no, no. No. if you’re leaving from Old Spanish Trail, if you cross the track,

3087
02:00:14.430 --> 02:00:18.506
my house . . . If you keep going . . . my house . . . there’s this . . . the church New Beginnings.

3088
02:00:18.506 --> 02:00:19.671
It’s on the left.

3089
02:00:19.671 --> 02:00:21.418
My house is on the right.

3090
02:00:21.418 --> 02:00:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

3091
02:00:22.000 --> 02:00:27.180
<v LEE>Okay, but right before you get to New Beginnings there’s this pink house that you talked about.

3092
02:00:27.180 --> 02:00:32.055
But before that, there’s the open space and then there’s the house that they were building.

3093
02:00:32.055 --> 02:00:33.883
A big house they were building.

3094
02:00:33.883 --> 02:00:34.188
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

3095
02:00:34.188 --> 02:00:35.711
<v LEE>And it was never completed.

3096
02:00:35.711 --> 02:00:36.930
It’s never been completed.

3097
02:00:36.930 --> 02:00:39.977
Well that’s what I’m saying was the Peters’ family home.

3098
02:00:39.977 --> 02:00:40.891
They were rebuilding.

3099
02:00:40.891 --> 02:00:45.156
I said, “Alright Mossville!” my daughter was moving back home after her father passed.

3100
02:00:45.156 --> 02:00:50.336
She was remodeling her grandmother’s house, Elaine, and her first . . . Her cousin was remodeling her grandmother’s house.

3101
02:00:50.336 --> 02:00:51.250
They were sisters.

3102
02:00:51.250 --> 02:00:52.773
So I said, “Alright Mossville!

3103
02:00:52.773 --> 02:00:53.383
Coming back!”

3104
02:00:53.383 --> 02:00:55.820
Heaven on Earth refurbishing and all of that.

3105
02:00:55.820 --> 02:00:56.734
I said, “Okay.

3106
02:00:56.734 --> 02:00:58.258
All of this is happening.

3107
02:00:58.258 --> 02:00:59.477
New church coming up.

3108
02:00:59.477 --> 02:01:00.086
Alright Mossville!”

3109
02:01:00.086 --> 02:01:01.000
I’ll be . . .

3110
02:01:01.000 --> 02:01:06.172
<v HOLMAN>In my grandmother’s home remodeling, my uncle walks in. he say, “Stop what you’re doing.”

3111
02:01:06.172 --> 02:01:07.552
<v LEE>Stop what you’re doing.

3112
02:01:07.552 --> 02:01:08.241
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Kenny?

3113
02:01:08.241 --> 02:01:08.586
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3114
02:01:08.586 --> 02:01:09.966
<v HOLMAN>I was like, “Why?”

3115
02:01:09.966 --> 02:01:11.000
<v LEE>Why, yeah.

3116
02:01:11.000 --> 02:01:13.970
<v HOLMAN>He said, “They’re going to buy us out.”

3117
02:01:13.970 --> 02:01:18.054
And when he told me that I was just like, “What?

3118
02:01:18.054 --> 02:01:18.426
Why?

3119
02:01:18.426 --> 02:01:19.168
I’ll stop.”

3120
02:01:19.168 --> 02:01:19.540
<v LEE>Yeah.

3121
02:01:19.540 --> 02:01:19.911
Yeah.

3122
02:01:19.911 --> 02:01:21.767
<v ARSENEAULT>They moved the house, huh?

3123
02:01:21.767 --> 02:01:22.881
<v HOLMAN>Say that again?

3124
02:01:22.881 --> 02:01:25.109
<v ARSENEAULT>They . . . Did they move the house?

3125
02:01:25.109 --> 02:01:26.965
<v HOLMAN>No they tore it down.

3126
02:01:26.965 --> 02:01:28.450
<v LEE>It was torn down.

3127
02:01:28.450 --> 02:01:29.193
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s gone?

3128
02:01:29.193 --> 02:01:30.678
<v LEE>It was torn down.

3129
02:01:30.678 --> 02:01:31.421
<v HOLMAN>Torn down.

3130
02:01:31.421 --> 02:01:37.733
<v LEE>And Kenny was able to use some of the wood and all for something that he has.

3131
02:01:37.733 --> 02:01:41.074
So that’s another thing I wanted to mention down.

3132
02:01:41.074 --> 02:01:46.272
But . . . and then Ms. Flo’s house next . . . after she passed on, it was sold.

3133
02:01:46.272 --> 02:01:51.842
I really don’t know what’s happening because I don’t think they’ve gotten it straight yet.

3134
02:01:51.842 --> 02:01:56.668
But the landscape . . . The ethnic landscape began to change because Hispanics . . . Rolando Martin.

3135
02:01:56.668 --> 02:01:58.896
He moved next door to us.

3136
02:01:58.896 --> 02:02:01.124
And they just . . . did really well.

3137
02:02:01.124 --> 02:02:05.950
They remodeled the house and kept a beautiful garden and all of that.

3138
02:02:05.950 --> 02:02:11.891
And now they’re gone and the [. . .?] so I don’t know if they settled that at all.

3139
02:02:11.891 --> 02:02:13.376
So I don’t know.

3140
02:02:13.376 --> 02:02:21.173
I just look I said, “Well I guess then maybe after all I will be here till God calls me home.”

3141
02:02:21.173 --> 02:02:23.401
But it wasn’t to be, so.

3142
02:02:23.401 --> 02:02:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you miss the most?

3143
02:02:26.000 --> 02:02:27.915
<v LEE>All of this that I’m talking about.

3144
02:02:27.915 --> 02:02:29.282
The closeness . . . The closely knit.

3145
02:02:29.282 --> 02:02:34.205
I could sit on my porch . . . we would play car where you sit on the porch and say,

3146
02:02:34.205 --> 02:02:38.034
“Okay I’m going to pick the red cars, you’re gonna pick the white cars.”

3147
02:02:38.034 --> 02:02:39.402
And whoever number of cars,

3148
02:02:39.402 --> 02:02:42.410
<c.p>white cars, pass before the red ones would be the winner.

3149
02:02:42.410 --> 02:02:44.872
And so I said I can’t do that anymore.

3150
02:02:44.872 --> 02:02:46.513
Just little things you can’t do.

3151
02:02:46.513 --> 02:02:48.974
Just seeing people passing you know who they are.

3152
02:02:48.974 --> 02:02:51.162
<v HOLMAN>Sitting on the porch or in the pew.

3153
02:02:51.162 --> 02:02:52.256
<v LEE>Sitting on the pew . . .

3154
02:02:52.256 --> 02:02:55.265
<v HOLMAN>And everybody coming over because they see you on the pew . . .

3155
02:02:55.265 --> 02:02:55.538
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3156
02:02:55.538 --> 02:02:58.000
<v HOLMAN>Talk about things going on in the community.

3157
02:02:58.000 --> 02:03:00.085
<v ARSENEAULT>Y’all had a church pew on the front porch?

3158
02:03:00.085 --> 02:03:00.317
<v HOLMAN>Yes.

3159
02:03:00.317 --> 02:03:00.549
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3160
02:03:00.549 --> 02:03:02.634
We had gotten it from my church in Jeanerette.

3161
02:03:02.634 --> 02:03:07.732
They had remodeled and they were giving and selling away some of the pews so we got one and matter of fact,

3162
02:03:07.732 --> 02:03:10.049
we painted it and put it in the game room.

3163
02:03:10.049 --> 02:03:10.280
Yeah.

3164
02:03:10.280 --> 02:03:11.902
They would sit out there on that.

3165
02:03:11.902 --> 02:03:17.000
<v HOLMAN>Even as growing up I played basketball and I was out there with all the boys playing basketball and what not . . .

3166
02:03:17.000 --> 02:03:18.322
<v LEE>She was the only girl.

3167
02:03:18.322 --> 02:03:24.138
<v HOLMAN>And when I moved back with my girls, they were out there and it was like the same thing was going on.

3168
02:03:24.138 --> 02:03:27.839
They were out there playing with all the boys in basketball and what not.

3169
02:03:27.839 --> 02:03:28.897
And I said, oh.

3170
02:03:28.897 --> 02:03:30.218
Kind of brings back memories.

3171
02:03:30.218 --> 02:03:31.540
You’ll never see that again.

3172
02:03:31.540 --> 02:03:33.126
<v LEE>That’s what I hate about that.

3173
02:03:33.126 --> 02:03:37.092
I hate that when you’re . . . over and over, I look at it as our people.

3174
02:03:37.092 --> 02:03:38.943
Their heritage is just being taken away.

3175
02:03:38.943 --> 02:03:40.264
You know what I’m saying?

3176
02:03:40.264 --> 02:03:41.322
And then you just . . .

3177
02:03:41.322 --> 02:03:43.172
<v HOLMAN>You can never go back and say . . .

3178
02:03:43.172 --> 02:03:46.345
<v LEE>You can never go back and say this is where I was . . .

3179
02:03:46.345 --> 02:03:48.195
<v HOLMAN>This is where I was brought up.

3180
02:03:48.195 --> 02:03:49.517
This is where I live.

3181
02:03:49.517 --> 02:03:49.782
<v LEE>Yeah.

3182
02:03:49.782 --> 02:03:51.103
This is the home place.

3183
02:03:51.103 --> 02:03:51.632
It’s gone.

3184
02:03:51.632 --> 02:03:54.011
You go back there now and you don’t recognize.

3185
02:03:54.011 --> 02:03:54.805
I hate that.

3186
02:03:54.805 --> 02:03:55.598
I hate that.

3187
02:03:55.598 --> 02:03:56.655
I really hate that.

3188
02:03:56.655 --> 02:03:59.299
<v HOLMAN>This is what we did here at the pool, or . . .

3189
02:03:59.299 --> 02:04:00.621
<v LEE>And they say what pool?

3190
02:04:00.621 --> 02:04:01.414
We see nothing.

3191
02:04:01.414 --> 02:04:01.943
It’s gone.

3192
02:04:01.943 --> 02:04:03.000
<v HOLMAN>It’s just grass.

3193
02:04:03.000 --> 02:04:03.796
<v LEE>Its just . . . Yeah.

3194
02:04:03.796 --> 02:04:04.591
It’s just gone.

3195
02:04:04.591 --> 02:04:06.182
It’s not even the same anymore.

3196
02:04:06.182 --> 02:04:13.077
<v HOLMAN>That’s why I told my mom we have to look at this as yes we’re leaving the home which is a shell, but we’re taking memories.

3197
02:04:13.077 --> 02:04:14.934
Everything in the home we’re taking it.

3198
02:04:14.934 --> 02:04:19.972
This is just a shell we’re leaving, and I think that’s the only way we were able to move.

3199
02:04:19.972 --> 02:04:20.238
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3200
02:04:20.238 --> 02:04:21.829
She knows how difficult it was.

3201
02:04:21.829 --> 02:04:23.950
She say, “Momma, it is time to go.”

3202
02:04:23.950 --> 02:04:28.989
Let me tell you Chelsea, and I know you guys are getting tired, but I’ve got to say this.

3203
02:04:28.989 --> 02:04:30.050
<v ARSENEAULT>No, we’re here for . . .

3204
02:04:30.050 --> 02:04:32.171
<v LEE>I guess the Lord is saying, look girl.

3205
02:04:32.171 --> 02:04:34.028
It is time for you to go.

3206
02:04:34.028 --> 02:04:35.354
So, what went out first?

3207
02:04:35.354 --> 02:04:35.884
The oven.

3208
02:04:35.884 --> 02:04:37.210
The oven in the wall.

3209
02:04:37.210 --> 02:04:38.801
The built in oven went out.

3210
02:04:38.801 --> 02:04:43.840
Well you can’t cook without your oven, on the top of the stove you can’t bake any . . . well, what?

3211
02:04:43.840 --> 02:04:44.901
The oven is out.

3212
02:04:44.901 --> 02:04:46.757
What are we going to do, okay?

3213
02:04:46.757 --> 02:04:48.083
<v HOLMAN>Then lightning struck the home.

3214
02:04:48.083 --> 02:04:49.939
<v LEE>Yeah and our computers were messed up.

3215
02:04:49.939 --> 02:04:50.204
<v HOLMAN>[Agrees].

3216
02:04:50.204 --> 02:04:51.000
Computers . . . T.V.s.

3217
02:04:51.000 --> 02:04:52.808
<v LEE>Okay so we’ve got that working again.

3218
02:04:52.808 --> 02:04:54.358
Then the deep freezer went out.

3219
02:04:54.358 --> 02:04:55.133
What the mess?

3220
02:04:55.133 --> 02:04:56.683
<v HOLMAN>And then the AC went out.

3221
02:04:56.683 --> 02:04:57.200
I said . . .

3222
02:04:57.200 --> 02:04:59.525
<v LEE>But when the AC went out that was it.

3223
02:04:59.525 --> 02:05:01.075
During the middle of the night . . .

3224
02:05:01.075 --> 02:05:03.400
<v HOLMAN>This was like all in a . . . Three months maybe?

3225
02:05:03.400 --> 02:05:04.175
<v LEE>I guess so.

3226
02:05:04.175 --> 02:05:04.433
Yes.

3227
02:05:04.433 --> 02:05:04.692
Yes.

3228
02:05:04.692 --> 02:05:04.950
Yes.

3229
02:05:04.950 --> 02:05:07.017
So look, we all grabbed . . . I said, “Ooh.”

3230
02:05:07.017 --> 02:05:08.567
I said, we can’t stay here.

3231
02:05:08.567 --> 02:05:11.667
She were . . . they were . . . We were like this pouring down a sweat.

3232
02:05:11.667 --> 02:05:13.475
I just grabbed . . . I said we’re going.

3233
02:05:13.475 --> 02:05:16.058
We didn’t have . . . we had one bed up in here . . .

3234
02:05:16.058 --> 02:05:17.092
<v ARSENEAULT>What month was it?

3235
02:05:17.092 --> 02:05:18.642
<v LEE>That was when we moved here.

3236
02:05:18.642 --> 02:05:18.900
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

3237
02:05:18.900 --> 02:05:20.967
<v HOLMAN>We had the home but nothing in here.

3238
02:05:20.967 --> 02:05:22.000
<v LEE>Nothing in here.

3239
02:05:22.000 --> 02:05:24.500
<v HOLMAN>So we got air mattresses and came here.

3240
02:05:24.500 --> 02:05:26.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Turned on the air conditioner.

3241
02:05:26.062 --> 02:05:26.375
<v HOLMAN>Yes.

3242
02:05:26.375 --> 02:05:27.000
<v LEE>Yes.

3243
02:05:27.000 --> 02:05:29.812
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you still in the house on Prater Road?

3244
02:05:29.812 --> 02:05:30.125
<v HOLMAN>Yeah.

3245
02:05:30.125 --> 02:05:30.438
<v LEE>[Agrees]!

3246
02:05:30.438 --> 02:05:31.688
<v HOLMAN>That’s where we’re staying.

3247
02:05:31.688 --> 02:05:33.562
We hadn’t started moving objects here.

3248
02:05:33.562 --> 02:05:35.438
We just had the actual [?] home.

3249
02:05:35.438 --> 02:05:36.375
<v ARSENEAULT>It was ready?

3250
02:05:36.375 --> 02:05:37.000
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3251
02:05:37.000 --> 02:05:38.298
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mr. Kenny build that?

3252
02:05:38.298 --> 02:05:40.115
Or did Mr. Richard build that house?

3253
02:05:40.115 --> 02:05:40.374
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3254
02:05:40.374 --> 02:05:41.153
<v ARSENEAULT>For . . . all himself?

3255
02:05:41.153 --> 02:05:42.711
<v LEE>Oh, no. no, not build it.

3256
02:05:42.711 --> 02:05:43.749
No not that way.

3257
02:05:43.749 --> 02:05:44.787
Not what you’re saying.

3258
02:05:44.787 --> 02:05:46.604
No, no, no. we had a contractor.

3259
02:05:46.604 --> 02:05:52.055
We had a contractor, but of course he knew what to order . . . What he had to order, and what he needed.

3260
02:05:52.055 --> 02:05:54.132
They couldn’t say, “You need all of this.”

3261
02:05:54.132 --> 02:05:56.468
He’d say, “No I don’t need all of that.”

3262
02:05:56.468 --> 02:06:00.102
And we built two houses now because that house . . . we built it in ’73,

3263
02:06:00.102 --> 02:06:02.698
it burned in ’83 and we rebuilt the same house.

3264
02:06:02.698 --> 02:06:07.111
That’s why if you notice this, it’s built like another foot up higher than it once was.

3265
02:06:07.111 --> 02:06:09.966
Because Richard would often say, “Ooh, this house is gonna flood.”

3266
02:06:09.966 --> 02:06:14.638
It never did from ’73 to ’83, but that’s why you have to watch what you pray for.

3267
02:06:14.638 --> 02:06:19.570
Because in ’83 the house burned down, so when we rebuilt it, we just put it up another foot.

3268
02:06:19.570 --> 02:06:20.868
<v ARSENEAULT>How did it burn down?

3269
02:06:20.868 --> 02:06:24.762
<v LEE>My husband had come in with . . . My daughter was a member of Nancy Shephard Dancers.

3270
02:06:24.762 --> 02:06:27.098
We had been to practice and all of that.

3271
02:06:27.098 --> 02:06:28.136
Come back that Monday.

3272
02:06:28.136 --> 02:06:30.991
April the eighteenth on a Monday 1983 around six o’ clock.

3273
02:06:30.991 --> 02:06:36.443
He had come in from the back and through the sliding door and he lit a match or something and it . . .

3274
02:06:36.443 --> 02:06:38.000
<v HOLMAN>No, it was a [bat?]

3275
02:06:38.000 --> 02:06:39.750
<v LEE>A [?]? he didn’t know what it was.

3276
02:06:39.750 --> 02:06:42.750
<v HOLMAN>Yeah, didn’t want the scary so he . . . instead of flipping the switch.

3277
02:06:42.750 --> 02:06:45.000
<v LEE>He lit a match . . . Been out in the backyard.

3278
02:06:45.000 --> 02:06:47.000
You know what men do in the backyard.

3279
02:06:47.000 --> 02:06:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Didn’t want to scare a bat [laughs].

3280
02:06:49.000 --> 02:06:56.743
<v LEE>And so he lit it and it caught the dried flowers on fire and we just couldn’t get out it was like . . . and he said,

3281
02:06:56.743 --> 02:06:57.673
“Get Dawn out.

3282
02:06:57.673 --> 02:06:58.602
Get Dawn out.”

3283
02:06:58.602 --> 02:07:01.080
And he tried to get out the door.

3284
02:07:01.080 --> 02:07:02.319
His arm got burned.

3285
02:07:02.319 --> 02:07:04.487
And it was just from one thing.

3286
02:07:04.487 --> 02:07:08.204
The car was parked under the carport full of gas . . . New tires.

3287
02:07:08.204 --> 02:07:11.611
<v HOLMAN>There was no water pressure because they were bleeding the hydrants.

3288
02:07:11.611 --> 02:07:12.850
<v ARSENEAULT>What does that mean?

3289
02:07:12.850 --> 02:07:13.469
<v LEE>The hydrants . . .

3290
02:07:13.469 --> 02:07:14.398
<v HOLMAN>The water hydrants.

3291
02:07:14.398 --> 02:07:15.947
They were . . . letting them run.

3292
02:07:15.947 --> 02:07:17.186
<v LEE>To clear them out.

3293
02:07:17.186 --> 02:07:18.735
<v HOLMAN>To clear the lines out.

3294
02:07:18.735 --> 02:07:20.283
So we turn on the . . .

3295
02:07:20.283 --> 02:07:20.903
<v LEE>Water hose.

3296
02:07:20.903 --> 02:07:24.000
<v HOLMAN>Water hose and there was real low water pressure.

3297
02:07:24.000 --> 02:07:24.933
<v LEE>And you were . . . eight.

3298
02:07:24.933 --> 02:07:28.900
So in Ms. Flo’s house next door, we ran to that and that’s where we called the . . .

3299
02:07:28.900 --> 02:07:29.367
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s traumatizing.

3300
02:07:29.367 --> 02:07:29.600
<v LEE>Yeah.

3301
02:07:29.600 --> 02:07:29.833
Yeah.

3302
02:07:29.833 --> 02:07:31.467
She was only . . . and she was sleeping.

3303
02:07:31.467 --> 02:07:35.433
I was sitting in a chair and she was sleeping on the coach and poor Maw Maw.

3304
02:07:35.433 --> 02:07:37.300
She lived down a couple blocks from us.

3305
02:07:37.300 --> 02:07:40.100
And she had dreamed something was going to happen to the house.

3306
02:07:40.100 --> 02:07:44.067
And when she was coming down the street she just knew that we were in there asleep.

3307
02:07:44.067 --> 02:07:45.467
She was just hollering and crying.

3308
02:07:45.467 --> 02:07:47.100
<v ARSENEAULT>She thought y’all were caught on fire?

3309
02:07:47.100 --> 02:07:50.600
<v LEE>Oh, yeah because we always slept in the afternoon after school and all of that.

3310
02:07:50.600 --> 02:07:53.167
And Ms. Flo . . . we went to Ms. Flo house next door.

3311
02:07:53.167 --> 02:07:56.900
We stayed there and let me tell you how good these people were in that neighborhood.

3312
02:07:56.900 --> 02:07:58.767
The firefighters had given up on our house.

3313
02:07:58.767 --> 02:08:03.433
Richard’s relatives were . . . got on top of the house with a water hose still trying to put the house out.

3314
02:08:03.433 --> 02:08:04.600
I mean the fire out.

3315
02:08:04.600 --> 02:08:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>While it was on fire?

3316
02:08:06.000 --> 02:08:06.260
<v LEE>Yes.

3317
02:08:06.260 --> 02:08:07.041
And they pulled . . .

3318
02:08:07.041 --> 02:08:08.081
<v HOLMAN>Pulled every furniture out.

3319
02:08:08.081 --> 02:08:09.122
<v LEE>Pulled the furniture out.

3320
02:08:09.122 --> 02:08:15.366
And they . . . cut the air conditioner condenser from the back and pulled it away from the house so we could use it later on.

3321
02:08:15.366 --> 02:08:16.927
Firefighters were just standing there looking.

3322
02:08:16.927 --> 02:08:18.488
They said, “Well it’s no use.”

3323
02:08:18.488 --> 02:08:20.569
<v HOLMAN>This was family and our community did that.

3324
02:08:20.569 --> 02:08:20.829
<v LEE>Yes.

3325
02:08:20.829 --> 02:08:21.610
They did that.

3326
02:08:21.610 --> 02:08:22.911
And my sister from Houston . . .

3327
02:08:22.911 --> 02:08:23.431
<v ARSENEAULT>Helping out.

3328
02:08:23.431 --> 02:08:27.073
<v LEE>When she came to visit . . . My sister from Houston, the oldest one, she said,

3329
02:08:27.073 --> 02:08:32.276
“T had this happened in Houston instead of people coming to help us they would have been taking our things.”

3330
02:08:32.276 --> 02:08:34.878
She said, “This is some awesome place you guys have.”

3331
02:08:34.878 --> 02:08:35.138
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

3332
02:08:35.138 --> 02:08:37.220
Free stuff they would probably be like, huh?

3333
02:08:37.220 --> 02:08:37.480
<v LEE>[Agrees].

3334
02:08:37.480 --> 02:08:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

3335
02:08:38.000 --> 02:08:38.615
<v LEE>That’s it.

3336
02:08:38.615 --> 02:08:42.923
And so it just came and Sulphur High school was awesome in supporting us.

3337
02:08:42.923 --> 02:08:47.538
And Richard’s job and Dawn’s school . . . How the teachers supported her emotionally and all that.

3338
02:08:47.538 --> 02:08:50.000
It was just . . . it was something to see.

3339
02:08:50.000 --> 02:08:57.077
Coming together . . . that’s the thing about being in a community where you really are a community and not just houses; just separate houses.

3340
02:08:57.077 --> 02:08:58.615
And that’s what I miss.

3341
02:08:58.615 --> 02:08:59.538
I miss that.

3342
02:08:59.538 --> 02:09:02.923
I miss the times that Willow Springs came to the house.

3343
02:09:02.923 --> 02:09:05.385
We met for gumbo and all of that.

3344
02:09:05.385 --> 02:09:06.923
And we went Christmas caroling.

3345
02:09:06.923 --> 02:09:14.923
We walked down Prater Road and Old Spanish Trail past the track to the [home of Mr. Wasey Rigmaiden, who sold fire wood] singing Christmas carols.

3346
02:09:14.923 --> 02:09:17.692
No one ran over you or anything, you know?

3347
02:09:17.692 --> 02:09:19.231
<v HOLMAN>And we didn’t have sidewalks.

3348
02:09:19.231 --> 02:09:20.769
<v LEE>And we didn’t have sidewalks.

3349
02:09:20.769 --> 02:09:21.077
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

3350
02:09:21.077 --> 02:09:26.000
<v LEE>And at each station, each house, we had where we stopped for hot chocolate or whatever.

3351
02:09:26.000 --> 02:09:26.923
<v ARSENEAULT>What about Halloween?

3352
02:09:26.923 --> 02:09:27.846
Did . . . was there . . .?

3353
02:09:27.846 --> 02:09:28.462
<v LEE>Oh, my.

3354
02:09:28.462 --> 02:09:30.000
<v HOLMAN>We were the home.

3355
02:09:30.000 --> 02:09:31.952
<v LEE>We were the home to go to.

3356
02:09:31.952 --> 02:09:33.346
We were the haunted house.

3357
02:09:33.346 --> 02:09:35.019
<v HOLMAN>We had a show every time.

3358
02:09:35.019 --> 02:09:37.249
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you dress up the pigs and the [?]?

3359
02:09:37.249 --> 02:09:37.807
<v HOLMAN>No. just . . .

3360
02:09:37.807 --> 02:09:41.431
<v LEE>We had the witch, we had the home made coffin on the porch,

3361
02:09:41.431 --> 02:09:44.498
we had the . . . I had macramé a skeleton on the porch.

3362
02:09:44.498 --> 02:09:49.238
We had the witch, we had the music, we had the window open up with the music . . .

3363
02:09:49.238 --> 02:09:52.305
<v HOLMAN>Every year they would ask, “What are y’all doing this year?”

3364
02:09:52.305 --> 02:09:52.862
<v LEE>This year.

3365
02:09:52.862 --> 02:09:53.141
Yes.

3366
02:09:53.141 --> 02:09:57.045
And the kids would come and they didn’t want to get out the car.

3367
02:09:57.045 --> 02:09:58.160
They were like, ooh.

3368
02:09:58.160 --> 02:09:58.996
<v ARSENEAULT>They were scared.

3369
02:09:58.996 --> 02:10:02.342
<v LEE>And the wind was blowing and it was . . . oh, the whole gamut.

3370
02:10:02.342 --> 02:10:04.015
It was . . . just something to behold.

3371
02:10:04.015 --> 02:10:05.409
We had Easter egg hunts.

3372
02:10:05.409 --> 02:10:07.361
I mean my sister’s children would come.

3373
02:10:07.361 --> 02:10:11.822
She has three children and all of them are two years apart, Nicki being the youngest.

3374
02:10:11.822 --> 02:10:16.283
And we often had Easter egg hunts at the house when they would come to visit.

3375
02:10:16.283 --> 02:10:18.234
And my sister had this, which I

3376
02:10:18.234 --> 02:10:22.695
<c.p>thought was neat, she would write a riddle for each child and hide that child’s basket.

3377
02:10:22.695 --> 02:10:26.320
So in order to find your basket you had to solve the riddle.

3378
02:10:26.320 --> 02:10:28.550
Well the other day, this past Easter, right?

3379
02:10:28.550 --> 02:10:30.223
We say, “Let’s do this again.”

3380
02:10:30.223 --> 02:10:34.684
So Dawn said let’s do it . . . she asked me to do it like Auntie did it.

3381
02:10:34.684 --> 02:10:35.799
So I’m saying okay.

3382
02:10:35.799 --> 02:10:37.472
So the tradition will live on.

3383
02:10:37.472 --> 02:10:39.424
So we did it with her girls.

3384
02:10:39.424 --> 02:10:40.818
And they were so excited.

3385
02:10:40.818 --> 02:10:42.770
We said, “Okay that was not bad.”

3386
02:10:42.770 --> 02:10:45.000
She even enjoyed it, so it’s just . . . .

3387
02:10:45.000 --> 02:10:46.592
<v ARSENEAULT>What about ghost stories?

3388
02:10:46.592 --> 02:10:49.776
Were there any haunted stories about Mossville, or?

3389
02:10:49.776 --> 02:10:50.571
<v HOLMAN>Oh Lord.

3390
02:10:50.571 --> 02:10:52.163
<v LEE>What about that Buddha?

3391
02:10:52.163 --> 02:10:54.153
<v HOLMAN>The sycamore tree down Evergreen.

3392
02:10:54.153 --> 02:10:57.735
That was the ghost story when we were teenagers.

3393
02:10:57.735 --> 02:11:04.898
Don’t ride down Evergreen, turn the lights off because . . . I think his name was [?] not [?], I don’t know.

3394
02:11:04.898 --> 02:11:08.082
But I know someone else will tell you.

3395
02:11:08.082 --> 02:11:15.643
But it was a man in the woods and they said he was holding his head and a knife.

3396
02:11:15.643 --> 02:11:18.429
This was a tale for the teenagers.

3397
02:11:18.429 --> 02:11:19.622
<v ARSENEAULT>Who told it?

3398
02:11:19.622 --> 02:11:24.000
<v HOLMAN>It was like an old folk tale that was just . . .

3399
02:11:24.000 --> 02:11:24.853
<v LEE>Carried on . . .

3400
02:11:24.853 --> 02:11:26.985
<v HOLMAN>Carried on throughout the generations.

3401
02:11:26.985 --> 02:11:29.544
And it was down Evergreen Drive.

3402
02:11:29.544 --> 02:11:36.794
They said if you turn your lights off driving at night you’ll see him through the trees. [ . . .?]

3403
02:11:36.794 --> 02:11:38.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you try that?

3404
02:11:38.500 --> 02:11:39.779
<v HOLMAN>No I didn’t.

3405
02:11:39.779 --> 02:11:44.471
I can’t think of his nickname though that they called it.

3406
02:11:44.471 --> 02:11:47.029
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve heard of the sycamore tree.

3407
02:11:47.029 --> 02:11:47.456
<v LEE>Okay.

3408
02:11:47.456 --> 02:11:47.882
Okay.

3409
02:11:47.882 --> 02:11:49.588
Oh boy so much . . .

3410
02:11:49.588 --> 02:11:50.868
<v ARSENEAULT>And buried treasure.

3411
02:11:50.868 --> 02:11:53.000
Heard of buried . . . there’s . . .

3412
02:11:53.000 --> 02:11:53.288
<v LEE>Yeah.

3413
02:11:53.288 --> 02:11:57.603
There was a dynamite hole behind our house they said things were back there too.

3414
02:11:57.603 --> 02:12:01.342
We never did . . . we never had neighbors behind us, so I don’t know.

3415
02:12:01.342 --> 02:12:07.384
I tell you what, I promised that if I would ever get out of those woods I would never go back.

3416
02:12:07.384 --> 02:12:16.014
We had gone to the end of the property next to us and walk from the back to walk to the front of ours to see how deep it was,

3417
02:12:16.014 --> 02:12:18.890
it had one of these . . . what you call those things?

3418
02:12:18.890 --> 02:12:20.616
A fake bun or something here.

3419
02:12:20.616 --> 02:12:22.630
And Rich was in front of me.

3420
02:12:22.630 --> 02:12:24.356
I was just walking, walking, walking.

3421
02:12:24.356 --> 02:12:28.959
And it must have gotten caught in a tree and I thought something was pulling me.

3422
02:12:28.959 --> 02:12:30.397
I said, “Oh my God!

3423
02:12:30.397 --> 02:12:36.438
Get me out of here and I’ll never come back!” and I promise I never did go back out that way.

3424
02:12:36.438 --> 02:12:36.726
Interesting.

3425
02:12:36.726 --> 02:12:37.014
<v ARSENEAULT>Funny.

3426
02:12:37.014 --> 02:12:39.890
Now High Hope Road, I have to ask this too.

3427
02:12:39.890 --> 02:12:40.178
<v LEE>Okay.

3428
02:12:40.178 --> 02:12:43.055
<v ARSENEAULT>The Willow Springs dumping, do you remember anything about that?

3429
02:12:43.055 --> 02:12:46.219
The trucks and the . . . I think it was in Willow Springs.

3430
02:12:46.219 --> 02:12:49.384
<v LEE>They probably did down . . . because it was right past the church.

3431
02:12:49.384 --> 02:12:51.110
Willow Springs Church on that corner.

3432
02:12:51.110 --> 02:12:51.973
The graveyard . . . yeah.

3433
02:12:51.973 --> 02:12:53.986
And they did some dumping back there.

3434
02:12:53.986 --> 02:12:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember anything about that?

3435
02:12:56.000 --> 02:13:02.571
<v LEE>I know when I first started going there they did not have the public sewage because there were outside toilets at that church.

3436
02:13:02.571 --> 02:13:06.000
I remember that way back and I started . . . that was in ’72.

3437
02:13:06.000 --> 02:13:09.714
I don’t know when they . . . I can’t remember when they got public water.

3438
02:13:09.714 --> 02:13:10.571
I don’t know.

3439
02:13:10.571 --> 02:13:14.286
<v ARSENEAULT>Cows eating the stuff and there being like green stuff in their bellies?

3440
02:13:14.286 --> 02:13:15.429
<v LEE>Oh, I don’t know.

3441
02:13:15.429 --> 02:13:15.714
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

3442
02:13:15.714 --> 02:13:16.857
<v LEE>See, I don’t know.

3443
02:13:16.857 --> 02:13:21.429
See I didn’t really live there and that street . . . You know where it is from Mossville?

3444
02:13:21.429 --> 02:13:22.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

3445
02:13:22.000 --> 02:13:22.312
<v LEE>Yeah.

3446
02:13:22.312 --> 02:13:24.500
So I just went to church, yeah.

3447
02:13:24.500 --> 02:13:26.062
<v ARSENEAULT>Just curious if you were . . .

3448
02:13:26.062 --> 02:13:32.000
<v LEE>From ’72 to . . . I never did . . . now Vera Payne and her dad, Mr. Prater, I think you’ve interviewed Prater.

3449
02:13:32.000 --> 02:13:35.750
Yeah he’s probably going to tell you because he still goes there.

3450
02:13:35.750 --> 02:13:37.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh I need to ask him.

3451
02:13:37.625 --> 02:13:37.938
